{
  "raw": {
    "https://jobs.sjsu.edu/en-us/job/562911/assistant-professor-in-occupational-therapy": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Occupational Therapy",
      "description": "Department: Occupational Therapy\nPosition Rank and Title:\nAssistant Professor – Occupational Therapy\nSchool/Department Name:\nOccupational Therapy\nCompensation:\nCommensurate with qualifications, experience, and rank as established by the\nCSU Salary Schedule\n. Anticipated hiring academic year annual salary range:   $105,000-$115,000.\nFaculty employee benefits are very competitive for the higher education sector.  See the\nCSU Employee Benefits Summary\nfor details about the CSU’s excellent benefits package.\nTarget Start Date:\nAugust, 2027\nApplication Deadline:\n(Applications accepted until filled or closed)\nPosition Description\nThe Occupational Therapy Department at San José State University is pleased to announce a search for a full-time tenure-track Assistant Professor beginning in August, 2027. Experienced clinicians dedicated to preparing the next generation of occupational therapists are encouraged to apply. We are seeking clinical experts to teach in our OTD and MSOT programs covering foundational, professional development, practice and research courses. The ideal candidate will be a motivated self-starter, with a passion for teaching and developing impactful university-community collaborations.\nThe Occupational Therapy Program at San Jose State University was founded in 1943 and is the oldest accredited Occupational Therapy program in the California State University. Our national reputation for excellence is based on a superior academic curriculum, distinguished faculty, and accomplished graduates. Our in-person program has strong community ties, and we take particular pride in our faculty-led programs, which provide services to children, adults with physical disabilities and mental health challenges, and older adults living in the community under the guidance of a skilled clinician instructor. We offer opportunities to engage in interprofessional research and collaboration on campus. Our highly recognized faculty hold advanced credentials in low vision, pediatrics, and physical disabilities, and are recognized for their deep commitment to the profession, with five current faculty awarded Fellow status by the American Occupational Therapy Association (AOTA).\nwww.sjsu.edu/occupationaltherapy\n.\nThe Department of Occupational Therapy and San José State University value institutional excellence and are committed to advancing equity through research, teaching, and service. San José State University has been recognized by the Carnegie Foundation and the American Council on Education as an R2 research institution, reflecting the university’s growing research activity, commitment to innovation, and expansion of graduate education. This designation further strengthens opportunities for faculty scholarship, interdisciplinary collaboration, and student engagement in research. We enroll more than 40,000 students, many of whom are historically underserved; approximately 45% of our undergraduate students are first-generation college students and 38% are Pell Grant recipients. In addition, we are both a Hispanic Serving Institution (HSI) and an Asian American and Native American Pacific Islander Serving Institution (AANAPISI). SJSU aspires to create holistic learning environments that facilitate both individual and collective transformation. We seek teacher-scholars who cultivate inclusive, empowering spaces for mutual learning, skill-building, and knowledge exchange for all students. Our commitment to lifelong learning supports meaningful connections among faculty, staff, students, alumni, and the broader communities we serve.\nWe invite all applicants to include a Statement of Institutional Excellence (or incorporate it into your cover letter) to share how your lived and professional experiences will contribute to the SJSU community—particularly in relation to student success. A guide to writing this statement can be found at\nSJSU Institutional Excellence Statement Guidelines\n.\nRequired Qualifications\nEarned research and/or clinical doctorate in Occupational Therapy, Occupational Science, or related field, and at least two years of clinical experience.\nEligible for occupational therapy licensure in California\nDemonstrate a commitment to teaching excellence, possess excellent communication and interpersonal skills, and exhibit a commitment to establish and interact effectively with community partnerships.\nApplicants should demonstrate an awareness of and sensitivity to the educational goals of a socially and economically diverse student population as might have been gained in cross-cultural study, training, teaching, research practices, scholarship, service engagement, and other comparable experiences that facilitate student success..\nExperience working in the field of pediatrics, mental health, physical disabilities, or gerontology across the continuum of care.\nPreferred Qualifications\nPriority will be given to candidates who possess two or more of the following:\nResearch doctorate and/or demonstrated record of research and scholarly productivity, including peer-reviewed publications, research-related teaching, and/or student research mentorship, are preferred.\nAt least two years of teaching experience and five years of clinical practice.\nExperience in interprofessional collaboration.\nKnowledge of the use and application of emerging technologies and AI in occupational therapy.\nKey Responsibilities\nDuties include teaching, scholarship, and service to the university and profession.\nTeach practice courses related to the area of expertise, research-related courses, courses related to current and emerging practice areas, and adaptive technology in both didactic and clinical practice courses in the entry-level OT Master of Science (MSOT) and entry-level OT Doctorate (OTD) programs.\nParticipate in student mentorship in the MSOT and OTD programs.\nThe candidate will participate in shared governance, usually in department, college, and university committees and other service assignments.\nFaculty shall organize all their classes within the Canvas Learning Management System (LMS).\nThe candidate must demonstrate awareness and experience in understanding the needs of a student population of great diversity – in age, abilities, cultural background, ethnicity, religion, economic background, primary language, sexual orientation, gender identity, and academic preparation – through inclusive course materials, teaching strategies, and student advisement.\nOther Duties\nNote that all San José State University employees are considered mandated reporters under the California Child Abuse and Neglect Reporting Act and are required to comply with the requirements set forth in CSU Executive Order 1083 as a condition of employment. Pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act) and CSU systemwide policy, some faculty duties may lead to designation as a Campus Security Authority (CSA). CSAs are required to complete Clery Act training and to immediately report Clery incidents to the institution.\nFaculty employees must complete CSU employee training as assigned and required based on their role (e.g., Data Security, FERPA, Preventing Discrimination and Harassment, Title IX, Health and Safety). The President may recommend or require compliance with safety measures that decrease the likelihood of COVID-19 transmission or illness and allows the core mission and activities of the campus to continue.\nApplication Procedure\nClick Apply Now to complete the SJSU Online Employment Application and attach the following documents:\nletter of interest\ncurriculum vitae\ninstitutional excellence statement\n(dedicated statement or addressed in letter of interest)\nstatement of teaching interests/philosophy\nresearch plan\nthree letters of reference with contact information for the person writing the letters\nInquiries may be directed to the Department Chair or Search:\nGigi Smith, Professor and Chair of Occupational Therapy,\ngigi.smith@sjsu.edu\n,\nTelephone: 408-924-3070.\nPursuant to California Education Code 89521, applicants will be required to disclose whether they have received a final administrative decision or final judicial decision determining that they have committed sexual harassment within the last 7 years only after a determination is made that they meet the minimum qualifications for the position, and before an offer of employment is extended.  Applicants who reach the final stages of the application process must also sign a release form that authorizes the release of information by the applicant’s current and/or former employers to the CSU concerning any substantiated allegations of misconduct.\nConditional Offer\nThe work for this faculty position may only be performed in the State of California and requires establishing an on-campus presence. Employment is contingent upon US residence and proof of eligibility to work in the United States. Satisfactory completion of background and reference checks (including criminal records and prior employment) are required. SJSU will make a conditional offer of employment, which may be rescinded if the background and reference checks reveal disqualifying information, and/or it is discovered that the candidate knowingly withheld or falsified information. Failure to satisfactorily complete the background and reference checks may affect the continued employment of a current employee who was conditionally offered the position.\nSan José State University: Silicon Valley's Public University\nLocated in the heart of Silicon Valley — one of the most innovative regions in the world —\nSan José State University\nis the founding campus of the 23-campus California State University (CSU) system and the first public university in the West. Recognized as a leading transformative educational institution, San José State is an essential partner in the technological, economic, cultural, and social development of Silicon Valley, the Bay Area, and California. SJSU is a top-200 school nationally in research funding and second highest in research productivity in the CSU system. Cutting-edge research, world-class scholarship, student-centered learning, diverse communities, and commitment to social justice, allow SJSU to provide life-changing opportunities and advance the public good locally and globally.\nEqual Employment Statement\nSan José State University prohibits discrimination on the basis of Age, Ancestry, Caste, Color, Disability, Ethnicity, Gender, Gender Expression, Gender Identity, Genetic Information, Marital Status, Medical Condition, Military Status, Nationality, Race, Religion, Religious Creed, Sex, Sexual Orientation, Sex Stereotype, and Veteran Status. This policy applies to all San José State University students, faculty, and staff as well as University programs and activities. Reasonable accommodations are made for applicants with disabilities who self-disclose.\nCampus Security and Fire Safety Notification\nPursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, the Annual Security Report (ASR) is also now available for viewing at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Security-Report.pdf\n. The ASR contains the current security and safety-related policy statements, emergency preparedness and evacuation information, crime prevention and Sexual Assault prevention information, and information about drug and alcohol prevention programming. The ASR also contains statistics of Clery crimes for San José State University locations for the three most recent calendar years. A paper copy of the ASR is available upon request by contacting the Office of the Clery Director by phone at 408-924-1501 or by email at clerycompliance@sjsu.edu.\nPursuant to the Higher Education Opportunity Act, the Annual Fire Safety Report (AFSR) is available at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Fire-Safety-Report.pdf\n. The purpose of this report is to disclose statistics for fires that occurred within SJSU on-campus housing facilities for the three most recent calendar years, and to distribute fire safety policies and procedures intended to promote safety on Campus. A paper copy of the AFSR is available upon request by contacting the Housing Office by phone at 408-795-5600 or by email at uhs-frontdesk@sjsu.edu."
    },
    "https://jobs.sjsu.edu/en-us/job/562966/assistant-professor-in-mechanical-engineering-mechatronics": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Mechanical Engineering - Mechatronics",
      "description": "Department: Mechanical Engineering\nPosition Rank and Title:\nTenure-track Assistant Professor—Mechatronics\nSchool/Department Name:\nMechanical Engineering\nCompensation:\nCommensurate with qualiﬁcations, experience, and rank, in accordance with the\nCSU\nSalary Schedule\n. The anticipated annual salary range is 101, 000–124,000\n.\nFaculty beneﬁts are competitive within higher education. See the\nCSU Employee Beneﬁts Summary\nfor details.\nTarget Start Date:\nAugust 1, 2027\nApplication Deadline:\nDecember 31, 2026\n(Applications will be accepted until the position is ﬁlled or the search is closed.)\nPosition Description\nThe Department of Mechanical Engineering at San José State University invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor with expertise in mechatronics. Applicants must hold a Ph.D. in mechanical engineering or a closely related ﬁeld by the time of appointment and demonstrate expertise in one or more areas such as robotics, control systems, automation, physical AI, sensors, embedded systems, or human–machine interfaces.\nThe successful candidate will demonstrate potential for excellence in teaching undergraduate and graduate students in classroom and hardware laboratory settings; developing an independent, externally funded research agenda; publishing in peer-reviewed journal and conference papers; and presenting at professional conferences. The candidate must also have strong communication, teamwork, and interpersonal skills; demonstrate awareness of and sensitivity to the educational goals of a diverse student population; and contribute to the interdisciplinary strengths, infrastructure, and industry partnerships of SJSU’s mechatronics programs.\nLocated in the heart of Silicon Valley, SJSU Mechanical Engineering provides an exceptional setting for teaching, research, and collaboration. The department has strong programs in robotics and mechatronics, advanced manufacturing, thermal and energy systems, and mechanical design, supported by hands-on laboratories and interdisciplinary research. Its proximity and connections to Silicon Valley's technology, semiconductor, manufacturing, and emerging engineering industries create opportunities for faculty to develop industry partnerships, pursue applied research, and bring real-world engineering challenges into the classroom.\nThe Mechanical Engineering Department and San José State University value institutional excellence and are committed to advancing equity through research, teaching, and service. We enroll more than 40,000 students, many of whom are historically underserved; approximately 45% of our undergraduate students are ﬁrst-generation college students and 38% are Pell Grant recipients. In addition, we are both a Hispanic Serving Institution (HSI) and an Asian American and Native American Paciﬁc Islander Serving Institution (AANAPISI). SJSU aspires to create holistic learning environments that facilitate both individual and collective transformation. We seek teacher-scholars who cultivate inclusive, empowering spaces for mutual learning, skill-building, and knowledge exchange for all students. Our commitment to lifelong learning supports meaningful connections among faculty, staﬀ, students, alumni, and the broader communities we serve.\nWe invite all applicants to include a Statement of Institutional Excellence (or incorporate it into your cover letter) to share how your lived and professional experiences will contribute to the SJSU community—particularly in relation to student success. A guide to writing this statement can be found at\nSJSU Institutional Excellence Statement Guidelines\n.\nRequired Qualiﬁcations\nPh.D. in mechanical engineering or a closely related engineering ﬁeld by the time of appointment.\nAbility to teach and develop undergraduate and graduate courses in dynamics, robotics, mechatronics, and control systems.\nDemonstrated ability to conduct research in mechatronics or a closely related ﬁeld. .\nAwareness of and sensitivity to the educational goals of a socially, culturally, and economically diverse student population, as demonstrated through cross-cultural study, training, teaching, or comparable experience.\nPreferred Qualiﬁcations\nPriority will be given to candidates who possess one or more of the following:\nStrong record of peer-reviewed publication.\nExperience or demonstrated interest in eﬀective teaching and pedagogical methods.\nExperience with extramural research funding proposals.\nExperience with industry collaboration, especially related to mechatronics..\nExperience supervising undergraduate and/or graduate students.\nExperience leading or contributing to interdisciplinary projects in collaborative environments.\nKey Responsibilities\nTeach and develop undergraduate and graduate courses in mechatronics and related ﬁelds, including course preparation, assessment of learning outcomes, and continuous improvement of instructional methods.\nConduct research, publish results in peer-reviewed venues, pursue external funding, and supervise student research.\nCollaborate with SJSU faculty, other universities, and/or industry partners on research and funding opportunities related to robotics, mechatronics, and physical AI.\nParticipate in shared governance and professional service at the department, college, university, and professional levels.\nOrganize courses and course materials in a university-supported , learning management system (e.g., Canvas by Instructure) and comply with all university-mandated accessibility requirements.\nSupport student success through inclusive course materials, teaching strategies, advising, and mentorship that address the needs of students with diverse backgrounds, abilities, identities, and levels of academic preparation.\nOther Duties\nNote that all San José State University employees are considered mandated reporters under the California Child Abuse and Neglect Reporting Act and are required to comply with the requirements set forth in CSU Executive Order 1083 as a condition of employment. Pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act) and CSU systemwide policy, some faculty duties may lead to designation as a Campus Security Authority (CSA). CSAs are required to complete Clery Act training and to immediately report Clery incidents to the institution.\nFaculty employees must complete CSU employee training as assigned and required based on their role (e.g., Data Security, FERPA, Preventing Discrimination and Harassment, Title IX, Health and Safety). The President may recommend or require compliance with safety measures that decrease the likelihood of COVID-19 transmission or illness and allows the core mission and activities of the campus to continue.\nApplication Procedure\nClick\nApply Now\nto complete the SJSU Online Employment Application and submit the following materials:\nLetter of interest (maximum two pages).\nCurriculum vitae.\nInstitutional Excellence Statement\n, either as a separate statement or incorporated into the letter of interest (maximum two pages).\nStatement of teaching interests and philosophy (maximum two pages).\nResearch Interests and Future Plans (maximum two pages) .\nContact information for at least three references.\nApplicants are encouraged to include an Institutional Excellence Statement, either separately or in the letter of interest, describing how their lived and professional experiences would contribute to the SJSU community, particularly student success. See the\nSJSU Institutional Excellence Statement Guidelines\nfor guidance.\nQuestions may be directed to the Department Chair, Hohyun Lee, at\nhohyun.lee@sjsu.edu\n.\nPursuant to California Education Code 89521, applicants will be required to disclose whether they have received a ﬁnal administrative decision or ﬁnal judicial decision determining that they have committed sexual harassment within the last 7 years only after a determination is made that they meet the minimum qualiﬁcations for the position, and before an oﬀer of employment is extended. Applicants who reach the ﬁnal stages of the application process must also sign a release form that authorizes the release of information by the applicant’s current and/or former employers to the CSU concerning any substantiated allegations of misconduct.\nConditional Oﬀer\nThe work for this faculty position may only be performed in the State of California and requires establishing an on-campus presence. Employment is contingent upon US residence and proof of eligibility to work in the United States. Satisfactory completion of background and reference checks (including criminal records and prior employment) are required. SJSU will make a conditional oﬀer of employment, which may be rescinded if the background and reference checks reveal disqualifying information, and/or it is discovered that the candidate knowingly withheld or falsiﬁed information. Failure to satisfactorily complete the background and reference checks may aﬀect the continued employment of a current employee who was conditionally oﬀered the position.\nSan José State University: Silicon Valley's Public University\nLocated in the heart of Silicon Valley — one of the most innovative regions in the world —\nSan José State\nUniversity\nis the founding campus of the 22-campus California State University (CSU) system and the ﬁrst public university in the West. Recognized as a leading transformative educational institution, San José State is an essential partner in the technological, economic, cultural, and social development of Silicon Valley, the Bay Area, and California. SJSU is a top-200 school nationally in research funding and second highest in research productivity in the CSU system. Cutting-edge research, world-class scholarship, student-centered learning, diverse communities, and commitment to social justice, allow SJSU to provide life-changing opportunities and advance the public good locally and globally.\nEqual Employment Statement\nSan José State University prohibits discrimination on the basis of Age, Ancestry, Caste, Color, Disability, Ethnicity, Gender, Gender Expression, Gender Identity, Genetic Information, Marital Status, Medical Condition, Military Status, Nationality, Race, Religion, Religious Creed, Sex, Sexual Orientation, Sex Stereotype, and Veteran Status. This policy applies to all San José State University students, faculty, and staﬀ as well as University programs and activities. Reasonable accommodations are made for applicants\nwith disabilities who self-disclose.\nCampus Security and Fire Safety Notiﬁcation\nPursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, the Annual Security Report (ASR) is also now available for viewing at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Security-Report.pdf\n. The ASR contains the current security and safety-related policy statements, emergency preparedness and evacuation information, crime prevention and Sexual Assault prevention information, and information about drug and alcohol prevention programming. The ASR also contains statistics of Clery crimes for San José State University locations for the three most recent calendar years. A paper copy of the ASR is available upon request by contacting the Oﬃce of the Clery Director by phone at 408-924-1501 or by email at\nclerycompliance@sjsu.edu.\nPursuant to the Higher Education Opportunity Act, the Annual Fire Safety Report (AFSR) is available at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Fire-Safety-Report.pdf\n. The purpose of this report is to disclose statistics for ﬁres that occurred within SJSU on-campus housing facilities for the three most recent calendar years, and to distribute ﬁre safety policies and procedures intended to promote safety on Campus. A paper copy of the AFSR is available upon request by contacting the Housing Oﬃce by phone at 408-795-5600 or by email at\nuhs-frontdesk@sjsu.edu."
    },
    "https://jobs.sjsu.edu/en-us/job/562854/director-of-the-africana-asian-american-chicano-native-american-aaacna-studies-center": {
      "title": "Director of the Africana, Asian American, Chicano, & Native American (AAACNA) Studies Center",
      "description": "Department: University Library\nPosition Rank and Title:\nTenured Librarian, and Director of the Africana, Asian American, Chicano, & Native American (AAACNA) Studies Center\nSchool/Department Name:\nUniversity Library\nCompensation:\nCommensurate with qualifications, experience, and rank as established by the\nCSU Salary Schedule\n. Anticipated hiring annual salary range: $112,000 - $118,000 for Associate Librarian, and $125,000 - $135,000 for Full Librarian.\nFaculty employee benefits are very competitive for the higher education sector.  See the\nCSU Employee Benefits Summary\nfor details about the CSU’s excellent benefits package.\nTarget Start Date:\nFebruary 2027\nApplication Deadline:\nNovember 12, 2026 (Applications accepted until filled or closed)\nPosition Description\nThe\nSJSU King Library\nat San José State University is pleased to announce a search for the Director of the Africana, Asian American, Chicano,  & Native American (AAACNA) Studies Center. The position is for a full-time, tenured Associate or Full Librarian beginning in February 2027. Faculty duties will be 0.6 FTE or 60% Directorship and 0.4 FTE or 40% Liaison Librarian. The Director normally serves for a renewable, four-year term.\nThe\nAAACNA Studies Center\ndirectorship offers a distinctive opportunity to provide strategic leadership and shape the Center’s high impact programs, initiatives and collections.\nThe AAACNA Studies Center is a unique, 6,000-square-foot academic and cultural hub located on the fifth floor of the Dr. Martin Luther King, Jr. Library at SJSU.  Born out of decades of student advocacy and campus equity movements, the Center serves as an open-stack research and community space dedicated to amplifying the histories, literature, and voices of historically underrepresented communities. What makes AAACNA truly distinct is its rare structural and community-driven model: it physically and academically integrates four distinct ethnic studies collections - Africana, Asian American, Chicano, Native American, and comparative Ethnic Studies—under one roof to foster cross-cultural dialogue, interdisciplinary research, and belonging.\nReporting to the Associate Dean for Student and Faculty Engagement, the AAACNA Director’s responsibilities include development of collections, programs, exhibits, and services that foster academic and community engagement, and providing research and reference services aligned with curricular and scholarly needs.\nAs faculty members, SJSU librarians participate in library and university governance and engage in scholarship. The AAACNA Director is a member of the University Library Leadership Team (ULLT). ULLT functions as a collaborative group that advances the\nmission, vision, and values\nof the Library. The SJSU King Library is a highly collaborative community, and the director will work with colleagues across the University Library to improve and enhance engagement with special attention to the AAACNA Studies Center.\nThe AAACNA Director is in charge of facilitating and leading the AAACNA Advisory Board. The Board advises the Director and creates opportunities for community connections. The Director is an important participant in advancing the philanthropic interests of the SJSU library, which includes development and management of the AAACNA Center’s endowments and creating opportunities for new gifts. Furthermore, there are opportunities to establish AAACNA as a recipient of grants and positioning the center as a national leader in the field.\nIn addition to advancing the Center’s programs, collections, and partnerships, the Director will strengthen the infrastructure that supports AAACNA’s long-term sustainability. This includes establishing clear priorities and annual work plans; clarifying roles and decision-making processes; and reporting practices that demonstrate the Center’s impact and inform future planning. The Director will also help ensure continuity by maintaining institutional knowledge, reviewing operating agreements and governance documents by consulting the existing MOU, and aligning the Center’s activities with the Library’s broader strategic priorities.\nAAACNA is directly supported and staffed by the Center's Director and up to three faculty librarians who have a portion of their academic assignment in the Center. The affiliate librarians work in close collaboration with the Center Director to advance programs, collections and advocacy.\nThe Center also holds a unique position for partnerships, which include collaborations with the San José Public Library, SJSU community, and other aligned community organizations.\nSubject to administrative discretion, the Director is appointed to a renewable, four-year term, providing the opportunity to guide sustained growth and long-term development of the Center.\nWhat we look for in a potential candidate\nWe are looking for a visionary , collaborative, and agile leader who is deeply steeped in ethnic studies work. The director of AAACNA will bring a track record in the field along with a passion for the work of the center to the SJSU community. We prioritize equity-minded and curious professionals dedicated to supporting the University Library in achieving its\nstrategic objectives rooted in student success\n.\nWhy would you want to join us and what can you expect?\nThe expert faculty and staff at the University Library are highly committed to excellence and service. You can expect to join a dedicated team of professionals with many opportunities to grow, develop, and lead innovative and SJSU-centered programming and services. The SJSU King Library shares space with the main branch of San José Public Library. As a joint library facility and a member of the 22 universities in the California State University system, there are possibilities to collaborate with multiple external stakeholders and partners across the full spectrum of librarianship.\nDr. Martin Luther King, Jr. Library\nDr. Martin Luther King, Jr. Library is a unique partnership between San José State University (SJSU) and the City of San José, which opened in August 2003. It is the first library in the United States to integrate the services and collections of a major university and public library system. Located in downtown San José, the King Library is an impressive 475,000 square feet with nine floors of collections, collaborative work spaces, meeting rooms, exhibit areas, and unique resources dedicated to lifelong learning for all to enjoy. The building serves as the SJSU library, the main library for the San José Public Library system, and as a cornerstone for the entire community.\nSJSU King Library\nbrings people, ideas, and information together through its spaces, resources, and expertise to facilitate community building, innovation, and knowledge creation. We are actively engaged in social justice issues through collaborative conversations, organizational review, and assessment. The leadership, faculty, staff, and student employees are dedicated to advancing equity practices in recruitment, retention, resources, and services. SJSU King Library is seeking to increase the diversity of its faculty, and encourages all members of underrepresented groups to apply.\nThe SJSU King Library and San José State University value institutional excellence and are committed to advancing equity through research, teaching, and service. We enroll more than 40,000 students, many of whom are historically underserved; approximately 45% of our undergraduate students are first-generation college students and 38% are Pell Grant recipients. In addition, we are both a Hispanic Serving Institution (HSI) and an Asian American and Native American Pacific Islander Serving Institution (AANAPISI). SJSU aspires to create holistic learning environments that facilitate both individual and collective transformation. We seek teacher-scholars who cultivate inclusive, empowering spaces for mutual learning, skill-building, and knowledge exchange for all students. Our commitment to lifelong learning supports meaningful connections among faculty, staff, students, alumni, and the broader communities we serve.\nWe invite all applicants to include a Statement of Institutional Excellence (or incorporate it into your cover letter) to share how your lived and professional experiences will contribute to the SJSU community—particularly in relation to student success. A guide to writing this statement can be found at\nSJSU Institutional Excellence Statement Guidelines\n.\nRequired Qualifications\nMaster’s degree from a program accredited by the American Library Association or an equivalent international degree plus minimum of five (5) years of experience as a leader of developing ethnic studies services, programming, and collections.\nDemonstrated leadership experience in providing strategic direction and advancing the university library’s outreach and user engagement initiatives across campus and within the broader community.\nDemonstrated success in establishing and sustaining strategic partnerships with academic programs, advisory groups, cultural and community-based organizations through effective communication, while cultivating philanthropic support and securing funding for programs and endowments.\nExpertise providing instruction and research consultations across a range of ethnic studies and interdisciplinary courses, as well as developing specialized collections aligned with AAACNA’s mission and areas of focus. Record of scholarly achievement in relevant areas of specialization, including research that examines or engages with diverse communities, populations, experiences, or perspectives.\nApplicants should demonstrate an awareness of and sensitivity to the educational goals of a socially and economically diverse student population as might have been gained in cross-cultural study, training, teaching, and other comparable experience.\nPreferred Qualifications\nPriority will be given to candidates who possess one or more of the following:\nExperience working in an academic library or comparable higher education environment.\nExperience incorporating diverse perspectives and culturally responsive practices into instruction, collections, exhibits, programs, or services.\nDemonstrated success leading significant organizational change or complex, multi-phase initiatives.\nPrior supervisory experience.\nKey Responsibilities\nAAACNA Studies Center Director Responsibilities - 0.6 FTE or 60%\nProvide strategic leadership for the AAACNA Studies Center, establishing priorities and advancing a vision that supports the Center’s mission and the teaching, research, and student-success goals of the University Library and SJSU.\nPlan, implement, and assess the Center’s programs, exhibits, events, research initiatives, and other activities highlighting the histories, experiences, scholarship, and contributions represented by the Center’s four focus areas.\nGuide the development, stewardship, visibility, and use of the Center’s specialized collections, including identifying opportunities to improve discovery, preservation, digitization, and access.\nBuild and sustain collaborative relationships with academic departments and programs, faculty and staff associations, student organizations, campus resource centers, advisory groups, community organizations, and other partners.\nOversee the Center’s daily operations, including its space, budget, program calendar, policies, workflows, and allocation of available resources.\nCoordinate the work of employees, student assistants, interns, volunteers, and project teams assigned to Center activities, fostering an inclusive, collaborative, and service-oriented environment.\nEstablish measures of effectiveness, collect and analyze relevant data, communicate the Center’s accomplishments, and use assessment findings to guide planning and continuous improvement.\nRepresent and advocate for the Center within the University Library, across SJSU, and in relevant community and professional settings, while pursuing grants, gifts, endowment support, and other opportunities that strengthen the Center’s programs and collections.\nLiaison Librarian Responsibilities - 0.4 FTE or 40%\nSupport student learning by collaborating with Ethnic Studies and other campus faculty to facilitate the effective use of curriculum-specific information resources.\nProvide research, reference, consultation, and information literacy instruction to undergraduate and graduate students, faculty, and researchers in synchronous and asynchronous formats.\nSelect, evaluate, and promote information resources in multiple formats that support the curricular and research needs of assigned academic programs and contribute to the development of the Center’s specialized collections.\nMaintain knowledge of current and emerging research practices, scholarly communication developments, digital scholarship, and artificial intelligence technologies relevant to the assigned disciplines and academic librarianship.\nDesign, implement, and assess user-centered library services, instructional activities, and resources for assigned academic departments and programs.\nThe candidate will participate in shared governance, usually in department, library, and university committees and other service assignments.\nThe candidate must demonstrate awareness and experience in understanding the needs of a student population of great diversity – in age, abilities, cultural background, ethnicity, religion, economic background, primary language, sexual orientation, gender identity, and academic preparation – through inclusive course materials, teaching strategies, and advisement.\nOther Duties\nNote that all San José State University employees are considered mandated reporters under the California Child Abuse and Neglect Reporting Act and are required to comply with the requirements set forth in CSU Executive Order 1083 as a condition of employment. Pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act) and CSU systemwide policy, some faculty duties may lead to designation as a Campus Security Authority (CSA). CSAs are required to complete Clery Act training and to immediately report Clery incidents to the institution.\nFaculty employees must complete CSU employee training as assigned and required based on their role (e.g., Data Security, FERPA, Preventing Discrimination and Harassment, Title IX, Health and Safety). The President may recommend or require compliance with safety measures that decrease the likelihood of COVID-19 transmission or illness and allows the core mission and activities of the campus to continue.\nApplication Procedure\nClick Apply Now to complete the SJSU Online Employment Application and attach the following documents [\ndepartments may specify length of items below (e.g. 2 pages)\n]:\nletter of interest\ncurriculum vitae\ninstitutional excellence statement\n(dedicated statement or addressed in letter of interest)\nstatement of teaching interests/philosophy\nresearch plans\nthree references with contact information (only finalists’s references will be contacted)\nInquiries may be directed to the Library Dean, Michael Meth, at\nmichael.meth@sjsu.edu\n, or to the Library Chair, Anamika Megwalu, at\nanamika.megwalu@sjsu.edu\n.\nPursuant to California Education Code 89521, applicants will be required to disclose whether they have received a final administrative decision or final judicial decision determining that they have committed sexual harassment within the last 7 years only after a determination is made that they meet the minimum qualifications for the position, and before an offer of employment is extended.  Applicants who reach the final stages of the application process must also sign a release form that authorizes the release of information by the applicant’s current and/or former employers to the CSU concerning any substantiated allegations of misconduct.\nConditional Offer\nThe work for this faculty position may only be performed in the State of California and requires establishing an on-campus presence. Employment is contingent upon US residence and proof of eligibility to work in the United States. Satisfactory completion of background and reference checks (including criminal records and prior employment) are required. SJSU will make a conditional offer of employment, which may be rescinded if the background and reference checks reveal disqualifying information, and/or it is discovered that the candidate knowingly withheld or falsified information. Failure to satisfactorily complete the background and reference checks may affect the continued employment of a current employee who was conditionally offered the position.\nSan José State University: Silicon Valley's Public University\nLocated in the heart of Silicon Valley — one of the most innovative regions in the world —\nSan José State University\nis the founding campus of the 22 universities forming the California State University (CSU) system and the first public university in the West. Recognized as a leading transformative educational institution, San José State is an essential partner in the technological, economic, cultural, and social development of Silicon Valley, the Bay Area, and California. SJSU is a top-200 school nationally in research funding and second highest in research productivity in the CSU system. Cutting-edge research, world-class scholarship, student-centered learning, diverse communities, and commitment to social justice, allow SJSU to provide life-changing opportunities and advance the public good locally and globally.\nEqual Employment Statement\nSan José State University prohibits discrimination on the basis of Age, Ancestry, Caste, Color, Disability, Ethnicity, Gender, Gender Expression, Gender Identity, Genetic Information, Marital Status, Medical Condition, Military Status, Nationality, Race, Religion, Religious Creed, Sex, Sexual Orientation, Sex Stereotype, and Veteran Status. This policy applies to all San José State University students, faculty, and staff as well as University programs and activities. Reasonable accommodations are made for applicants with disabilities who self-disclose.\nCampus Security and Fire Safety Notification\nPursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, the Annual Security Report (ASR) is also now available for viewing at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Security-Report.pdf\n. The ASR contains the current security and safety-related policy statements, emergency preparedness and evacuation information, crime prevention and Sexual Assault prevention information, and information about drug and alcohol prevention programming. The ASR also contains statistics of Clery crimes for San José State University locations for the three most recent calendar years. A paper copy of the ASR is available upon request by contacting the Office of the Clery Director by phone at 408-924-1501 or by email at clerycompliance@sjsu.edu.\nPursuant to the Higher Education Opportunity Act, the Annual Fire Safety Report (AFSR) is available at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Fire-Safety-Report.pdf\n. The purpose of this report is to disclose statistics for fires that occurred within SJSU on-campus housing facilities for the three most recent calendar years, and to distribute fire safety policies and procedures intended to promote safety on Campus. A paper copy of the AFSR is available upon request by contacting the Housing Office by phone at 408-795-5600 or by email at uhs-frontdesk@sjsu.edu."
    },
    "https://jobs.sjsu.edu/en-us/job/562909/assistant-professor-in-public-relations": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Public Relations",
      "description": "Department: School of Journalism and Mass Communications\nPosition Rank and Title:\nAssistant Professor, Tenure Track, Public Relations\nSchool/Department Name:\nSchool of Journalism and Mass Communications\nCompensation:\nCommensurate with qualifications, experience, and rank as established by the\nCSU Salary Schedule\n. Anticipated hiring academic year annual salary range: $ 90,000 -\n$ 94,000.\nFaculty employee benefits are very competitive for the higher education sector. See the\nCSU Employee Benefits Summary\nfor details about the CSU’s excellent benefits package.\nTarget Start Date:\nAugust 17, 2027\nApplication Deadline:\nOctober 31st, 2026 (Applications accepted until filled or closed)\nPosition Description\nThe School of Journalism and Mass Communications at San José State University is pleased to announce a search for a tenure-track Assistant Professor in Public Relations beginning in August 2027. We seek a colleague with practical and scholarly expertise in public relations and strategic communication, including areas such as public relations writing, media relations, digital and social media, research and analytics, crisis communication, campaign planning, emerging technologies, or related fields. The successful candidate will teach in both our on-campus and growing SJSU Online Public Relations degree programs, with courses potentially including Principles of Public Relations, Public Relations Writing, Social Media Strategies, Public Relations Research, Strategic Communication Planning, and Campaigns.\nThe School of Journalism and Mass Communications (JMC) offers leading programs in advertising, public relations, journalism, and mass communications—all rooted in Silicon Valley’s culture of innovation. As an Adobe Creative Campus, and with a new VR and Emerging Technologies Lab in development, JMC provides students and faculty with opportunities to explore possibilities in media, AI, and immersive storytelling. Our award-winning student media and the student-run DBH Agency highlight our hands-on, collaborative approach, while faculty actively lead research and practice in emerging technologies\nThe School of Journalism and Mass Communications and San José State University value institutional excellence and are committed to advancing equity through research, teaching, and service. We enroll more than 40,000 students, many of whom are historically underserved; approximately 45% of our undergraduate students are first-generation college students and 38% are Pell Grant recipients. In addition, we are both a Hispanic Serving Institution (HSI) and an Asian American and Native American Pacific Islander Serving Institution (AANAPISI). SJSU aspires to create holistic learning environments that facilitate both individual and collective transformation. We seek teacher-scholars who cultivate inclusive, empowering spaces for mutual learning, skill-building, and knowledge exchange for all students. Our commitment to lifelong learning supports meaningful connections among faculty, staff, students, alumni, and the broader communities we serve.\nWe invite all applicants to include a Statement of Institutional Excellence (or incorporate it into your cover letter) to share how your lived and professional experiences will contribute to the SJSU community—particularly in relation to student success. A guide to writing this statement can be found at SJSU Institutional Excellence Statement Guidelines.\nWe invite all applicants to include a Statement of Institutional Excellence (or incorporate it into your cover letter) to share how your lived and professional experiences will contribute to the SJSU community—particularly in relation to student success. A guide to writing this statement can be found at SJSU Institutional Excellence Statement Guidelines.\nRequired Qualifications\nMasters degree (MFA, MA, MS) in Public Relations, Strategic Communication, Mass Communication, Communication, or a closely related discipline by the start of the appointment. Candidates who are ABD may be considered, provided the degree is completed by the start of the appointment.\nApplicants should demonstrate an awareness of and sensitivity to the educational goals of a socially and economically diverse student population as might have been gained in cross-cultural study, training, teaching, and other comparable experience.\nPreferred Qualifications\nPriority will be given to candidates who possess one or more of the following:\nPhD in Public Relations, Strategic Communication, Mass Communication, Communication, or a closely related discipline by the start of the appointment. Candidates who are ABD may be considered, provided the doctoral degree is completed by the start of the appointment.\nExperience teaching public relations courses in online (synchronous/asynchronous), hybrid, and in-person formats.\nAbility to teach core public relations courses, particularly public relations writing, crisis, research methods, digital and social media, strategic planning, and campaigns. ● Professional experience in agency, corporate, nonprofit, government, or community based public relations.\nA developed or developing research or creative agenda in public relations, strategic communication, digital media, artificial intelligence, or emerging communication technologies.\nExperience integrating artificial intelligence, social listening, media monitoring, digital analytics, multimedia production, or data visualization into public relations teaching, research, or professional practice.\nExperience with client-based projects, experiential learning, community-engaged learning, or other innovative teaching approaches.\nEvidence of scholarly, teaching, or professional achievement, including publications, grants, awards, accreditation, or industry recognition.\nExperience mentoring and advising students from varied educational, cultural, and socioeconomic backgrounds.\nEngagement with professional or scholarly organizations such as PRSA, PRSSA, AEJMC, ICA, or NCA.\nBreadth of experience that supports curriculum development, internship programs, student-run agencies, or partnerships with professional and community organizations.\nKey Responsibilities\nTeach undergraduate courses in Public Relations in both the on-campus and SJSU Online degree programs.\nTeach core and elective courses in areas such as public relations writing, research, media relations, digital and social media, strategic planning, crisis communication, and campaigns.\nDevelop and maintain an active program of research, scholarship, or creative activity that contributes to the field of public relations or strategic communication. ● Design and deliver inclusive, engaging, and applied learning experiences appropriate to in-person and online instructional environments.\nAdvise and mentor students regarding academic progress, internships, professional development, and career preparation.\nContribute to curriculum development, assessment, accreditation, and ongoing program improvement.\nCultivate relationships with public relations professionals, community partners, and professional organizations to support experiential learning and student opportunities. ● The candidate will participate in shared governance, usually in department, college, and university committees and other service assignments.\nFaculty shall organize all their classes within the Canvas Learning Management System (LMS).\nThe candidate must demonstrate awareness and experience in understanding the needs of a student population of great diversity—in age, abilities, cultural background, ethnicity, religion, economic background, primary language, sexual orientation, gender identity, and academic preparation—through inclusive course materials, teaching strategies, and advisement.\nOther Duties\nNote that all San José State University employees are considered mandated reporters under the California Child Abuse and Neglect Reporting Act and are required to comply with the requirements set forth in CSU Executive Order 1083 as a condition of employment. Pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act) and CSU systemwide policy, some faculty duties may lead to designation as a Campus Security Authority (CSA). CSAs are required to complete Clery Act training and to immediately report Clery incidents to the institution.\nFaculty employees must complete CSU employee training as assigned and required based on their role (e.g., Data Security, FERPA, Preventing Discrimination and Harassment, Title IX, Health and Safety). The President may recommend or require compliance with safety measures that decrease the likelihood of COVID-19 transmission or illness and allows the core mission and activities of the campus to continue.\nApplication Procedure\nClick Apply Now to complete the SJSU Online Employment Application and attach the following documents [\ndepartments may specify length of items below (e.g. 2 pages)\n]:\nletter of interest\ncurriculum vitae\ninclusive excellence statement\n(dedicated statement or addressed in letter of interest)\nstatement of teaching interests/philosophy\nResearch plans\nPortfolio of relevant work\nThree references with contact information . Inquiries may be directed to the School Director: John Delacruz,\njohn.delacruz@sjsu.edu\n.\nConditional Offer\nThe work for this faculty position may only be performed in the State of California and requires establishing an on-campus presence. Employment is contingent upon US residence and proof of eligibility to work in the United States. Satisfactory completion of a background check (including a criminal records check) is required for employment. SJSU will make a conditional offer of employment, which may be rescinded if the background check reveals disqualifying information, and/or it is discovered that the candidate knowingly withheld or falsified information. Failure to satisfactorily complete the background check may affect the continued employment of a current employee who was conditionally offered the position.\nSan José State University: Silicon Valley's Public University\nLocated in the heart of Silicon Valley — one of the most innovative regions in the world — San José State University is the founding campus of the 23-campus California State University (CSU) system and the first public university in the West. Recognized as a leading transformative educational institution, San José State is an essential partner in the technological, economic, cultural, and social development of Silicon Valley, the Bay Area, and California. SJSU is a top-200 school nationally in research funding and second highest in research productivity in the CSU system. Cutting-edge research, world-class scholarship, student-centered learning, diverse communities, and commitment to social justice, allow SJSU to provide life-changing opportunities and advance the public good locally and globally.\nEqual Employment Statement\nSan José State University prohibits discrimination on the basis of Age, Ancestry, Caste, Color, Disability, Ethnicity, Gender, Gender Expression, Gender Identity, Genetic Information, Marital Status, Medical Condition, Military Status, Nationality, Race, Religion, Religious Creed, Sex, Sexual Orientation, Sex Stereotype, and Veteran Status. This policy applies to all San José State University students, faculty, and staff as well as University programs and activities. Reasonable accommodations are made for applicants with disabilities who self-disclose.\nCampus Security and Fire Safety Notification\nPursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, the Annual Security Report (ASR) is also now available for viewing at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Security-Report.pdf.\nThe ASR contains the current security and safety-related policy statements, emergency preparedness and evacuation information, crime prevention and Sexual Assault prevention information, and information about drug and alcohol prevention programming. The ASR also contains statistics of Clery crimes for San José State University locations for the three most recent calendar years. A paper copy of the ASR is available upon request by contacting the Office of the Clery Director by phone at 408-924-1501 or by email at\nclerycompliance@sjsu.edu.\nPursuant to the Higher Education Opportunity Act, the Annual Fire Safety Report (AFSR) is available at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Fire-Safety-Report.pdf.\nThe purpose of this report is to disclose statistics for fires that occurred within SJSU on-campus housing facilities for the three most recent calendar years, and to distribute fire safety policies and procedures intended to promote safety on Campus. A paper copy of the AFSR is available upon request by contacting the Housing Office by phone at 408-795-5600 or by email at\nuhs-frontdesk@sjsu.edu.\nPursuant to California Education Code 89521, applicants will be required to disclose whether they have received a final administrative decision or final judicial decision determining that they have committed sexual harassment within the last 7 years only after a determination is made that they meet the minimum qualifications for the position, and before an offer of employment is extended. Applicants who reach the final stages of the application process must also sign a release form that authorizes the release of information by the applicant’s current and/or former employers to the CSU concerning any substantiated allegations of misconduct."
