    "https://jobs.sjsu.edu/en-us/job/562713/assistant-professor-in-physiology": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Physiology",
      "description": "Department: Biological Sciences\nPosition Rank and Title:\nAssistant Professor\nSchool/Department Name:\nBiological Sciences\nCompensation:\nCommensurate with qualifications, experience, and rank as established by the\nCSU Salary Schedule\n. Anticipated hiring academic year annual salary range: $108,000   -  $110,000\nFaculty employee benefits are very competitive for the higher education sector.  See the\nCSU Employee Benefits Summary\nfor details about the CSU’s excellent benefits package.\nTarget Start Date:\nAugust 17, 2027\nApplication Deadline:\nNovember 14, 2026\n(Applications accepted until filled or closed)\nPosition Description\nThe Department of Biological Sciences at San José State University is pleased to announce a search for a tenure-track Assistant Professor in Physiology beginning in August 2027. The successful candidate will be responsible for teaching foundational and advanced courses in physiology, which may include Cell Physiology, Systems Physiology lecture and lab, Toxicology, Endocrine Physiology, Advanced Human Anatomy, and service courses in both anatomy and physiology. Candidates will be expected to develop an independent research program and mentor both undergraduate and masters students in research experiences. The successful candidate will also contribute to department and institutional service activities.\nThe Department of Biological Sciences at San José State University is a team of dedicated teacher-scholars recognized for our commitment to excellent teaching, engaging students in research projects, and promoting equity, diversity, and inclusion in Biology and in STEM disciplines, more broadly. We offer degrees in Biology with concentrations in Microbiology, Molecular Biology, and Systems Physiology as well as degrees in Ecology & Evolution and Marine Biology. The highest degree offered is the Master's. There are approximately 1300 undergraduate and 70 graduate students enrolled across our programs. Facilities include molecular and microbiology research and teaching labs (BSL2 capable), confocal laser microscopy and imaging, a flow cytometry facility, cell culture facilities, anatomy and physiology research and teaching labs, animal facilities for rodents and frogs, bioinformatics and general computing labs, greenhouses, a herbarium, plant growth chambers, and a variety of natural history museums.\nThe Department of Biological Sciences and San José State University value institutional excellence and are committed to advancing equity through research, teaching, and service. We enroll more than 40,000 students, many of whom are historically underserved; approximately 45% of our undergraduate students are first-generation college students and 38% are Pell Grant recipients. In addition, we are both a Hispanic Serving Institution (HSI) and an Asian American and Native American Pacific Islander Serving Institution (AANAPISI). SJSU aspires to create holistic learning environments that facilitate both individual and collective transformation. We seek teacher-scholars who cultivate inclusive, empowering spaces for mutual learning, skill-building, and knowledge exchange for all students. Our commitment to lifelong learning supports meaningful connections among faculty, staff, students, alumni, and the broader communities we serve.\nWe invite all applicants to include a Statement of Institutional Excellence (or incorporate it into your cover letter) to share how your lived and professional experiences will contribute to the SJSU community—particularly in relation to student success. A guide to writing this statement can be found at\nSJSU Institutional Excellence Statement Guidelines\n.\nRequired Qualifications\nPhD in Physiology or a related discipline\nPost-doctoral experience in physiology or a related field.\nPublication record reflective of the candidate’s professional experience and the potential to become an excellent teacher-scholar.\nApplicants should demonstrate an awareness of and sensitivity to the educational goals of a socially and economically diverse student population as might have been gained in cross-cultural study, training, teaching, and other comparable experience.\nPreferred Qualifications\nPriority will be given to candidates who possess one or more of the following:\nExperience in any field of physiology will be considered; however, experience in cell physiology or exercise physiology, toxicology, renal physiology, gastrointestinal physiology, respiratory physiology and/or anatomy is desirable.\nEvidence of undergraduate or graduate research student mentoring.\nExperience working with historically underserved students and demonstrated evidence of integrating pedagogical approaches that support the success of all students\nKey Responsibilities\nCandidate is expected to develop a research program, publish in peer reviewed journals, pursue extramural research grants, and involve masters and undergraduate students in research.\nCandidate is expected to teach in courses at both the undergraduate and graduate level. At the undergraduate level, teaching duties will include one or more of the following courses: Cell and Molecular Physiology, Systems Physiology lecture and laboratory, Integrative Physiology, Advanced Human Anatomy, Introduction to Principles of Toxicology, and the frosh core biology series. At the graduate level, teaching duties will include: Physiology lecture or laboratory courses and courses on Scientific Communication.\nCandidate is expected to participate in curriculum development and teaching of other courses to meet the needs of the program and its diverse student population.\nThe candidate will participate in shared governance, usually in department, college, and university committees and other service assignments.\nFaculty shall organize all their classes within the Canvas Learning Management System (LMS).\nThe candidate must demonstrate awareness and experience in understanding the needs of a student population of great diversity – in age, abilities, cultural background, ethnicity, religion, economic background, primary language, sexual orientation, gender identity, and academic preparation – through inclusive course materials, teaching strategies, and advisement.\nOther Duties\nNote that all San José State University employees are considered mandated reporters under the California Child Abuse and Neglect Reporting Act and are required to comply with the requirements set forth in CSU Executive Order 1083 as a condition of employment. Pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act) and CSU systemwide policy, some faculty duties may lead to designation as a Campus Security Authority (CSA). CSAs are required to complete Clery Act training and to immediately report Clery incidents to the institution.\nFaculty employees must complete CSU employee training as assigned and required based on their role (e.g., Data Security, FERPA, Preventing Discrimination and Harassment, Title IX, Health and Safety). The President may recommend or require compliance with safety measures that decrease the likelihood of COVID-19 transmission or illness and allows the core mission and activities of the campus to continue.\nApplication Procedure\nClick Apply Now to complete the SJSU Online Employment Application and attach the following documents:\nletter of interest\ncurriculum vitae\ninstitutional excellence statement\n(dedicated statement (2 pages) or addressed in letter of interest)\nstatement of teaching interests/philosophy (2 pages)\nresearch plans (2 pages) including the candidate’s plans to engage undergraduate and masters students in research\nContact information for three references\nInquiries may be directed to the Department Chair: Rachael French, rachael.french@sjsu.edu\nPursuant to California Education Code 89521, applicants will be required to disclose whether they have received a final administrative decision or final judicial decision determining that they have committed sexual harassment within the last 7 years only after a determination is made that they meet the minimum qualifications for the position, and before an offer of employment is extended.  Applicants who reach the final stages of the application process must also sign a release form that authorizes the release of information by the applicant’s current and/or former employers to the CSU concerning any substantiated allegations of misconduct.\nConditional Offer\nThe work for this faculty position may only be performed in the State of California and requires establishing an on-campus presence. Employment is contingent upon US residence and proof of eligibility to work in the United States. Satisfactory completion of background and reference checks (including criminal records and prior employment) are required. SJSU will make a conditional offer of employment, which may be rescinded if the background and reference checks reveal disqualifying information, and/or it is discovered that the candidate knowingly withheld or falsified information. Failure to satisfactorily complete the background and reference checks may affect the continued employment of a current employee who was conditionally offered the position.\nSan José State University: Silicon Valley's Public University\nLocated in the heart of Silicon Valley — one of the most innovative regions in the world —\nSan José State University\nis the founding campus of the 23-campus California State University (CSU) system and the first public university in the West. Recognized as a leading transformative educational institution, San José State is an essential partner in the technological, economic, cultural, and social development of Silicon Valley, the Bay Area, and California. SJSU is a top-200 school nationally in research funding and second highest in research productivity in the CSU system. Cutting-edge research, world-class scholarship, student-centered learning, diverse communities, and commitment to social justice, allow SJSU to provide life-changing opportunities and advance the public good locally and globally.\nEqual Employment Statement\nSan José State University prohibits discrimination on the basis of Age, Ancestry, Caste, Color, Disability, Ethnicity, Gender, Gender Expression, Gender Identity, Genetic Information, Marital Status, Medical Condition, Military Status, Nationality, Race, Religion, Religious Creed, Sex, Sexual Orientation, Sex Stereotype, and Veteran Status. This policy applies to all San José State University students, faculty, and staff as well as University programs and activities. Reasonable accommodations are made for applicants with disabilities who self-disclose.\nCampus Security and Fire Safety Notification\nPursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, the Annual Security Report (ASR) is also now available for viewing at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Security-Report.pdf\n. The ASR contains the current security and safety-related policy statements, emergency preparedness and evacuation information, crime prevention and Sexual Assault prevention information, and information about drug and alcohol prevention programming. The ASR also contains statistics of Clery crimes for San José State University locations for the three most recent calendar years. A paper copy of the ASR is available upon request by contacting the Office of the Clery Director by phone at 408-924-1501 or by email at clerycompliance@sjsu.edu.\nPursuant to the Higher Education Opportunity Act, the Annual Fire Safety Report (AFSR) is available at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Fire-Safety-Report.pdf\n. The purpose of this report is to disclose statistics for fires that occurred within SJSU on-campus housing facilities for the three most recent calendar years, and to distribute fire safety policies and procedures intended to promote safety on Campus. A paper copy of the AFSR is available upon request by contacting the Housing Office by phone at 408-795-5600 or by email at uhs-frontdesk@sjsu.edu."
    },
    "https://jobs.sjsu.edu/en-us/job/562916/assistant-professor-of-radiotelevisionfilm-rtvf-cinematography": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Radio-Television-Film (RTVF) - Cinematography",
      "description": "Department: School/Department Name:\nPosition Rank and Title:\nAssistant Professor of Cinematography (Tenure-Track)\nSchool/Department Name:\nSchool/Department Name:\nSchool of Film, Theatre, and Dance, College of Humanities and the Arts\nCompensation:\nCommensurate with qualifications, experience, and rank as established by the\nCSU Salary Schedule\n. Anticipated hiring academic year annual salary range: $90,000 - $94,000. Faculty employee benefits are very competitive for the higher education sector.  See the\nCSU Employee Benefits Summary\nfor details about the CSU’s excellent benefits package.\nTarget Start Date:\nAugust 17, 2027\nApplication Deadline:\nJanuary 5, 2027 (Applications accepted until filled or closed)\nPosition Description\nThe School of Film, Theatre, and Dance at San José State University invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor specializing in cinematography and visual storytelling, with strong technical expertise in film and television production. We invite applications from accomplished media makers and educators with demonstrated professional expertise or teaching background in camera work, lighting, and moving image production. Successful candidates will exhibit thorough mastery of visual narrative alongside creative and technical film craft, with particular interest in those whose creative work engages historically marginalized voices and perspectives.\nThe School of Film, Theatre, and Dance brings together Radio-Television-Film, Theatre Arts, and Dance, creating opportunities for collaboration across media and performing arts. The RTVF program combines hands-on training in radio, television, film, screenwriting, and sound production with critical study of media history, aesthetics, and culture. Students develop their creative and technical skills through coursework and collaborative productions, including opportunities with Spartan Film Studios and the university’s radio station, 90.5 FM KSJS. Located in Silicon Valley, the school offers a setting in which faculty can connect creative practice, critical inquiry, and interdisciplinary teaching while preparing students for careers in an evolving media landscape.\nThe School of Film, Theatre, and Dance and San José State University value institutional excellence and are committed to advancing equity through research, teaching, and service. We enroll more than 40,000 students, many of whom are historically underserved; approximately 45% of our undergraduate students are first-generation college students and 38% are Pell Grant recipients. In addition, we are both a Hispanic Serving Institution (HSI) and an Asian American and Native American Pacific Islander Serving Institution (AANAPISI). SJSU aspires to create holistic learning environments that facilitate both individual and collective transformation. We seek teacher-scholars who cultivate inclusive, empowering spaces for mutual learning, skill-building, and knowledge exchange for all students. Our commitment to lifelong learning supports meaningful connections among faculty, staff, students, alumni, and the broader communities we serve.\nWe invite all applicants to include a Statement of Institutional Excellence (or incorporate it into your cover letter) to share how your lived and professional experiences will contribute to the SJSU community—particularly in relation to student success. A guide to writing this statement can be found at\nSJSU Institutional Excellence Statement Guidelines\n.\nRequired Qualifications\nMFA in Film Production, or a graduate degree in a closely related field.\nA record of creative practice in cinematography, visual storytelling, and film and/or television production, demonstrating both aesthetic and technical expertise.\nUndergraduate-level teaching experience, with demonstrated ability to integrate theoretical concepts and aesthetic principles into hands-on instruction in cinematography and/or film production.\nTechnical proficiency in camera operation, lighting, grip and electrical systems, and digital cinema technologies, including experience with some of the following: digital cinema cameras and camera support systems, lenses, exposure, composition, camera movement, studio and location lighting, color theory, image acquisition, and color management workflows.\nApplicants should demonstrate an awareness of and sensitivity to the educational goals of a socially and economically diverse student population as might have been gained in cross-cultural study, training, teaching, and other comparable experience.\nPreferred Qualifications\nPriority will be given to candidates who possess several of the following:\nDemonstrated professional experience as a cinematographer and/or filmmaker in the film and television industry, including work in one or more production formats such as narrative feature or short films, episodic/serial television, web-based media, music videos, commercials, or other professional media productions.\nFamiliarity with contemporary filmmaking practices, workflows, technologies, and production environments.\nExcellent communication skills and ability to work collaboratively with others in a small, dynamic department.\nAdditional professional experience or technical proficiency in areas such as color correction and post-production imaging, editing and contemporary post-production practices, directing within narrative production environments, and emerging technologies in filmmaking, such as AI-related and virtual production tools.\nExperience mentoring students in collaborative film and media production environments, with attention to inclusive collaboration, professional equipment use, set safety, and production planning.\nExperience developing or contributing to cinematography and/or production curricula and courses.\nA record of obtaining external grants from governmental or private foundation sources to support creative work or research.\nAn interest in interdepartmental collaborations and cross-disciplinary teamwork beyond the School of Film, Theatre, and Dance, for example, with the Animation program, Digital Media Art program, Digital Humanities Center, or School of Journalism and Mass Communication.\nDemonstrated commitment to addressing equity gaps in film and television and to advancing social justice, diversity, equity, inclusion, and belonging, including creative work that engages historically underrepresented voices and perspectives.\nKey Responsibilities\nDesign and teach introductory and advanced-level courses in cinematography and film and television production, with the possibility of teaching related courses based on the candidate’s specific areas of expertise, emphasizing professional equipment use, set safety, and production planning.\nAdvise and mentor students in coursework, creative projects, collaborative production environments, and professional development.\nContribute to curriculum development in the Radio-Television-Film program, including the development and revision of courses in cinematography and emerging areas of film production.\nParticipate in student recruitment.\nDevelop and sustain an ongoing record of professional engagement, including research and/or creative activities.\nThe candidate will participate in shared governance, usually in department, college, and university committees and other service assignments.\nFaculty shall organize all their classes within the Canvas Learning Management System (LMS).\nThe candidate must demonstrate awareness and experience in understanding the needs of a student population of great diversity – in age, abilities, cultural background, ethnicity, religion, economic background, primary language, sexual orientation, gender identity, and academic preparation – through inclusive course materials, teaching strategies, and advisement.\nOther Duties\nNote that all San José State University employees are considered mandated reporters under the California Child Abuse and Neglect Reporting Act and are required to comply with the requirements set forth in CSU Executive Order 1083 as a condition of employment. Pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act) and CSU systemwide policy, some faculty duties may lead to designation as a Campus Security Authority (CSA). CSAs are required to complete Clery Act training and to immediately report Clery incidents to the institution.\nFaculty employees must complete CSU employee training as assigned and required based on their role (e.g., Data Security, FERPA, Preventing Discrimination and Harassment, Title IX, Health and Safety). The President may recommend or require compliance with safety measures that decrease the likelihood of COVID-19 transmission or illness and allows the core mission and activities of the campus to continue.\nApplication Procedure\nClick Apply Now to complete the SJSU Online Employment Application and attach the following documents:\nletter of interest\ncurriculum vitae\ninstitutional excellence statement\n(dedicated statement or addressed in letter of interest)\none-page statement of teaching interests/philosophy, describing the candidate’s plans to engage undergraduate students in film production and studies\none-page statement of current and future research and/or creative production plans\nlink to an online portfolio and/or inclusion of work in cinematography and visual storytelling\nthree professional references, who will be contacted for the final pool of applicants\nInquiries may be directed to: Dr. Matthew Spangler, Director of the School of Film, Theatre, and Dance, at\nmatthew.spangler@sjsu.edu.\nPursuant to California Education Code 89521, applicants will be required to disclose whether they have received a final administrative decision or final judicial decision determining that they have committed sexual harassment within the last 7 years only after a determination is made that they meet the minimum qualifications for the position, and before an offer of employment is extended.  Applicants who reach the final stages of the application process must also sign a release form that authorizes the release of information by the applicant’s current and/or former employers to the CSU concerning any substantiated allegations of misconduct.\nConditional Offer\nThe work for this faculty position may only be performed in the State of California and requires establishing an on-campus presence.  Employment is contingent upon US residence and proof of eligibility to work in the United States. Satisfactory completion of background and reference checks (including criminal records and prior employment) are required. SJSU will make a conditional offer of employment, which may be rescinded if the background and reference checks reveal disqualifying information, and/or it is discovered that the candidate knowingly withheld or falsified information. Failure to satisfactorily complete the background and reference checks may affect the continued employment of a current employee who was conditionally offered the position.\nSan José State University: Silicon Valley's Public University\nLocated in the heart of Silicon Valley — one of the most innovative regions in the world —\nSan José State University\nis the founding campus of the 23-campus California State University (CSU) system and the first public university in the West. Recognized as a leading transformative educational institution, San José State is an essential partner in the technological, economic, cultural, and social development of Silicon Valley, the Bay Area, and California. SJSU is a top-200 school nationally in research funding and second highest in research productivity in the CSU system. Cutting-edge research, world-class scholarship, student-centered learning, diverse communities, and commitment to social justice, allow SJSU to provide life-changing opportunities and advance the public good locally and globally.\nEqual Employment Statement\nSan José State University prohibits discrimination on the basis of Age, Ancestry, Caste, Color, Disability, Ethnicity, Gender, Gender Expression, Gender Identity, Genetic Information, Marital Status, Medical Condition, Military Status, Nationality, Race, Religion, Religious Creed, Sex, Sexual Orientation, Sex Stereotype, and Veteran Status. This policy applies to all San José State University students, faculty, and staff as well as University programs and activities. Reasonable accommodations are made for applicants with disabilities who self-disclose.\nCampus Security and Fire Safety Notification\nPursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, the Annual Security Report (ASR) is also now available for viewing at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Security-Report.pdf\n. The ASR contains the current security and safety-related policy statements, emergency preparedness and evacuation information, crime prevention and Sexual Assault prevention information, and information about drug and alcohol prevention programming. The ASR also contains statistics of Clery crimes for San José State University locations for the three most recent calendar years. A paper copy of the ASR is available upon request by contacting the Office of the Clery Director by phone at 408-924-1501 or by email at\nclerycompliance@sjsu.edu\n.\nPursuant to the Higher Education Opportunity Act, the Annual Fire Safety Report (AFSR) is available at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Fire-Safety-Report.pdf\n. The purpose of this report is to disclose statistics for fires that occurred within SJSU on-campus housing facilities for the three most recent calendar years, and to distribute fire safety policies and procedures intended to promote safety on Campus. A paper copy of the AFSR is available upon request by contacting the Housing Office by phone at 408-795-5600 or by email at uhs-frontdesk@sjsu.edu."
    },
    "https://jobs.sjsu.edu/en-us/job/562515/assistant-professor-in-advertising": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Advertising",
      "description": "Department: School of Journalism and Mass Communications\nPosition Rank and Title:\nTenure-Track Assistant Professor in Advertising\nSchool/Department Name:\nSchool of Journalism and Mass Communications\nCompensation:\nCommensurate with qualiﬁcations, experience, and rank as established by the\nCSU Salary\nSchedule\n. Anticipated hiring academic year annual salary range: $ 90,000 - $ 94,000.\nFaculty employee beneﬁts are very competitive for the higher education sector. See the\nCSU Employee\nBeneﬁts Summary\nfor details about the CSU’s excellent beneﬁts package.\nTarget Start Date:\nAugust 17, 2027\nApplication Deadline:\nOctober 31st, 2026 (Applications accepted until ﬁlled or closed)\nPosition Description\nThe School of Journalism and Mass Communications at San José State University is pleased to announce a search for a Tenure Track Assistant Professor in Advertising beginning in August 2027. We are seeking a colleague who brings both professional expertise and scholarly insight in advertising with a focus on emerging technologies and AI. The ideal candidate will demonstrate a strong record (or clear potential) in teaching, research, and creative work at the intersection of advertising, technology, and society. We value industry experience, the ability to engage students with hands-on, project-based learning, and a commitment to equity, diversity, and inclusion in the classroom and in professional practice. As part of our collaborative faculty community, the new hire will help prepare students to shape the future of media and advertising in Silicon Valley and beyond.\nThe\nSchool of Journalism and Mass Communications\n(JMC) oﬀers leading programs in advertising, public relations, journalism, and mass communications—all rooted in Silicon Valley’s culture of innovation. As an Adobe Creative Campus, and with a new VR and Emerging Technologies Lab in development, JMC provides students and faculty with opportunities to explore possibilities in media, AI, and immersive storytelling. Our award-winning student media and the student-run DBH Agency highlight our hands-on, collaborative approach, while faculty actively lead research and practice in emerging technologies.\nThe School of Journalism and Mass Communications and San José State University value institutional excellence and are committed to advancing equity through research, teaching, and service. We enroll more than 40,000 students, many of whom are historically underserved; approximately 45% of our undergraduate students are ﬁrst-generation college students and 38% are Pell Grant recipients. In addition, we are both a Hispanic Serving Institution (HSI) and an Asian American and Native American Paciﬁc Islander Serving Institution (AANAPISI). SJSU aspires to create holistic learning environments that facilitate both individual and collective transformation. We seek teacher-scholars who cultivate inclusive, empowering spaces for mutual learning, skill-building, and knowledge exchange for all students. Our commitment to lifelong learning supports meaningful connections among faculty, staﬀ, students, alumni, and the broader communities we serve.\nWe invite all applicants to include a Statement of Institutional Excellence (or incorporate it into your cover letter) to share how your lived and professional experiences will contribute to the SJSU community—particularly in relation to student success. A guide to writing this statement can be found at\nSJSU Institutional Excellence Statement Guidelines\n.\nRequired Qualiﬁcations\nMA or MFA in Advertising, Graphic/Communication Design or other related disciplines\nApplicants should demonstrate an awareness of and sensitivity to the educational goals of a socially and economically diverse student population as might have been gained in cross-cultural study, training, teaching, and other comparable experience.\nPreferred Qualiﬁcations\nPriority will be given to candidates who possess one or more of the following:\nCandidates with an PhD or MFA terminal degree are preferred; however, applicants holding a Master’s degree with substantial industry experience are also encouraged to apply.\nExperience working with emerging technologies as part of a creative workﬂow.\nExperience with, primarily, Adobe tools, and other creative tools with a focus on AI driven and generative production.\nKnowledge of the integration of AI and other emerging media in the advertising and PR industries with a particular focus on creative practice.\nA developed or developing research or creative agenda that focuses on the application of AI in creative production workﬂows, and/or the ethical and sustainability issues surrounding the application of said technologies.\nKey Responsibilities\nIntegrating AI and emerging technologies in existing courses on both the Creative and the Management tracks of the Advertising program as well as courses taken by the PR students and the Advertising Marcom track.\nDeveloping new courses that address the ethical and sustainability issues surround the wholesale adaptation of AI and emerging technologies in the communication design and creative industries\nCollaborate with SJSU’s Advanced Institute for Ethical Technology (ai-et) to transform narratives around technology through creative practice and humanistic inquiry.\nThe candidate will participate in shared governance, usually in department, college, and university committees and other service assignments.\nFaculty shall organize all their classes within the Canvas Learning Management System (LMS).\nThe candidate must demonstrate awareness and experience in understanding the needs of a student population of great diversity – in age, abilities, cultural background, ethnicity, religion, economic background, primary language, sexual orientation, gender identity, and academic preparation – through inclusive course materials, teaching strategies, and advisement.\nOther Duties\nNote that all San José State University employees are considered mandated reporters under the California Child Abuse and Neglect Reporting Act and are required to comply with the requirements set forth in CSU Executive Order 1083 as a condition of employment. Pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act) and CSU systemwide policy, some faculty duties may lead to designation as a Campus Security Authority (CSA). CSAs are required to complete Clery Act training and to immediately report Clery incidents to the institution.\nFaculty employees must complete CSU employee training as assigned and required based on their role (e.g., Data Security, FERPA, Preventing Discrimination and Harassment, Title IX, Health and Safety). The President may recommend or require compliance with safety measures that decrease the likelihood of COVID-19 transmission or illness and allows the core mission and activities of the campus to continue.\nApplication Procedure\nClick Apply Now to complete the SJSU Online Employment Application and attach the following documents [\ndepartments may specify length of items below (e.g. 2 pages)\n]:\nletter of interest\ncurriculum vitae\ninstitutional excellence statement\n(dedicated statement or addressed in letter of interest)\nstatement of teaching interests/philosophy\nresearch plans\nthree references with contact information (three reference letters to be submitted by semi-ﬁnalists prior to their interview)\nInquiries may be directed to the Department Chair: John Delacruz,\njohn.delacruz@sjsu.edu\nPursuant to California Education Code 89521, applicants will be required to disclose whether they have received a ﬁnal administrative decision or ﬁnal judicial decision determining that they have committed sexual harassment within the last 7 years only after a determination is made that they meet the minimum qualiﬁcations for the position, and before an oﬀer of employment is extended. Applicants who reach the ﬁnal stages of the application process must also sign a release form that authorizes the release of information by the applicant’s current and/or former employers to the CSU concerning any substantiated allegations of misconduct.\nConditional Oﬀer\nThe work for this faculty position may only be performed in the State of California and requires establishing an on-campus presence. Employment is contingent upon US residence and proof of eligibility to work in the United States. Satisfactory completion of background and reference checks (including criminal records and prior employment) are required. SJSU will make a conditional oﬀer of employment, which may be rescinded if the background and reference checks reveal disqualifying information, and/or it is discovered that the candidate knowingly withheld or falsiﬁed information. Failure to satisfactorily complete the background and reference checks may aﬀect the continued employment of a current employee who was conditionally oﬀered the position.\nSan José State University: Silicon Valley's Public University\nLocated in the heart of Silicon Valley — one of the most innovative regions in the world —\nSan José State\nUniversity\nis the founding campus of the 23-campus California State University (CSU) system and the ﬁrst public university in the West. Recognized as a leading transformative educational institution, San José State is an essential partner in the technological, economic, cultural, and social development of Silicon Valley, the Bay Area, and California. SJSU is a top-200 school nationally in research funding and second highest in research productivity in the CSU system. Cutting-edge research, world-class scholarship, student-centered learning, diverse communities, and commitment to social justice, allow SJSU to provide life-changing opportunities and advance the public good locally and globally.\nEqual Employment Statement\nSan José State University prohibits discrimination on the basis of Age, Ancestry, Caste, Color, Disability, Ethnicity, Gender, Gender Expression, Gender Identity, Genetic Information, Marital Status, Medical Condition, Military Status, Nationality, Race, Religion, Religious Creed, Sex, Sexual Orientation, Sex Stereotype, and Veteran Status. This policy applies to all San José State University students, faculty, and staﬀ as well as University programs and activities. Reasonable accommodations are made for applicants with disabilities who self-disclose.\nCampus Security and Fire Safety Notiﬁcation\nPursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, the Annual Security Report (ASR) is also now available for viewing at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Security-Report.pdf\n. The ASR contains the current security and safety-related policy statements, emergency preparedness and evacuation information, crime prevention and Sexual Assault prevention information, and information about drug and alcohol prevention programming. The ASR also contains statistics of Clery crimes for San José State University locations for the three most recent calendar years. A paper copy of the ASR is available upon request by contacting the Oﬃce of the Clery Director by phone at 408-924-1501 or by email at\nclerycompliance@sjsu.edu.\nPursuant to the Higher Education Opportunity Act, the Annual Fire Safety Report (AFSR) is available at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Fire-Safety-Report.pdf\n. The purpose of this report is to disclose statistics for ﬁres that occurred within SJSU on-campus housing facilities for the three most recent calendar years, and to distribute ﬁre safety policies and procedures intended to promote safety on Campus. A paper copy of the AFSR is available upon request by contacting the Housing Oﬃce by phone at 408-795-5600 or by email at\nuhs-frontdesk@sjsu.edu."
    },
    "https://jobs.sjsu.edu/en-us/job/562380/science-education-lecturer-pool": {
      "title": "Science Education Lecturer Pool",
      "description": "Department Summary\nThe\nScience Education Program\nat San José State University invites applications for a part-time lecturer position as a university supervisor to support preservice science teacher development. The Science Education Program is well positioned to meet the increasing demand for well-qualified and prepared science teachers in our community.\nThe program is led by an interdisciplinary team of dedicated teacher-scholars recognized for their commitment to the preparation and ongoing support of justice-centered, socially responsive secondary science teachers. Our faculty work on the transformation of undergraduate science courses and student learning experiences, and they engage in innovative science education research uniting the College of Science with the Lurie College of Education. Science Education offers a Master of Science degree in Science Education that can be done separately or in conjunction with the teaching credential.\nBrief Description of Duties\nDemonstrate awareness of and address the needs of a student population of great diversity–including age, abilities, cultural background, ethnicity, religion, economic background, primary language, sexual orientation, gender identity, and academic preparation–through inclusive course materials, teaching strategies and advisement.\nFaculty shall organize all their classes within the Canvas Learning Management System (LMS).\nAll classes must be offered and meet as scheduled throughout the entire semester or term, in the mode assigned and listed in the schedule of classes (i.e., asynchronous, synchronous, bichronous, in-person, or hybrid).\nRequired Qualifications\nA master’s degree in any science or science education area or education focused field is required.\nWe also require applicants to hold a valid teaching credential (California clear credential or another state's equivalent) and have 5 or more years of teaching experience.\nApplicants must be knowledgeable of the Next Generation Science Standards and active-based teaching practices. They should have the ability to teach and evaluate adult learners.\nApplicants should demonstrate an awareness of and sensitivity to the educational goals of a multicultural population as might have been gained in cross-cultural study, training, teaching and other comparable experience.\nCompensation\nCompensation and salary range placement are determined by qualifications and experience. To learn more about range placement, please see University Policy\nS21-2\n.\nAnticipated starting salary:\nL-A /\nRange 2\n- $5507 - $5959\nL-B /\nRange 3\n- $6221 - $7481\nL-C /\nRange 4\n- $6825 - $9431\nL-D /\nRange 5\n- $8593 - $10347\nRange salaries above are the anticipated monthly salary rate of full-time AY faculty--part-time rates are prorated. There are 6 monthly payments per semester (full-time AY annual salary = rate x 12). See the\nFaculty Salary Schedule\nfor more information.\nApplication Procedure\nClick Apply Now to complete the application and attach the following documents:\nCV\nCover Letter\nList of at least three (3) or as many as five (5) references\nStatement of Teaching Philosophy (optional)\nStatement of Expertise, including professional experience, courses you are qualified to teach\nThis is a continuing open position.\nInquiries may be directed to the Program Director/Chair. Please direct questions to:\nResa Kelly, Program Director/Chair\nScience Education Program\nresa.kelly@sjsu.edu\nPursuant to California Education Code 89521, applicants will be required to disclose whether they have received a final administrative decision or final judicial decision determining that they have committed sexual harassment within the last 7 years only after a determination is made that they meet the minimum qualifications for the position, and before an offer of employment is extended.  Applicants who reach the final stages of the application process must also sign a release form that authorizes the release of information by the applicant’s current and/or former employers to the CSU concerning any substantiated allegations of misconduct.\nEmployment Conditions\nFaculty employees must complete CSU employee training as assigned and required based on their role (e.g., preventing discrimination and harassment, gender equity and Title IX, health and safety). Pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act) and CSU systemwide policy, the faculty member in this position will subject to ongoing review for designation as a Campus Security Authority (CSA). Individuals designated as Campus Security Authorities are required to immediately report Clery incidents to the institution and to complete Clery Act training as determined by the University Clery Director. Questions regarding CSA designation and training can be sent to the Clery Director at\nclerycompliance@sjsu.edu\n. The President may recommend or require compliance with safety measures that decrease the likelihood of COVID-19 transmission or illness and allows the core mission and activities of the campus to continue.\nConditional Offer\nThe work for this faculty position is located in the State of California and requires commuting to the campus. Employment is contingent upon US residence and proof of eligibility to work in the United States. Satisfactory completion of background and reference checks (including criminal records and prior employment) are required. SJSU will make a conditional offer of employment, which may be rescinded if the background and reference checks reveal disqualifying information, and/or it is discovered that the candidate knowingly withheld or falsified information. Failure to satisfactorily complete the background and reference checks may affect the continued employment of a current employee who was conditionally offered the position.\nAppointment is contingent upon budget and enrollment considerations and subject to order of assignment provisions in the collective bargaining agreement between California State University and California Faculty Association. These provisions state the order in which available courses must be assigned to faculty, starting with tenure line faculty and ending with new lecturer appointees.\nSan José State University: Silicon Valley's Public University\nLocated in the heart of Silicon Valley—one of the most innovative and diverse regions in the world—\nSan José State University\nis the founding campus of the 23-campus California State University (CSU) system and the first public university in the West. Recognized as a national leader in transformative higher education, San José State University is an essential contributor to the economic, cultural, and social development of Silicon Valley, the Bay Area, and California. SJSU ranks high nationally in research funding and is second highest in research productivity in the CSU system. Cutting-edge research, combined with world-class scholarship, student-centered learning opportunities, and experiential and interdisciplinary programs, allows SJSU to provide transformative opportunities that advance the public good locally and globally.\nSan José State enrolls more than 36,000 students — many are historically underserved, and around 45% are first-generation and 38% are Pell-recipients. SJSU is a Hispanic Serving Institution (HSI) and an Asian American and Native American Pacific Islander (AANAPISI) Serving Institution. The university’s commitment to social justice extends from its vibrant, inclusive campus to an international network of over 275,000 alumni. As such, San José State is committed to increasing the diversity of its faculty so our disciplines, students, and community can benefit from different and divergent cultural and identity perspectives.\nEqual Employment Statement\nSan José State University prohibits discrimination on the basis of Age, Ancestry, Caste, Color, Disability, Ethnicity, Gender, Gender Expression, Gender Identity, Genetic Information, Marital Status, Medical Condition, Military Status, Nationality, Race, Religion, Religious Creed, Sex, Sexual Orientation, Sex Stereotype, and Veteran Status. This policy applies to all San José State University students, faculty, and staff as well as University programs and activities. Reasonable accommodations are made for applicants with disabilities who self-disclose. Note that all San José State University employees are considered mandated reporters under the California Child Abuse and Neglect Reporting Act and are required to comply with the requirements set forth in CSU Executive Order 1083 as a condition of employment.\nJeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Crime Statistics Act and Campus Housing Fire Safety Notification\nPursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, the Annual Security Report (ASR) is also now available for viewing at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Security-Report.pdf.\nThe ASR contains the current security and safety-related policy statements, emergency preparedness and evacuation information, crime prevention and Sexual Assault prevention information, and information about drug and alcohol prevention programming. The ASR also contains statistics of Clery crimes for San José State University locations for the three most recent calendar years. A paper copy of the ASR is available upon request by contacting the Office of the Clery Director by phone at 408-924-1501 or by email at clerycompliance@sjsu.edu.\nPursuant to the Higher Education Opportunity Act, the Annual Fire Safety Report (AFSR) is also available for viewing at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Fire-Safety-Report.pdf\n. The purpose of this report is to disclose statistics for fires that occurred within SJSU on-campus housing facilities for the three most recent calendar years, and to distribute fire safety policies and procedures intended to promote safety on Campus. A paper copy of the AFSR is available upon request by contacting the Housing Office by phone at 408-795-5600 or by email at uhs-frontdesk@sjsu.edu."
    },
    "https://jobs.sjsu.edu/en-us/job/562688/assistant-professor-in-dietetics-and-nutrition": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Dietetics and Nutrition",
      "description": "Department: Department of Nutrition, Food Science, and Packaging\nPosition Rank and Title:\nAssistant Professor\nSchool/Department Name:\nDepartment of Nutrition, Food Science, and Packaging\nCompensation:\nCommensurate with qualifications, experience, and rank as established by the\nCSU Salary Schedule\n. Anticipated hiring academic year annual salary range: $93,000-$99,000.\nFaculty employee benefits are very competitive for the higher education sector.  See the\nCSU Employee Benefits Summary\nfor details about the CSU’s excellent benefits package.\nTarget Start Date:\nAugust 17, 2027\nApplication Deadline:\nDecember 1, 2026\n(Applications accepted until filled or closed)\nPosition Description\nThe Department of Nutrition, Food Science, and Packaging at San José State University is pleased to announce a search for an Assistant Professor in Dietetics and Nutrition beginning in August 2027.\nWe welcome teacher-scholars whose work applies emerging technologies to questions in nutrition, dietetics, and health. Relevant areas may include digital health, artificial intelligence and machine learning, nutrition informatics, health data science, computational or precision nutrition, mobile and wearable technologies, technology-supported nutrition care or education, and other related applications. We welcome work spanning clinical, community, public health, foodservice, behavioral, population, and educational contexts.\nThe Department of Nutrition, Food Science, and Packaging at San José State University is a dynamic, interdisciplinary academic community committed to preparing students for professional leadership in nutrition, dietetics, food science, food management, and packaging through high-quality teaching, applied research, experiential learning, and engagement with community and industry partners. Established in 1911, the department was the first nutrition department in the California State University system and today offers a B.S. in Nutritional Science with concentrations in Dietetics, and Applied Nutrition and Food Sciences; a B.S. in Packaging; M.S. programs in Nutritional Science and Packaging; the Spartan Accelerated Graduate Education (SAGE) Scholars Program in Nutritional Science and Packaging; an ACEND-accredited Didactic Program in Dietetics; and a Dietetic Internship. A distinctive strength of the department is its Packaging program, one of only a small number of packaging degree programs in the United States, which combines science, engineering, design, and business and benefits from SJSU’s location in Silicon Valley and strong connections with the medical device, pharmaceutical, food, technology, and packaging industries.\nThe department provides students with hands-on learning through laboratories, applied research, internships, professional experiences, and community-based activities, while fostering a diverse, inclusive, and collaborative environment in which faculty, staff, and students work across disciplinary boundaries. Recent student accomplishments in academic, professional, and culinary competitions reflect the department’s emphasis on student achievement and professional preparation. Faculty joining the department will have opportunities to contribute to innovative undergraduate and graduate education, mentor a diverse student population, develop interdisciplinary and applied research programs, collaborate with colleagues and external partners throughout Silicon Valley and the greater Bay Area, and help shape the continued growth of programs addressing evolving challenges in nutrition, food systems, health, and sustainable and innovative packaging. Additional information about the department, its programs, faculty, facilities, and activities is available at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/nufspkg/\n.\nThe Department of Nutrition, Food Science, and Packaging and San José State University value institutional excellence and are committed to advancing equity through research, teaching, and service. We enroll more than 40,000 students, many of whom are historically underserved; approximately 45% of our undergraduate students are first-generation college students and 38% are Pell Grant recipients. In addition, we are both a Hispanic Serving Institution (HSI) and an Asian American and Native American Pacific Islander Serving Institution (AANAPISI). SJSU aspires to create holistic learning environments that facilitate both individual and collective transformation. We seek teacher-scholars who cultivate inclusive, empowering spaces for mutual learning, skill-building, and knowledge exchange for all students. Our commitment to lifelong learning supports meaningful connections among faculty, staff, students, alumni, and the broader communities we serve.\nWe invite all applicants to include a Statement of Institutional Excellence (or incorporate it into your cover letter) to share how your lived and professional experiences will contribute to the SJSU community—particularly in relation to student success. A guide to writing this statement can be found at\nSJSU Institutional Excellence Statement Guidelines\n.\nRequired Qualifications\nAn earned Doctoral degree in Nutrition or a closely related field is required for appointment.\nCurrent Registered Dietitian Nutritionist (RDN) credential or evidence to be credentialed by August 17, 2027.\nEvidence of or potential for university-level teaching effectiveness in dietetics, nutrition or closely related areas.\nRecord of scholarly publications or strong potential for a productive scholarly agenda.\nApplicants should demonstrate an awareness of and sensitivity to the educational goals of a socially and economically diverse student population as might have been gained in cross-cultural study, training, teaching, and other comparable experience.\nPreferred Qualifications\nPriority will be given to candidates who possess one or more of the following:\nExpertise or an emerging research program in health technology, artificial intelligence, digital health, nutrition informatics, health data science, computational nutrition, or related applications to nutrition and dietetics, particularly work that advances ethical, equitable, and evidence-based uses of technology.\nProfessional work experience related to dietetics and nutrition in areas such as clinical nutrition, foodservice, or nutrition education.\nAn active, well-articulated research agenda with potential to secure external funding and develop interdisciplinary collaborations.\nDemonstrated ability or potential to mentor undergraduate and graduate students in academic, research, and professional development settings.\nCommitment to reflective and inclusive teaching practices, including adapting in-person and online instruction to engage and support historically underserved students.\nKey Responsibilities\nDesign and teach introductory and advanced undergraduate and graduate courses in nutrition and dietetics, which may include Lifecycle Nutrition, Nutrition Metabolism, Medical Nutrition Therapy, Research Methods, Biostatistics, and courses aligned with the candidate’s area of expertise; contribute, as appropriate, to integration of emerging technologies into nutrition and dietetics curricula.\nDevelop and maintain an active scholarly agenda, including pursuing external grant funding, disseminating scholarship through publications and professional presentations, and engaging in interdisciplinary collaboration.\nParticipate in curriculum planning, development, assessment, and revision to meet program needs and support the success of a diverse student population.\nAdvise and mentor undergraduate and graduate students in academic, professional, and research development including research design, data analysis, academic writing, grant applications, and scholarly dissemination.\nCollaborate with nutrition and dietetics professionals and community partners to support field experiences, graduate student projects, and curriculum development.\nParticipate in professional organizations related to nutrition/dietetics and related fields.\nThe candidate will participate in shared governance, usually in department, college, and university committees and other service assignments.\nFaculty shall organize all their classes within the Canvas Learning Management System (LMS).\nThe candidate must demonstrate awareness and experience in understanding the needs of a student population of great diversity – in age, abilities, cultural background, ethnicity, religion, economic background, primary language, sexual orientation, gender identity, and academic preparation – through inclusive course materials, teaching strategies, and advisement.\nOther Duties\nNote that all San José State University employees are considered mandated reporters under the California Child Abuse and Neglect Reporting Act and are required to comply with the requirements set forth in CSU Executive Order 1083 as a condition of employment. Pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act) and CSU systemwide policy, some faculty duties may lead to designation as a Campus Security Authority (CSA). CSAs are required to complete Clery Act training and to immediately report Clery incidents to the institution.\nFaculty employees must complete CSU employee training as assigned and required based on their role (e.g., Data Security, FERPA, Preventing Discrimination and Harassment, Title IX, Health and Safety). The President may recommend or require compliance with safety measures that decrease the likelihood of COVID-19 transmission or illness and allows the core mission and activities of the campus to continue.\nApplication Procedure\nClick Apply Now to complete the SJSU Online Employment Application and attach the following documents:\nletter of interest (maximum 2 pages)\ncurriculum vitae\ninstitutional excellence statement\n(dedicated statement or addressed in letter of interest)\nstatement of teaching interests/philosophy (maximum 2 pages).  We encourage applicants to identify courses they feel well qualified to teach.\nresearch plans (maximum 2 pages). We encourage applicants to explain how their research connects to Health Technology.\nThree references with contact information.\nInquiries may be directed to the Search Committee Chair, Dr. Giselle Pignotti at\ngiselle.pignotti@sjsu.edu\n.\nPursuant to California Education Code 89521, applicants will be required to disclose whether they have received a final administrative decision or final judicial decision determining that they have committed sexual harassment within the last 7 years only after a determination is made that they meet the minimum qualifications for the position, and before an offer of employment is extended.  Applicants who reach the final stages of the application process must also sign a release form that authorizes the release of information by the applicant’s current and/or former employers to the CSU concerning any substantiated allegations of misconduct.\nConditional Offer\nThe work for this faculty position may only be performed in the State of California and requires establishing an on-campus presence. Employment is contingent upon US residence and proof of eligibility to work in the United States. Satisfactory completion of background and reference checks (including criminal records and prior employment) are required. SJSU will make a conditional offer of employment, which may be rescinded if the background and reference checks reveal disqualifying information, and/or it is discovered that the candidate knowingly withheld or falsified information. Failure to satisfactorily complete the background and reference checks may affect the continued employment of a current employee who was conditionally offered the position.\nSan José State University: Silicon Valley's Public University\nLocated in the heart of Silicon Valley — one of the most innovative regions in the world —\nSan José State University\nis the founding campus of the 23-campus California State University (CSU) system and the first public university in the West. Recognized as a leading transformative educational institution, San José State is an essential partner in the technological, economic, cultural, and social development of Silicon Valley, the Bay Area, and California. SJSU is a top-200 school nationally in research funding and second highest in research productivity in the CSU system. Cutting-edge research, world-class scholarship, student-centered learning, diverse communities, and commitment to social justice, allow SJSU to provide life-changing opportunities and advance the public good locally and globally.\nEqual Employment Statement\nSan José State University prohibits discrimination on the basis of Age, Ancestry, Caste, Color, Disability, Ethnicity, Gender, Gender Expression, Gender Identity, Genetic Information, Marital Status, Medical Condition, Military Status, Nationality, Race, Religion, Religious Creed, Sex, Sexual Orientation, Sex Stereotype, and Veteran Status. This policy applies to all San José State University students, faculty, and staff as well as University programs and activities. Reasonable accommodations are made for applicants with disabilities who self-disclose.\nCampus Security and Fire Safety Notification\nPursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, the Annual Security Report (ASR) is also now available for viewing at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Security-Report.pdf\n. The ASR contains the current security and safety-related policy statements, emergency preparedness and evacuation information, crime prevention and Sexual Assault prevention information, and information about drug and alcohol prevention programming. The ASR also contains statistics of Clery crimes for San José State University locations for the three most recent calendar years. A paper copy of the ASR is available upon request by contacting the Office of the Clery Director by phone at 408-924-1501 or by email at clerycompliance@sjsu.edu.\nPursuant to the Higher Education Opportunity Act, the Annual Fire Safety Report (AFSR) is available at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Fire-Safety-Report.pdf\n. The purpose of this report is to disclose statistics for fires that occurred within SJSU on-campus housing facilities for the three most recent calendar years, and to distribute fire safety policies and procedures intended to promote safety on Campus. A paper copy of the AFSR is available upon request by contacting the Housing Office by phone at 408-795-5600 or by email at uhs-frontdesk@sjsu.edu."
    },
    "https://jobs.sjsu.edu/en-us/job/562672/assistant-professor-school-of-information-cultural-heritage-informatics-aidigital-transformation-and-critical-technology-studies": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - School of Information - Cultural Heritage Informatics, AI/Digital Transformation, and Critical Technology Studies",
      "description": "Department: School of Information\nPosition Rank and Title:\nAssistant Professor - Cultural Heritage Informatics, AI/Digital Transformation, and Critical Technology Studies\nSchool/Department Name:\nSchool of Information\nCompensation:\nCommensurate with qualifications, experience, and rank as established by the\nCSU Salary Schedule\n. Anticipated hiring academic year annual salary range: $100,000 - $110,000\nFaculty employee benefits are very competitive for the higher education sector.  See the\nCSU Employee Benefits Summary\nfor details about the CSU’s excellent benefits package.\nTarget Start Date:\nAugust 17, 2027\nApplication Deadline:\nNovember 19th, 2026 (Applications accepted until filled or closed)\nPosition Description\nThe School of Information at San José State University is pleased to announce a search for Assistant Professor of Cultural Heritage Informatics, AI/Digital Transformation, and Critical Technology Studies beginning in August 2027.  We especially welcome applicants whose work advances the emerging field of Critical Technology Studies, investigating how technological systems shape knowledge production, cultural memory, access to information, social equity, democratic participation, and institutional practice.\nThe School of Information (iSchool -\nSJSU - School of Information - Information Science School\n) prepares professionals who organize, preserve, analyze, protect, teach, and improve access to information across communities and institutions. Our new faculty member will join a scholarly community with:\nthousands of students and a broad global alumni community;\none of the largest and most diverse schools in the information field;\nfully online graduate programs that support working professionals;\nan ALA-accredited MLIS program;\nan emphasis on access, equity, community service, digital literacy, and ethical technology; and\na commitment to working on problems connected to information access, AI literacy, digital preservation, cybersecurity, and social justice.\nThe School of Information and San José State University value institutional excellence and are committed to advancing equity through research, teaching, and service. We enroll more than 40,000 students, many of whom are historically underserved; approximately 45% of our undergraduate students are first-generation college students and 38% are Pell Grant recipients. In addition, we are both a Hispanic Serving Institution (HSI) and an Asian American and Native American Pacific Islander Serving Institution (AANAPISI). SJSU aspires to create holistic learning environments that facilitate both individual and collective transformation. We seek teacher-scholars who cultivate inclusive, empowering spaces for mutual learning, skill-building, and knowledge exchange for all students. Our commitment to lifelong learning supports meaningful connections among faculty, staff, students, alumni, and the broader communities we serve.\nWe invite all applicants to include a Statement of Institutional Excellence (or incorporate it into your cover letter) to share how your lived and professional experiences will contribute to the SJSU community—particularly in relation to student success. A guide to writing this statement can be found at\nSJSU Institutional Excellence Statement Guidelines\n.\nRequired Qualifications\nEarned doctorate in Library and Information Science, Information Studies, Informatics, Archives, Museum Studies, Digital Humanities, Science and Technology Studies, Computer Science, Data Science, or a closely related field by the time of appointment.\nDemonstrated expertise in cultural heritage collections, archives, libraries, museums, information systems, or related information environments.\nEvidence of scholarship, teaching, or professional practice related to digital transformation, emerging technologies, or critical technology studies.\nThe candidate will be expected to contribute to interdisciplinary initiatives, pursue external funding opportunities, and collaborate with libraries, archives, museums, educational institutions, government agencies, industry partners, and community organizations.\nDemonstrated commitment to student success, accessibility, and community-engaged information practice.\nApplicants should demonstrate an awareness of and sensitivity to the educational goals of a socially and economically diverse student population as might have been gained in cross-cultural study, training, teaching, and other comparable experience.\nPreferred Qualifications\nAn active scholarly agenda examining the relationships among information systems, artificial intelligence, cultural heritage, data infrastructures, communities, and society.\nRecord of collaboration with libraries, archives, museums, schools, technology organizations, policy organizations, or community partners.\nAbility to contribute across multiple School of Information degree programs.\nExperience teaching in online, hybrid, or distributed learning environments.\nKey Responsibilities\nTeach undergraduate and graduate courses that may include these titles (and potentially others):\nArchives and Records Management\nCultural Heritage Informatics\nCritical Technology Studies\nInformation Ethics and the Social Impacts of AI\nTechnology Policy and Governance\n[1]\n[2]\nContribute across multiple graduate programs and support the School's tradition of innovative online education, interdisciplinary collaboration, and socially engaged information practice.\nTeach and collaborate across the School of Information's programs and certificates.\nParticipate in service activities that support the School, University, profession, and broader information community.\nParticipate in shared governance, usually in department, college, and university committees and other service assignments.\nFaculty shall organize all their classes within the Canvas Learning Management System (LMS).\nThe candidate must demonstrate awareness and experience in understanding the needs of a student population of great diversity – in age, abilities, cultural background, ethnicity, religion, economic background, primary language, sexual orientation, gender identity, and academic preparation – through inclusive course materials, teaching strategies, and advisement.\nOther Duties\nNote that all San José State University employees are considered mandated reporters under the California Child Abuse and Neglect Reporting Act and are required to comply with the requirements set forth in CSU Executive Order 1083 as a condition of employment. Pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act) and CSU systemwide policy, some faculty duties may lead to designation as a Campus Security Authority (CSA). CSAs are required to complete Clery Act training and to immediately report Clery incidents to the institution.\nFaculty employees must complete CSU employee training as assigned and required based on their role (e.g., Data Security, FERPA, Preventing Discrimination and Harassment, Title IX, Health and Safety). The President may recommend or require compliance with safety measures that decrease the likelihood of COVID-19 transmission or illness and allows the core mission and activities of the campus to continue.\nApplication Procedure\nClick Apply Now to complete the SJSU Online Employment Application and attach the following documents:\nletter of interest (2 pages max)\ncurriculum vitae\ninstitutional excellence statement\n(dedicated statement or addressed in letter of interest)\nstatement of teaching interests/philosophy (2 pages max)\nresearch plans (2 pages max)\nthree references with contact information\nInquiries may be directed to the Department Chair or Search Committee Chair:\nDr. Kristen Radsliff;\nkristen.radsliff@sjsu.edu\n.\nPursuant to California Education Code 89521, applicants will be required to disclose whether they have received a final administrative decision or final judicial decision determining that they have committed sexual harassment within the last 7 years only after a determination is made that they meet the minimum qualifications for the position, and before an offer of employment is extended.  Applicants who reach the final stages of the application process must also sign a release form that authorizes the release of information by the applicant’s current and/or former employers to the CSU concerning any substantiated allegations of misconduct.\nConditional Offer\nThe work for this faculty position may only be performed in the State of California and requires establishing an on-campus presence.  Employment is contingent upon US residence and proof of eligibility to work in the United States. Satisfactory completion of background and reference checks (including criminal records and prior employment) are required. SJSU will make a conditional offer of employment, which may be rescinded if the background and reference checks reveal disqualifying information, and/or it is discovered that the candidate knowingly withheld or falsified information. Failure to satisfactorily complete the background and reference checks may affect the continued employment of a current employee who was conditionally offered the position.\nSan José State University: Silicon Valley's Public University\nLocated in the heart of Silicon Valley — one of the most innovative regions in the world —\nSan José State University\nis the founding campus of the 23-campus California State University (CSU) system and the first public university in the West. Recognized as a leading transformative educational institution, San José State is an essential partner in the technological, economic, cultural, and social development of Silicon Valley, the Bay Area, and California. SJSU is a top-200 school nationally in research funding and second highest in research productivity in the CSU system. Cutting-edge research, world-class scholarship, student-centered learning, diverse communities, and commitment to social justice, allow SJSU to provide life-changing opportunities and advance the public good locally and globally.\nEqual Employment Statement\nSan José State University prohibits discrimination on the basis of Age, Ancestry, Caste, Color, Disability, Ethnicity, Gender, Gender Expression, Gender Identity, Genetic Information, Marital Status, Medical Condition, Military Status, Nationality, Race, Religion, Religious Creed, Sex, Sexual Orientation, Sex Stereotype, and Veteran Status. This policy applies to all San José State University students, faculty, and staff as well as University programs and activities. Reasonable accommodations are made for applicants with disabilities who self-disclose.\nCampus Security and Fire Safety Notification\nPursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, the Annual Security Report (ASR) is also now available for viewing at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Security-Report.pdf\n. The ASR contains the current security and safety-related policy statements, emergency preparedness and evacuation information, crime prevention and Sexual Assault prevention information, and information about drug and alcohol prevention programming. The ASR also contains statistics of Clery crimes for San José State University locations for the three most recent calendar years. A paper copy of the ASR is available upon request by contacting the Office of the Clery Director by phone at 408-924-1501 or by email at\nclerycompliance@sjsu.edu\n.\nPursuant to the Higher Education Opportunity Act, the Annual Fire Safety Report (AFSR) is available at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Fire-Safety-Report.pdf\n. The purpose of this report is to disclose statistics for fires that occurred within SJSU on-campus housing facilities for the three most recent calendar years, and to distribute fire safety policies and procedures intended to promote safety on Campus. A paper copy of the AFSR is available upon request by contacting the Housing Office by phone at 408-795-5600 or by email at uhs-frontdesk@sjsu.edu."
