    "https://jobs.sjsu.edu/en-us/job/562674/assistant-professor-school-of-information-ethical-ai-information-generation-retrieval-and-knowledge-systems": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - School of Information - Ethical AI, Information Generation & Retrieval, and Knowledge Systems",
      "description": "Department: School of Information\nPosition Rank and Title:\nAssistant Professor - Ethical AI, Information Generation & Retrieval, and Knowledge Systems\nSchool/Department Name:\nSchool of Information\nCompensation:\nCommensurate with qualifications, experience, and rank as established by the\nCSU Salary Schedule\n. Anticipated hiring academic year annual salary range: $100,000 - $110,000\nFaculty employee benefits are very competitive for the higher education sector.  See the\nCSU Employee Benefits Summary\nfor details about the CSU’s excellent benefits package.\nTarget Start Date:\nAugust 17, 2027\nApplication Deadline:\nNovember 19th, 2026 (Applications accepted until filled or closed)\nPosition Description\nThe School of Information at San José State University is pleased to announce a search for Assistant Professor of Ethical AI, Information Generation & Retrieval, and Knowledge Systems beginning in August 2027.The successful candidate will contribute to SJSU's strategic investments in artificial intelligence, large language models, data analytics, and critical technologies through research, teaching, and service that advances the design, evaluation, governance, and use of intelligent information systems. We seek candidates whose work examines how humans and computational systems interact to create, organize, discover, evaluate, and use information in increasingly AI-mediated environments.\nThe successful candidate will contribute to the School's mission of preparing information professionals to serve diverse communities in an increasingly complex information landscape. The School of Information (iSchool -\nSJSU - School of Information - Information Science School\n) prepares professionals who organize, preserve, analyze, protect, teach, and improve access to information across communities and institutions. Our new faculty member will join a scholarly community with:\nthousands of students and a broad global alumni community\none of the largest and most diverse schools in the information field\nfully online graduate programs that support working professionals\nan ALA-accredited MLIS program\nan emphasis on access, equity, community service, digital literacy, and ethical technology\nworking on problems connected to information access, AI literacy, digital preservation, cybersecurity, and social justice.\nThe School of Information and San José State University value institutional excellence and are committed to advancing equity through research, teaching, and service. We enroll more than 40,000 students, many of whom are historically underserved; approximately 45% of our undergraduate students are first-generation college students and 38% are Pell Grant recipients. In addition, we are both a Hispanic Serving Institution (HSI) and an Asian American and Native American Pacific Islander Serving Institution (AANAPISI). SJSU aspires to create holistic learning environments that facilitate both individual and collective transformation. We seek teacher-scholars who cultivate inclusive, empowering spaces for mutual learning, skill-building, and knowledge exchange for all students. Our commitment to lifelong learning supports meaningful connections among faculty, staff, students, alumni, and the broader communities we serve.\nWe invite all applicants to include a Statement of Institutional Excellence (or incorporate it into your cover letter) to share how your lived and professional experiences will contribute to the SJSU community—particularly in relation to student success. A guide to writing this statement can be found at\nSJSU Institutional Excellence Statement Guidelines\n.\nRequired Qualifications\nEarned doctorate in Library and Information Science, Information Science, Informatics, Information Studies, Human-Computer Interaction, Computer Science, Data Science, Knowledge Management, or a closely related field.\nEvidence of scholarship in one or more of the following:\nArtificial intelligence and intelligent information retrieval systems\nCritical Technology Studies\nData stewardship and governance\nApplicants should demonstrate an awareness of and sensitivity to the educational goals of a socially and economically diverse student population as might have been gained in cross-cultural study, training, teaching, and other comparable experience.\nPreferred Qualifications\nExperience applying artificial intelligence, machine learning, or large language models to information discovery, knowledge management, digital curation, user services, or decision support.\nResearch addressing the social, ethical, legal, and policy implications of AI and emerging technologies.\nExperience working in libraries, archives, museums, technology organizations, public-sector organizations, or other information-centered environments.\nEvidence of potential for external funding, industry partnerships, or applied research collaborations.\nExperience teaching in online, hybrid, or distributed learning environments.\nAn understanding of how the fields of information science, library & information science, as well as related fields frame issues connected to:\nTrust, credibility, and information quality\nEthical, legal, and societal implications of AI\nInformation policy and governance\nKey Responsibilities\nDevelop curricula for undergraduate and/or graduate courses across the School of Information’s many programs and certificates, for topics such as:\nHuman and autonomous information retrieval systems\nAI governance, ethics, and policy\nResearch methods and analytics\nAdvise students pursuing careers in libraries, archives, information management, user experience, digital curation, knowledge management, data services, AI-enabled information services, and related professions.\nConduct an active program of research, scholarship, and/or creative activity.\nParticipate in service activities that support the School, University, profession, and broader information community.\nThe candidate will participate in shared governance, usually in department, college, and university committees and other service assignments.\nFaculty shall organize all their classes within the Canvas Learning Management System (LMS).\nThe candidate must demonstrate awareness and experience in understanding the needs of a student population of great diversity – in age, abilities, cultural background, ethnicity, religion, economic background, primary language, sexual orientation, gender identity, and academic preparation – through inclusive course materials, teaching strategies, and advisement.\nOther Duties\nNote that all San José State University employees are considered mandated reporters under the California Child Abuse and Neglect Reporting Act and are required to comply with the requirements set forth in CSU Executive Order 1083 as a condition of employment. Pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act) and CSU systemwide policy, some faculty duties may lead to designation as a Campus Security Authority (CSA). CSAs are required to complete Clery Act training and to immediately report Clery incidents to the institution.\nFaculty employees must complete CSU employee training as assigned and required based on their role (e.g., Data Security, FERPA, Preventing Discrimination and Harassment, Title IX, Health and Safety). The President may recommend or require compliance with safety measures that decrease the likelihood of COVID-19 transmission or illness and allows the core mission and activities of the campus to continue.\nApplication Procedure\nClick Apply Now to complete the SJSU Online Employment Application and attach the following documents:\nletter of interest (2 pages max)\ncurriculum vitae\ninstitutional excellence statement\n(dedicated statement or addressed in letter of interest)\nstatement of teaching interests/philosophy (2 pages max)\nresearch plans (2 pages max)\nthree references with contact information\nInquiries may be directed to the Department Chair or Search Committee Chair:\nDr. Kristen Radsliff;\nkristen.radsliff@sjsu.edu\n.\nPursuant to California Education Code 89521, applicants will be required to disclose whether they have received a final administrative decision or final judicial decision determining that they have committed sexual harassment within the last 7 years only after a determination is made that they meet the minimum qualifications for the position, and before an offer of employment is extended.  Applicants who reach the final stages of the application process must also sign a release form that authorizes the release of information by the applicant’s current and/or former employers to the CSU concerning any substantiated allegations of misconduct.\nConditional Offer\nThe work for this faculty position may only be performed in the State of California and requires establishing an on-campus presence.  Employment is contingent upon US residence and proof of eligibility to work in the United States. Satisfactory completion of background and reference checks (including criminal records and prior employment) are required. SJSU will make a conditional offer of employment, which may be rescinded if the background and reference checks reveal disqualifying information, and/or it is discovered that the candidate knowingly withheld or falsified information. Failure to satisfactorily complete the background and reference checks may affect the continued employment of a current employee who was conditionally offered the position.\nSan José State University: Silicon Valley's Public University\nLocated in the heart of Silicon Valley — one of the most innovative regions in the world —\nSan José State University\nis the founding campus of the 23-campus California State University (CSU) system and the first public university in the West. Recognized as a leading transformative educational institution, San José State is an essential partner in the technological, economic, cultural, and social development of Silicon Valley, the Bay Area, and California. SJSU is a top-200 school nationally in research funding and second highest in research productivity in the CSU system. Cutting-edge research, world-class scholarship, student-centered learning, diverse communities, and commitment to social justice, allow SJSU to provide life-changing opportunities and advance the public good locally and globally.\nEqual Employment Statement\nSan José State University prohibits discrimination on the basis of Age, Ancestry, Caste, Color, Disability, Ethnicity, Gender, Gender Expression, Gender Identity, Genetic Information, Marital Status, Medical Condition, Military Status, Nationality, Race, Religion, Religious Creed, Sex, Sexual Orientation, Sex Stereotype, and Veteran Status. This policy applies to all San José State University students, faculty, and staff as well as University programs and activities. Reasonable accommodations are made for applicants with disabilities who self-disclose.\nCampus Security and Fire Safety Notification\nPursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, the Annual Security Report (ASR) is also now available for viewing at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Security-Report.pdf\n. The ASR contains the current security and safety-related policy statements, emergency preparedness and evacuation information, crime prevention and Sexual Assault prevention information, and information about drug and alcohol prevention programming. The ASR also contains statistics of Clery crimes for San José State University locations for the three most recent calendar years. A paper copy of the ASR is available upon request by contacting the Office of the Clery Director by phone at 408-924-1501 or by email at\nclerycompliance@sjsu.edu\n.\nPursuant to the Higher Education Opportunity Act, the Annual Fire Safety Report (AFSR) is available at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Fire-Safety-Report.pdf\n. The purpose of this report is to disclose statistics for fires that occurred within SJSU on-campus housing facilities for the three most recent calendar years, and to distribute fire safety policies and procedures intended to promote safety on Campus. A paper copy of the AFSR is available upon request by contacting the Housing Office by phone at 408-795-5600 or by email at uhs-frontdesk@sjsu.edu."
    },
    "https://jobs.sjsu.edu/en-us/job/562676/assistant-professor-school-of-information-youth-information-ecosystems-critical-technology-studies-and-ai": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - School of Information - Youth Information Ecosystems, Critical Technology Studies, and AI",
      "description": "Department: School of Information\nPosition Rank and Title:\nAssistant Professor - Youth Information Ecosystems, Critical Technology Studies, and AI Literacy\nSchool/Department Name:\nSchool of Information\nCompensation:\nCommensurate with qualifications, experience, and rank as established by the\nCSU Salary Schedule\n. Anticipated hiring academic year annual salary range: $100,000 - $110,000\nFaculty employee benefits are very competitive for the higher education sector.  See the\nCSU Employee Benefits Summary\nfor details about the CSU’s excellent benefits package.\nTarget Start Date:\nAugust 17, 2027\nApplication Deadline:\nNovember 19th, 2026 (Applications accepted until filled or closed)\nPosition Description\nThe School of Information at San José State University is pleased to announce a search for Assistant Professor of Youth Information Ecosystems, Critical Technology Studies, and AI Literacy beginning in August 2027.  We seek a scholar and educator whose work examines how children, adolescents, and emerging adults engage with information, digital technologies, social media, artificial intelligence, and networked environments, particularly in relation to issues of equity, access, identity, learning, participation, and well-being.\nThe successful candidate will contribute to the School's mission of preparing information professionals to serve diverse communities in an increasingly complex information landscape. In addition to advancing scholarship and teaching related to youth, information, media, and technology, the successful candidate will provide curricular leadership and strategic vision for the Teacher Librarian Program, strengthening its relevance in a rapidly changing educational and technological environment while expanding its national and international reach. The School of Information (iSchool -\nSJSU - School of Information - Information Science School\n) prepares professionals who organize, preserve, analyze, protect, teach, and improve access to information across communities and institutions. Our new faculty member will join a scholarly community with:\nthousands of students and a broad global alumni community\none of the largest and most diverse schools in the information field\nfully online graduate programs that support working professionals\nan ALA-accredited MLIS program\nan emphasis on access, equity, community service, digital literacy, and ethical technology\nworking on problems connected to information access, AI literacy, digital preservation, cybersecurity, and social justice.\nThe School of Information and San José State University value institutional excellence and are committed to advancing equity through research, teaching, and service. We enroll more than 40,000 students, many of whom are historically underserved; approximately 45% of our undergraduate students are first-generation college students and 38% are Pell Grant recipients. In addition, we are both a Hispanic Serving Institution (HSI) and an Asian American and Native American Pacific Islander Serving Institution (AANAPISI). SJSU aspires to create holistic learning environments that facilitate both individual and collective transformation. We seek teacher-scholars who cultivate inclusive, empowering spaces for mutual learning, skill-building, and knowledge exchange for all students. Our commitment to lifelong learning supports meaningful connections among faculty, staff, students, alumni, and the broader communities we serve.\nWe invite all applicants to include a Statement of Institutional Excellence (or incorporate it into your cover letter) to share how your lived and professional experiences will contribute to the SJSU community—particularly in relation to student success. A guide to writing this statement can be found at\nSJSU Institutional Excellence Statement Guidelines\n.\nRequired Qualifications\nEarned doctorate in Library and Information Science, Information Science, Informatics, Information Studies, Instructional Design, Education, or a closely related field.\nDemonstrated expertise in several of the following:\nyouth information behavior/practices\nmedia/digital literacies\nemerging technologies\nteacher/school librarianship\ncritical technology/social media studies.\nAbility to provide curricular thought leadership for the Teacher Librarian Program.\nEvidence of the capacity to develop and maintain an active program of research, scholarship, and/or professional engagement focused on youth and information, media, technology, and learning.\nApplicants should demonstrate an awareness of and sensitivity to the educational goals of a socially and economically diverse student population as might have been gained in cross-cultural study, training, teaching, and other comparable experience.\nPreferred Qualifications\nProfessional experience as a school librarian, teacher librarian, school library coordinator, educational technology specialist, or related educational leader.\nEvidence of scholarship, research, or professional leadership related to artificial intelligence, critical technology studies, youth and technology, digital citizenship, platform studies, information ethics, educational technology, or the future of school libraries.\nExperience developing partnerships with schools, districts, educational agencies, professional associations, libraries, community organizations, or international educational institutions.\nPotential to contribute to interdisciplinary research and teaching initiatives related to AI, information science, education, ethics, technology policy, or human-centered technology design.\nDemonstrated expertise needed to shape the future of teacher librarianship at a time of significant technological and social change.\nKey Responsibilities\nTeach graduate and undergraduate courses in areas related to critical technology studies, multiliteracies, teacher librarianship, and information practices.\nProvide curricular thought leadership for the Teacher Librarian Program.\nDevelop and maintain an active program of research, scholarship, and/or professional engagement.\nMentor and advise students from diverse professional and academic backgrounds.\nTeach and collaborate across the School of Information's programs and certificates.\nParticipate in service activities that support the School, University, profession, and broader community.\nThe candidate will participate in shared governance, usually in department, college, and university committees and other service assignments.\nFaculty shall organize all their classes within the Canvas Learning Management System (LMS).\nThe candidate must demonstrate awareness and experience in understanding the needs of a student population of great diversity – in age, abilities, cultural background, ethnicity, religion, economic background, primary language, sexual orientation, gender identity, and academic preparation – through inclusive course materials, teaching strategies, and advisement.\nOther Duties\nNote that all San José State University employees are considered mandated reporters under the California Child Abuse and Neglect Reporting Act and are required to comply with the requirements set forth in CSU Executive Order 1083 as a condition of employment. Pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act) and CSU systemwide policy, some faculty duties may lead to designation as a Campus Security Authority (CSA). CSAs are required to complete Clery Act training and to immediately report Clery incidents to the institution.\nFaculty employees must complete CSU employee training as assigned and required based on their role (e.g., Data Security, FERPA, Preventing Discrimination and Harassment, Title IX, Health and Safety). The President may recommend or require compliance with safety measures that decrease the likelihood of COVID-19 transmission or illness and allows the core mission and activities of the campus to continue.\nApplication Procedure\nClick Apply Now to complete the SJSU Online Employment Application and attach the following documents:\nletter of interest (2 pages max)\ncurriculum vitae\ninstitutional excellence statement\n(dedicated statement or addressed in letter of interest)\nstatement of teaching interests/philosophy (2 pages max)\nresearch plans (2 pages max)\nthree references with contact information\nInquiries may be directed to the Department Chair or Search Committee Chair:\nDr. Kristen Radsliff;\nkristen.radsliff@sjsu.edu\n.\nPursuant to California Education Code 89521, applicants will be required to disclose whether they have received a final administrative decision or final judicial decision determining that they have committed sexual harassment within the last 7 years only after a determination is made that they meet the minimum qualifications for the position, and before an offer of employment is extended.  Applicants who reach the final stages of the application process must also sign a release form that authorizes the release of information by the applicant’s current and/or former employers to the CSU concerning any substantiated allegations of misconduct.\nConditional Offer\nThe work for this faculty position may only be performed in the State of California and requires establishing an on-campus presence.  Employment is contingent upon US residence and proof of eligibility to work in the United States. Satisfactory completion of background and reference checks (including criminal records and prior employment) are required. SJSU will make a conditional offer of employment, which may be rescinded if the background and reference checks reveal disqualifying information, and/or it is discovered that the candidate knowingly withheld or falsified information. Failure to satisfactorily complete the background and reference checks may affect the continued employment of a current employee who was conditionally offered the position.\nSan José State University: Silicon Valley's Public University\nLocated in the heart of Silicon Valley — one of the most innovative regions in the world —\nSan José State University\nis the founding campus of the 23-campus California State University (CSU) system and the first public university in the West. Recognized as a leading transformative educational institution, San José State is an essential partner in the technological, economic, cultural, and social development of Silicon Valley, the Bay Area, and California. SJSU is a top-200 school nationally in research funding and second highest in research productivity in the CSU system. Cutting-edge research, world-class scholarship, student-centered learning, diverse communities, and commitment to social justice, allow SJSU to provide life-changing opportunities and advance the public good locally and globally.\nEqual Employment Statement\nSan José State University prohibits discrimination on the basis of Age, Ancestry, Caste, Color, Disability, Ethnicity, Gender, Gender Expression, Gender Identity, Genetic Information, Marital Status, Medical Condition, Military Status, Nationality, Race, Religion, Religious Creed, Sex, Sexual Orientation, Sex Stereotype, and Veteran Status. This policy applies to all San José State University students, faculty, and staff as well as University programs and activities. Reasonable accommodations are made for applicants with disabilities who self-disclose.\nCampus Security and Fire Safety Notification\nPursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, the Annual Security Report (ASR) is also now available for viewing at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Security-Report.pdf\n. The ASR contains the current security and safety-related policy statements, emergency preparedness and evacuation information, crime prevention and Sexual Assault prevention information, and information about drug and alcohol prevention programming. The ASR also contains statistics of Clery crimes for San José State University locations for the three most recent calendar years. A paper copy of the ASR is available upon request by contacting the Office of the Clery Director by phone at 408-924-1501 or by email at clerycompliance@sjsu.edu.\nPursuant to the Higher Education Opportunity Act, the Annual Fire Safety Report (AFSR) is available at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Fire-Safety-Report.pdf\n. The purpose of this report is to disclose statistics for fires that occurred within SJSU on-campus housing facilities for the three most recent calendar years, and to distribute fire safety policies and procedures intended to promote safety on Campus. A paper copy of the AFSR is available upon request by contacting the Housing Office by phone at 408-795-5600 or by email at uhs-frontdesk@sjsu.edu."
    },
    "https://jobs.sjsu.edu/en-us/job/562255/assistant-professor-aviation-aircraft-systems-professional-flight-andor-aviation-management": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor – Aviation - Aircraft Systems, Professional Flight, and/or Aviation Management",
      "description": "Department: Department of Aviation and Technology, College of Engineering\nPosition Rank and Title:\nAssistant Professor/Aviation (Aircraft Systems and/or Professional Flight)\nSchool/Department Name:\nDepartment of Aviation and Technology, College of Engineering\nCompensation:\nCommensurate with qualifications, experience, and rank as established by the\nCSU Salary Schedule\n. Anticipated hiring academic year annual salary range: $100,000 - $120,000.\nFaculty employee benefits are very competitive for the higher education sector.  See the\nCSU Employee Benefits Summary\nfor details about the CSU’s excellent benefits package.\nTarget Start Date:\nAugust 17, 2027\nApplication Deadline:\nJanuary 24, 2027 (Applications accepted until filled or closed)\nPosition Description\nThe Department of Aviation and Technology at San José State University is pleased to announce a search for a tenure-track faculty position at the rank of Assistant Professor in the area of Aircraft Systems and/or Professional Flight. The incumbent will be required to teach courses primarily in aircraft systems design, principles of flight, flight controls and avionics, flight operations, and courses in the General Aviation area such as air traffic management and aviation safety and management. In addition, the incumbent is expected to carry out sponsored research and projects involving students.\nThe Department of Aviation and Technology is the oldest department in the College of Engineering. The\nengineering technology\nis ranked #1 (Collegefactual.com, 2026) and the\naviation program\nis ranked #2 in the nation in 2026. The Department is the fastest growing department in the college attracting students at both freshman and transfer levels. The Department has an enrollment of 1,214 students in its programs and has a strong relationship with local industry through continuous support of its Industry Advisory Boards. The Department has had a long-standing internship program as part of its curriculum to allow students to gain industry experience while industry evaluates such students as long-term potential employees. Our full-time faculty are all active researchers and are successful in attracting major funding from industry. The Department of Aviation and Technology website is at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/avtech/index.php\nThe Department offers a B.S. degree program in Aviation, a B.S. degree program and a MS degree program in Technology. The B.S. degree program in Aviation has four options: 1) Professional Flight, 2) Airline Operations, 3) Aviation Management, and 4) Maintenance Management.\nThe B.S. degree in Technology has two concentrations: 1) Computer Network System Management (CNSM) and 2) Manufacturing Systems. The CNSM prepares students to meet the demand by industry in recruiting graduates with knowledge, skills, and experience in Computer Network Systems. The Manufacturing Systems prepares students with modern tools related to Smart Manufacturing, Industry 4.0, Industrial Internet of Things (IIoT), and data analytics in Advance Factory Operation and Modern Manufacturing Ecosystem.\nThe Department of Aviation and Technology and San José State University value institutional excellence and are committed to advancing equity through research, teaching, and service. We enroll more than 40,000 students, many of whom are historically underserved; approximately 45% of our undergraduate students are first-generation college students and 38% are Pell Grant recipients. In addition, we are both a Hispanic Serving Institution (HSI) and an Asian American and Native American Pacific Islander Serving Institution (AANAPISI). SJSU aspires to create holistic learning environments that facilitate both individual and collective transformation. We seek teacher-scholars who cultivate inclusive, empowering spaces for mutual learning, skill-building, and knowledge exchange for all students. Our commitment to lifelong learning supports meaningful connections among faculty, staff, students, alumni, and the broader communities we serve.\nWe invite all applicants to include a Statement of Institutional Excellence (or incorporate it into your cover letter) to share how your lived and professional experiences will contribute to the SJSU community—particularly in relation to student success. A guide to writing this statement can be found at\nSJSU Institutional Excellence Statement Guidelines\n.\nRequired Qualifications\nA Doctorate degree in Aviation/Aeronautics or aviation-related field (ABD may be considered provided the degree will be completed before appointment.); or a master’s degree in aviation/aeronautics or aviation-related field with significant aviation industry experience\nAn Airframe and Powerplant (A&P) certificate with direct experience in aircraft maintenance; or valid pilot licenses and ratings, including Commercial Pilot License (CPL) with an Instrument Rating (IR); or an extensive track record of aviation-related research.\nApplicants must demonstrate a strong commitment to: (a) teaching and working with a diverse population of students, (b) curricular innovation to complement and further advance the program, (c) laboratory development that enhances the quality of education and research, (d) development of externally funded research programs and laboratory capabilities, and (e) publications of research work in peer-reviewed research journals and professional conferences.\nApplicants should demonstrate an awareness of and sensitivity to the educational goals of a socially and economically diverse student population as might have been gained in cross-cultural study, training, teaching, and other comparable experience.\nPreferred Qualifications\nPriority will be given to candidates who possess one or more of the following:\nExperience and a strong research record in one of the following areas: aircraft systems design, flight operation and management, aviation data analytics, and air traffic control.\nExtensive teaching experience relevant to the aviation field in the higher education\nA record of developing competitive grant proposals and securing external funding or industry sponsorship.\nAbility to contribute to program assessment, accreditation activities, and student recruitment.\nKey Responsibilities\nFaculty in the Aviation Program are expected to have expertise in the subject matter of the classes and labs they teach.\nThe regular teaching duties of the faculty include: (a) Developing all necessary course materials, (b) Teaching the assigned classes in accordance with standard SJSU practices, (c) Grading course assignments, examinations and project reports, (d) Enforcing academic policies in the assigned courses, such as academic integrity violations and reporting these to the appropriate authorities, (e) Coordinating the activities of teaching assistants/graders who may have been assigned to the courses, (f) Posting the final grades in a timely manner at the end of the semester, and (g) contributing to the regular assessment and continuous improvement of teaching effectiveness at both course and program levels.\nFaculty are also expected to maintain a student-centric approach at all times, thus contributing to the development of students as productive, responsible, and caring citizens of the world. This includes maintaining an active research lab that provides research opportunities for students.\nFaculty will participate in shared governance, usually in department, college, and university committees and other service assignments.\nFaculty shall organize all their classes within the Californian State University (CSU) Canvas Learning Management System (LMS).\nThe candidate must demonstrate awareness and experience in understanding the needs of a student population of great diversity – in age, abilities, cultural background, ethnicity, religion, economic background, primary language, sexual orientation, gender identity, and academic preparation – through inclusive course materials, teaching strategies, and advisement.\nOther Duties\nNote that all San José State University employees are considered mandated reporters under the California Child Abuse and Neglect Reporting Act and are required to comply with the requirements set forth in CSU Executive Order 1083 as a condition of employment. Pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act) and CSU systemwide policy, some faculty duties may lead to designation as a Campus Security Authority (CSA). CSAs are required to complete Clery Act training and to immediately report Clery incidents to the institution.\nFaculty employees must complete CSU employee training as assigned and required based on their role (e.g., Data Security, FERPA, Preventing Discrimination and Harassment, Title IX, Health and Safety). The President may recommend or require compliance with safety measures that decrease the likelihood of COVID-19 transmission or illness and allows the core mission and activities of the campus to continue.\nApplication Procedure\nClick Apply Now to complete the\nSJSU Online Employment Application\nand attach the following documents:\nLetter of interest (2 pages max.)\nCurriculum vitae (CV)\nInstitutional excellence statement\n(dedicated statement or addressed in letter of interest) (2 pages max.)\nStatement of teaching interests/philosophy (2 pages max.)\nResearch plans (2 pages max.)\nThree letters of reference with contact information or three references whom we may contact for letters\nInquiries may be directed to the Department Chair: Dr. Chien-Tsung Lu, chien-tsung.lu@sjsu.edu\nPursuant to California Education Code 89521, applicants will be required to disclose whether they have received a final administrative decision or final judicial decision determining that they have committed sexual harassment within the last 7 years only after a determination is made that they meet the minimum qualifications for the position, and before an offer of employment is extended.  Applicants who reach the final stages of the application process must also sign a release form that authorizes the release of information by the applicant’s current and/or former employers to the CSU concerning any substantiated allegations of misconduct.\nConditional Offer\nThe work for this faculty position may only be performed in the State of California and requires establishing an on-campus presence. Employment is contingent upon US residence and proof of eligibility to work in the United States. Satisfactory completion of background and reference checks (including criminal records and prior employment) are required. SJSU will make a conditional offer of employment, which may be rescinded if the background and reference checks reveal disqualifying information, and/or it is discovered that the candidate knowingly withheld or falsified information. Failure to satisfactorily complete the background and reference checks may affect the continued employment of a current employee who was conditionally offered the position.\nSan José State University: Silicon Valley's Public University\nLocated in the heart of Silicon Valley — one of the most innovative regions in the world —\nSan José State University\nis the founding campus of the 23-campus California State University (CSU) system and the first public university in the West. Recognized as a leading transformative educational institution, San José State is an essential partner in the technological, economic, cultural, and social development of Silicon Valley, the Bay Area, and California. SJSU is a top-200 school nationally in research funding and second highest in research productivity in the CSU system. Cutting-edge research, world-class scholarship, student-centered learning, diverse communities, and commitment to social justice, allow SJSU to provide life-changing opportunities and advance the public good locally and globally.\nEqual Employment Statement\nSan José State University prohibits discrimination on the basis of Age, Ancestry, Caste, Color, Disability, Ethnicity, Gender, Gender Expression, Gender Identity, Genetic Information, Marital Status, Medical Condition, Military Status, Nationality, Race, Religion, Religious Creed, Sex, Sexual Orientation, Sex Stereotype, and Veteran Status. This policy applies to all San José State University students, faculty, and staff as well as University programs and activities. Reasonable accommodations are made for applicants with disabilities who self-disclose.\nCampus Security and Fire Safety Notification\nPursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, the Annual Security Report (ASR) is also now available for viewing at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Security-Report.pdf\n. The ASR contains the current security and safety-related policy statements, emergency preparedness and evacuation information, crime prevention and Sexual Assault prevention information, and information about drug and alcohol prevention programming. The ASR also contains statistics of Clery crimes for San José State University locations for the three most recent calendar years. A paper copy of the ASR is available upon request by contacting the Office of the Clery Director by phone at 408-924-1501 or by email at clerycompliance@sjsu.edu.\nPursuant to the Higher Education Opportunity Act, the Annual Fire Safety Report (AFSR) is available at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Fire-Safety-Report.pdf\n. The purpose of this report is to disclose statistics for fires that occurred within SJSU on-campus housing facilities for the three most recent calendar years, and to distribute fire safety policies and procedures intended to promote safety on Campus. A paper copy of the AFSR is available upon request by contacting the Housing Office by phone at 408-795-5600 or by email at uhs-frontdesk@sjsu.edu."
    },
    "https://jobs.sjsu.edu/en-us/job/562256/assistant-professor-technology-computer-network-system-management-cnsm": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor – Technology - Computer Network System Management (CNSM)",
      "description": "Department: Department of Aviation and Technology, College of Engineering\nPosition Rank and Title:\nAssistant Professor in Engineering Technology (Computer System Analyst, Network & Systems Administrator, and Network Architect Management)\nSchool/Department Name:\nDepartment of Aviation and Technology, College of Engineering\nCompensation:\nCommensurate with qualifications, experience, and rank as established by the\nCSU Salary Schedule\n. Anticipated hiring academic year annual salary range: $100,000 - $120,000.\nFaculty employee benefits are very competitive for the higher education sector.  See the\nCSU Employee Benefits Summary\nfor details about the CSU’s excellent benefits package.\nTarget Start Date:\nAugust 2027\nApplication Deadline:\nJanuary 24, 2027 (Applications accepted until filled or closed)\nPosition Description\nThe Department of Aviation and Technology at San José State University is pleased to announce a search for a tenure-track faculty position at the rank of Assistant Professor in the Computer Network System Management (CNSM) Concentration. The incumbent will be required to teach undergraduate and graduate level courses primarily related to software quality management, mobile technology, edge computing, and courses in the General Networks area, including introduction to networks, network administration, Internet of things, cloud computing, and database management. The incumbent is also expected to carry out sponsored research and projects involving students.\nThe Department of Aviation and Technology is the oldest department in the College of Engineering. The\nengineering technology\nprogram is ranked #1 (Collegefactual.com, 2026) and the\naviation program\nis ranked #2 in the nation in 2026. The Department is the fastest growing department in the college attracting students at both freshman and transfer levels. The Department has an enrollment of 1,214 students in its programs and has a strong relationship with local industry through continuous support of its Industry Advisory Boards. The Department has had a long-standing internship program as part of its curriculum to allow students to gain industry experience while industry evaluates such students as long-term potential employees. Our full-time faculty are all active researchers and are successful in attracting major funding from industry. The Department of Aviation and Technology website is at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/avtech/index.php\nThe Department offers a BS degree program in Aviation, a BS degree program and a MS degree program in Technology. The BS degree program in Aviation has four options: 1) Professional Flight, 2) Airline Operations, 3) Aviation Management, and 4) Maintenance Management.\nThe BS degree in Technology has two concentrations: 1) Computer Network System Management (CNSM) and 2) Manufacturing Systems. The Computer Network System Management prepares students to meet the demand by industry in recruiting graduates with knowledge, skills, and experience in Computer Network Systems. This concentration utilizes the most advanced computer network equipment laboratory sponsored by industry. Students of CNSM enjoy internship opportunities at many local high-tech industries, including Cisco, Juniper Network, Palo Alto Network, NASA Ames Research Center, and Lockheed-Martin. The Manufacturing Systems prepares students with modern tools related to Smart Manufacturing, Industry 4.0, Industrial Internet of Things (IIoT), and data analytics in Advance Factory Operation and Modern Manufacturing Ecosystem. Our students in BS and MS technology programs are engaged in academic research with faculty, or work on projects with industries.\nThe Department of Aviation and Technology and San José State University value institutional excellence and are committed to advancing equity through research, teaching, and service. We enroll more than 40,000 students, many of whom are historically underserved; approximately 45% of our undergraduate students are first-generation college students and 38% are Pell Grant recipients. In addition, we are both a Hispanic Serving Institution (HSI) and an Asian American and Native American Pacific Islander Serving Institution (AANAPISI). SJSU aspires to create holistic learning environments that facilitate both individual and collective transformation. We seek teacher-scholars who cultivate inclusive, empowering spaces for mutual learning, skill-building, and knowledge exchange for all students. Our commitment to lifelong learning supports meaningful connections among faculty, staff, students, alumni, and the broader communities we serve.\nWe invite all applicants to include a Statement of Institutional Excellence (or incorporate it into your cover letter) to share how your lived and professional experiences will contribute to the SJSU community—particularly in relation to student success. A guide to writing this statement can be found at\nSJSU Institutional Excellence Statement Guidelines\n.\nRequired Qualifications\nA Doctorate degree in an engineering or technology related field such as computer science,     computer engineering, electrical, electronics, computer networking, telecommunications, software, etc.\nApplicants must demonstrate a strong commitment to: (a) teaching and working with a diverse population of undergraduate and graduate students, (b) curricular innovation to complement and further advance the program, (c) laboratory development that enhances the quality of education and research, (d) development of externally funded research programs and laboratory capabilities, and (e) publications of research work in peer-reviewed research journals and professional conferences.\nApplicants should demonstrate an awareness of and sensitivity to the educational goals of a socially and economically diverse student population as might have been gained in cross-cultural study, training, teaching, and other comparable experience.\nPreferred Qualifications\nPriority will be given to candidates who possess one or more of the following:\nExperience in teaching undergraduate and graduate courses related to software quality, wireless/mobile technologies, IoT, cybersecurity, edge and cloud computing.\nExtensive industry and/or academic research experience relevant to computer network systems.\nResearch focus on software quality, wireless/mobile technologies, IoT, edge and cloud computing\nGrant awards and research proposal writing experience\nKey Responsibilities\nFaculty in the Engineering Technology Program are expected to have expertise in the subject matter of the classes and labs they teach.\nThe regular teaching duties of the faculty include: (a) Developing all necessary course materials, (b) Teaching the assigned classes in accordance with standard SJSU practices, (c) Grading course assignments, examinations and project reports, (d) Enforcing academic policies in the assigned courses, such as academic integrity violations and reporting these to the appropriate authorities, (e) Coordinating the activities of teaching assistants/graders who may have been assigned to the courses, (f) Posting the final grades in a timely manner at the end of the semester, and (g) contributing to the regular assessment and continuous improvement of teaching effectiveness at both course and program levels.\nFaculty are also expected to maintain a student-centric approach at all times, thus contributing to the development of students as productive, responsible, and caring citizens of the world. This includes maintaining an active research lab that provides research opportunities for undergraduate and graduate students.\nFaculty will participate in shared governance, usually in department, college, and university committees and other service assignments.\nFaculty shall organize all their classes within the Canvas Learning Management System (LMS).\nThe candidate must demonstrate awareness and experience in understanding the needs of a student population of great diversity – in age, abilities, cultural background, ethnicity, religion, economic background, primary language, sexual orientation, gender identity, and academic preparation – through inclusive course materials, teaching strategies, and advisement.\nOther Duties\nNote that all San José State University employees are considered mandated reporters under the California Child Abuse and Neglect Reporting Act and are required to comply with the requirements set forth in CSU Executive Order 1083 as a condition of employment. Pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act) and CSU systemwide policy, some faculty duties may lead to designation as a Campus Security Authority (CSA). CSAs are required to complete Clery Act training and to immediately report Clery incidents to the institution.\nFaculty employees must complete CSU employee training as assigned and required based on their role (e.g., Data Security, FERPA, Preventing Discrimination and Harassment, Title IX, Health and Safety). The President may recommend or require compliance with safety measures that decrease the likelihood of COVID-19 transmission or illness and allows the core mission and activities of the campus to continue.\nApplication Procedure\nClick Apply Now to complete the SJSU Online Employment Application and attach the following documents:\nLetter of interest (2 pages max.)\ncurriculum vitae\nInstitutional excellence statement\n(dedicated statement or addressed in letter of interest) (2 pages max.)\nStatement of teaching interests/philosophy (2 pages max.)\nResearch plans (2 pages max.)\nThree letters of reference with contact information or three references whom we may contact for letters\nInquiries may be directed to the Department Chair: Dr. Chien-Tsung Lu,\nchien-tsung.lu@sjsu.edu\nPursuant to California Education Code 89521, applicants will be required to disclose whether they have received a final administrative decision or final judicial decision determining that they have committed sexual harassment within the last 7 years only after a determination is made that they meet the minimum qualifications for the position, and before an offer of employment is extended. Applicants who reach the final stages of the application process must also sign a release form that authorizes the release of information by the applicant’s current and/or former employers to the CSU concerning any substantiated allegations of misconduct.\nConditional Offer\nThe work for this faculty position may only be performed in the State of California and requires establishing an on-campus presence. Employment is contingent upon US residence and proof of eligibility to work in the United States. Satisfactory completion of background and reference checks (including criminal records and prior employment) are required. SJSU will make a conditional offer of employment, which may be rescinded if the background and reference checks reveal disqualifying information, and/or it is discovered that the candidate knowingly withheld or falsified information. Failure to satisfactorily complete the background and reference checks may affect the continued employment of a current employee who was conditionally offered the position.\nSan José State University: Silicon Valley's Public University\nLocated in the heart of Silicon Valley — one of the most innovative regions in the world —\nSan José State University\nis the founding campus of the 23-campus California State University (CSU) system and the first public university in the West. Recognized as a leading transformative educational institution, San José State is an essential partner in the technological, economic, cultural, and social development of Silicon Valley, the Bay Area, and California. SJSU is a top-200 school nationally in research funding and second highest in research productivity in the CSU system. Cutting-edge research, world-class scholarship, student-centered learning, diverse communities, and commitment to social justice, allow SJSU to provide life-changing opportunities and advance the public good locally and globally.\nEqual Employment Statement\nSan José State University prohibits discrimination on the basis of Age, Ancestry, Caste, Color, Disability, Ethnicity, Gender, Gender Expression, Gender Identity, Genetic Information, Marital Status, Medical Condition, Military Status, Nationality, Race, Religion, Religious Creed, Sex, Sexual Orientation, Sex Stereotype, and Veteran Status. This policy applies to all San José State University students, faculty, and staff as well as University programs and activities. Reasonable accommodations are made for applicants with disabilities who self-disclose.\nCampus Security and Fire Safety Notification\nPursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, the Annual Security Report (ASR) is also now available for viewing at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Security-Report.pdf\n. The ASR contains the current security and safety-related policy statements, emergency preparedness and evacuation information, crime prevention and Sexual Assault prevention information, and information about drug and alcohol prevention programming. The ASR also contains statistics of Clery crimes for San José State University locations for the three most recent calendar years. A paper copy of the ASR is available upon request by contacting the Office of the Clery Director by phone at 408-924-1501 or by email at clerycompliance@sjsu.edu.\nPursuant to the Higher Education Opportunity Act, the Annual Fire Safety Report (AFSR) is available at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Fire-Safety-Report.pdf\n. The purpose of this report is to disclose statistics for fires that occurred within SJSU on-campus housing facilities for the three most recent calendar years, and to distribute fire safety policies and procedures intended to promote safety on Campus. A paper copy of the AFSR is available upon request by contacting the Housing Office by phone at 408-795-5600 or by email at uhs-frontdesk@sjsu.edu."
    },
    "https://jobs.sjsu.edu/en-us/job/561655/assistant-professor-graphic-design": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Graphic Design",
      "description": "Department: Department of Design\nJob Number:\n561655\nPosition Rank and Title:\nAssistant Professor, Graphic Design\nSchool/Department Name:\nDepartment of Design\nCompensation:\nCommensurate with qualifications, experience, and rank as established by the\nCSU Salary Schedule\n. Anticipated hiring academic year annual salary range: $90,000–$94,000 depending on qualifications.\nFaculty employee benefits are very competitive for the higher education sector. See the\nCSU Employee Benefits Summary\nfor details about the CSU’s excellent benefits package.\nTarget Start Date:\nAugust 17, 2027 (Fall 2027)\nApplication Deadline:\nJanuary 8, 2027.\nPosition Description\nThe Department of Design at San José State University invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position in Graphic Design, beginning in Fall 2027. This is a nine-month, academic-year appointment. We seek a designer, educator, and researcher whose practice bridges analog and digital forms, and who can teach across the undergraduate and graduate graphic design curriculum, including foundational studios, typography, visual systems, interaction design, and advanced electives aligned with the candidate's expertise. The ideal candidate will bring a strong foundation in typography, visual systems, and critical and experimental approaches to design-making, with agility in contemporary digital tools, technologies, and emerging practices.\nWe are particularly interested in designers who expand the possibilities of graphic design through experimentation, systems thinking, collaboration, and engagement with contemporary social and technological questions. The ideal candidate demonstrates formal rigor, conceptual depth, and an active practice that contributes new knowledge through creative practice, design research, scholarship, or public engagement. The department recognizes multiple forms of scholarly and creative activity, including peer-reviewed publications, exhibitions, commissioned work, funded research, public scholarship, and other contributions that advance the discipline.\nThe\nDepartment of Design\nis centered on design research, creative technologies, and interdisciplinary collaboration. We seek faculty who use technology in adaptive, inclusive, poetic, collaborative, and experimental ways while advancing socially responsible design practices. The department offers four professional degrees and is organized around five discipline groups: Interior Design, Industrial Design, Interaction Design, Graphic Design, and Animation. Our faculty includes designers, architects, artists, and historians committed to excellence and collaboration across the department, college, university, Silicon Valley, and beyond. Prospective faculty will have opportunities to engage with the\nAdvanced Institute for Ethical Technologies (AI-ET)\n, which advances interdisciplinary and community-centered dialogue about responsible technology, and the\nDigital Humanities Center\n, a collaborative hub for digitally informed research, teaching, and public engagement.\nThe Department of Design and San José State University value institutional excellence and are committed to advancing equity through research, teaching, and service. We enroll more than 40,000 students, many of whom are historically underserved; approximately 45% of our undergraduate students are first-generation college students and 38% are Pell Grant recipients. In addition, we are both a Hispanic Serving Institution (HSI) and an Asian American and Native American Pacific Islander Serving Institution (AANAPISI). SJSU aspires to create holistic learning environments that facilitate both individual and collective transformation. We seek teacher-scholars who cultivate inclusive, empowering spaces for mutual learning, skill-building, and knowledge exchange for all students. Our commitment to lifelong learning supports meaningful connections among faculty, staff, students, alumni, and the broader communities we serve.\nWe invite all applicants to include a Statement of Institutional Excellence (or incorporate it into your cover letter) to share how your lived and professional experiences will contribute to the SJSU community—particularly in relation to student success. A guide to writing this statement can be found at\nSJSU Institutional Excellence Statement Guidelines\n.\nRequired Qualifications\nA terminal degree (MFA, MDes, PhD) in a graphic design discipline or closely and demonstrably related field. The degree must be completed before the targeted start date.\nEvidence of successful teaching at the undergraduate and/or graduate college level.\nAn active and sustained program of creative practice, graphic design research, scholarship, or a combination thereof.\nDemonstrated engagement with contemporary graphic design methods, tools, and modes of practice across digital and material media, including critical approaches to design making.\nApplicants should demonstrate an awareness of and sensitivity to the educational goals of a socially and economically diverse student population as might have been gained in cross-cultural study, training, teaching, and other comparable experience.\nPreferred Qualifications\nPriority will be given to candidates who possess two or more of the following:\nEvidence of professional visibility through exhibitions, publications, commissions, presentations, professional organizations, or other contributions to the discipline.\nA demonstrated ability to teach across the core curriculum at both undergraduate and graduate levels.\nDemonstrated engagement with evolving design practices that advance accessibility, equity, and broader cultural perspectives.\nA track record of success, or demonstrated potential for success, conducting research or creative activity relevant to the position that results in recognition (e.g., projects that have been published, exhibited, commissioned, implemented, recognized through awards, or otherwise disseminated within the discipline).\nPotential for collaboration across disciplines within the Department of Design and at the college and University levels. We believe such collaborations create a charged atmosphere for study and a critical edge for research, and we seek candidates who can thrive within this context. Applicants should consider how their work might connect with the college’s\nPublic Programming Opportunities\n.\nKey Responsibilities\nTeach undergraduate and graduate courses across the graphic design curriculum, including foundation studios, typography, visual systems, interaction, emerging technologies, and electives aligned with their expertise.\nDevelop and sustain an active program of research and/or creative practice appropriate for a tenure-track appointment.\nAdvise and mentor undergraduate and graduate students.\nContribute to curriculum development and ongoing assessment within the graphic design program.\nThe candidate will participate in shared governance, usually in department, college, and university committees and other service assignments.\nFaculty shall organize all their classes within the Canvas Learning Management System (LMS).\nThe candidate must demonstrate awareness and experience in understanding the needs of a student population of great diversity—in age, abilities, cultural background, ethnicity, religion, economic background, primary language, sexual orientation, gender identity, and academic preparation—through inclusive course materials, teaching strategies, and advisement.\nOther Duties\nNote that all San José State University employees are considered mandated reporters under the California Child Abuse and Neglect Reporting Act and are required to comply with the requirements set forth in CSU Executive Order 1083 as a condition of employment. Pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act) and CSU systemwide policy, some faculty duties may lead to designation as a Campus Security Authority (CSA). CSAs are required to complete Clery Act training and to immediately report Clery incidents to the institution.\nFaculty employees must complete CSU employee training as assigned and required based on their role (e.g., Data Security, FERPA, Preventing Discrimination and Harassment, Title IX, Health and Safety). The President may recommend or require compliance with safety measures that decrease the likelihood of COVID-19 transmission or illness and allows the core mission and activities of the campus to continue.\nApplication Procedure\nSelect\nApply Now\nto complete the SJSU Online Employment Application and attach the following documents:\nletter of interest\ncurriculum vitae\ninstitutional excellence statement\n(dedicated statement or addressed in letter of interest)\nportfolio of professional, scholarly, or creative work (PDF or link to website)\noptional: a portfolio of examples of your students’ design work (PDF or link to website)\nstatement of teaching interests/philosophy\nresearch and/or creative activity plans\nReferences: Three names with contact information are due at the time of application.  Note: Three full letters of reference are due at the first stage interview\nInquiries may be directed to the Department Chair or Search Committee Chair:\nChair Virginia San Fratello (\nvirginia.sanfratello@sjsu.edu\n)\nProfessor Connie Hwang (\nconnie.hwang@sjsu.edu\n)\nPursuant to California Education Code 89521, applicants will be required to disclose whether they have received a final administrative decision or final judicial decision determining that they have committed sexual harassment within the last 7 years only after a determination is made that they meet the minimum qualifications for the position, and before an offer of employment is extended.  Applicants who reach the final stages of the application process must also sign a release form that authorizes the release of information by the applicant’s current and/or former employers to the CSU concerning any substantiated allegations of misconduct.\nConditional Offer\nThe work for this faculty position may only be performed in the State of California and requires establishing an on-campus presence. Employment is contingent upon US residence and proof of eligibility to work in the United States. Satisfactory completion of background and reference checks (including criminal records and prior employment) are required. SJSU will make a conditional offer of employment, which may be rescinded if the background and reference checks reveal disqualifying information, and/or it is discovered that the candidate knowingly withheld or falsified information. Failure to satisfactorily complete the background and reference checks may affect the continued employment of a current employee who was conditionally offered the position.\nSan José State University: Silicon Valley's Public University\nLocated in the heart of Silicon Valley — one of the most innovative regions in the world —\nSan José State University\nis the founding campus of the 23-campus California State University (CSU) system and the first public university in the West. Recognized as a leading transformative educational institution, San José State is an essential partner in the technological, economic, cultural, and social development of Silicon Valley, the Bay Area, and California. SJSU is a top-200 school nationally in research funding and second highest in research productivity in the CSU system. Cutting-edge research, world-class scholarship, student-centered learning, diverse communities, and commitment to social justice, allow SJSU to provide life-changing opportunities and advance the public good locally and globally.\nEqual Employment Statement\nSan José State University prohibits discrimination on the basis of Age, Ancestry, Caste, Color, Disability, Ethnicity, Gender, Gender Expression, Gender Identity, Genetic Information, Marital Status, Medical Condition, Military Status, Nationality, Race, Religion, Religious Creed, Sex, Sexual Orientation, Sex Stereotype, and Veteran Status. This policy applies to all San José State University students, faculty, and staff as well as University programs and activities. Reasonable accommodations are made for applicants with disabilities who self-disclose.\nCampus Security and Fire Safety Notification\nPursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, the Annual Security Report (ASR) is also now available for viewing at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Security-Report.pdf\n. The ASR contains the current security and safety-related policy statements, emergency preparedness and evacuation information, crime prevention and Sexual Assault prevention information, and information about drug and alcohol prevention programming. The ASR also contains statistics of Clery crimes for San José State University locations for the three most recent calendar years. A paper copy of the ASR is available upon request by contacting the Office of the Clery Director by phone at 408-924-1501 or by email at clerycompliance@sjsu.edu.\nPursuant to the Higher Education Opportunity Act, the Annual Fire Safety Report (AFSR) is available at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Fire-Safety-Report.pdf\n. The purpose of this report is to disclose statistics for fires that occurred within SJSU on-campus housing facilities for the three most recent calendar years, and to distribute fire safety policies and procedures intended to promote safety on Campus. A paper copy of the AFSR is available upon request by contacting the Housing Office by phone at 408-795-5600 or by email at uhs-frontdesk@sjsu.edu."
    },
    "https://jobs.sjsu.edu/en-us/job/562272/audiology-department-chair": {
      "title": "Audiology Department Chair",
      "description": "Department: Audiology\nPosition Rank and Title:\nProfessor and Chair\nSchool/Department Name:\nAudiology\nCompensation:\nCommensurate with qualifications, experience, and rank as established by the\nCSU Salary Schedule\n. Anticipated hiring academic year annual salary range: $191,000 - $214,000 (Salary of 60% Department Chair and 40% AY faculty salary, with a base of $160,000 - $180,000)\nFaculty employee benefits are very competitive for the higher education sector.  See the\nCSU Employee Benefits Summary\nfor details about the CSU’s excellent benefits package.\nTarget Start Date:\nAugust 16, 2027\nApplication Deadline:\nNovember 30, 2026 (Applications accepted until filled or closed)\nPosition Description\nThe Department of Audiology at San José State University is pleased to announce a search for Department Chair beginning in August 2027. This position is a full-time tenured faculty appointment with a fractional administrative appointment as chair, responsible for the academic, administrative, and strategic direction of the program. The successful candidates will possess a substantive record of published research, teaching effectiveness, leadership experiences, and professional achievements, coupled with a demonstrated commitment to advancing health equity, interprofessional practice, and supporting diverse student populations.\nThe Chair provides visionary academic and administrative leadership for the Department of Audiology and ensures rigorous compliance with ACAE and CAA accreditation standards while fostering a collaborative, shared governance environment with the faculty to guide curriculum, clinical training, and programmatic decisions. The Chair also serves as a mentor and advocate for the department’s faculty, actively supporting their ongoing teaching effectiveness, research productivity, and professional advancement. Furthermore, the Chair provides essential administrative oversight and strategic support to the Audiology Clinic Director in clinic operations, ensuring the clinic’s operational success, community impact, and long-term financial sustainability. Teaching assignments will be in graduate audiology and will reflect the candidate’s expertise, departmental needs, and the workload assigned to the chair role. The term of the chair appointment is four years. Upon conclusion of service as Chair, the incumbent continues as a tenured member of the Audiology faculty with a full-time assignment in teaching, research, and service in graduate audiology.\nEstablished in response to the need for highly trained audiologists in California, particularly Northern California, the Department of Audiology offers a four-year, 110-unit Doctor of Audiology program with a comprehensive research and clinical training mission. Located in Silicon Valley, the program prepares students to serve the hearing and balance healthcare needs of the region’s diverse communities through comprehensive academic and clinical training. Students gain hands-on experience through the department’s on-campus audiology clinic, vestibular laboratory, clinical rotations, and externships, while also completing a multi-semester doctoral research project that integrates evidence-based practice and research with clinical education. Small cohorts of 8–12 students allow for close faculty mentorship and individualized professional development. As the only audiology program located in Silicon Valley, the program plays an important role in developing the next generation of clinical audiologists and strengthening the audiology workforce pipeline for Northern California and the broader state.\nThe Department of Audiology and San José State University value institutional excellence and are committed to advancing equity through research, teaching, and service. We enroll more than 40,000 students, many of whom are historically underserved; approximately 45% of our undergraduate students are first-generation college students and 38% are Pell Grant recipients. In addition, we are both a Hispanic Serving Institution (HSI) and an Asian American and Native American Pacific Islander Serving Institution (AANAPISI). SJSU aspires to create holistic learning environments that facilitate both individual and collective transformation. We seek teacher-scholars who cultivate inclusive, empowering spaces for mutual learning, skill-building, and knowledge exchange for all students. Our commitment to lifelong learning supports meaningful connections among faculty, staff, students, alumni, and the broader communities we serve.\nWe invite all applicants to include a Statement of Institutional Excellence (or incorporate it into your cover letter) to share how your lived and professional experiences will contribute to the SJSU community—particularly in relation to student success. A guide to writing this statement can be found at\nSJSU Institutional Excellence Statement Guidelines\n.\nRequired Qualifications\nPh.D. in a field related to hearing and speech sciences, balance, or communication disorders.\nDemonstrate effectiveness in teaching, service, leadership, research, scholarly and creative activities for appointment as a tenured associate professor or full professor.\nPrior experience in university academic administrative positions.\nExperience with project management, strategic planning, and budgeting.\nApplicants should demonstrate an awareness of and sensitivity to the educational goals of a socially and economically diverse student population, including diversity in age, abilities, cultural background, ethnicity, religion, economic background, primary language, sexual orientation, gender identity, and academic preparation, as might have been gained in cross-cultural study, training, teaching, advising, and other comparable experiences.\nPreferred Qualifications\nPriority will be given to candidates who possess one or more of the following:\nA clinical degree in Audiology\nExperience with accreditation processes and clinical compliance requirements\nExperience with research and externally funded programs, including NIH or other national funding agencies.\nExperience with developing and maintaining interdisciplinary partnerships\nTwo or more years of clinical experience\nKnowledge of clinical operations, HIPAA compliance, quality assurance, and clinical documentation systems\nEligibility for California State Audiology License\nKey Responsibilities\nAcademic and Departmental Leadership\n:\nProvides visionary academic and administrative leadership for the Department of Audiology, including strategic planning, faculty and staff supervision, hiring, performance evaluation, and professional development. Manages departmental resources, including the allocated budget, travel, facilities, and space, and presides over faculty meetings.\nAccreditation, Assessment, and Program Development\n:\nLeads and administers the Doctor of Audiology program consistent with ACAE and CAA standards for program leadership, ensuring the department maintains accreditation and overseeing program assessment and improvement efforts at the University level. Shares responsibility for curriculum, governance, and program decisions with the department’s faculty, and provides administrative oversight for the retention, tenure, and promotion of AUD faculty. In collaboration with key stakeholders and the Dean of the College of Health and Human Sciences, leads strategic planning to meet student, staff, faculty, and community needs, including administrative oversight and support for the Program Director, Audiology Clinic Director, Director of Clinical Education in clinic operations, strategic growth, and the long-term sustainability of the SJSU Audiology Clinic.\nTeaching\n:\nTeaching assignments will be in graduate audiology and will reflect the candidate’s expertise, departmental needs, and the workload assigned to the chair role.\nResearch and Faculty Development\n:\nSupports an evidence-based research agenda for the Department of Audiology and facilitates professional development activities and mentorship for faculty.\nCommunity and Professional Engagement\n:\nAdvocates for the Department of Audiology mission with university administration and to professional and academic groups at local, regional, state, and national levels. Supports the Directors to develop and maintain collaborative partnerships with clinical sites and community organizations and maintains active involvement in professional organizations governing audiology.\nStudent and Stakeholder Support\n:\nServes as an advocate for students, staff, and faculty, and effectively responds to their concerns and those of external stakeholders. Engages in activities that support diversity, equity, and inclusion on campus, in local and global communities, and the profession. University requirements also include participating in shared governance, usually in department, college, and university committees and other service assignments.\nDemonstrate awareness and experience understanding the needs of a student population of great diversity – in age, abilities, cultural background, ethnicity, religion, economic background, primary language, sexual orientation, gender identity, and academic preparation – through inclusive course materials, teaching strategies and advisement.\nOther Duties\nNote that all San José State University employees are considered mandated reporters under the California Child Abuse and Neglect Reporting Act and are required to comply with the requirements set forth in CSU Executive Order 1083 as a condition of employment. Pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act) and CSU systemwide policy, some faculty duties may lead to designation as a Campus Security Authority (CSA). CSAs are required to complete Clery Act training and to immediately report Clery incidents to the institution.\nFaculty employees must complete CSU employee training as assigned and required based on their role (e.g., Data Security, FERPA, Preventing Discrimination and Harassment, Title IX, Health and Safety). The President may recommend or require compliance with safety measures that decrease the likelihood of COVID-19 transmission or illness and allows the core mission and activities of the campus to continue.\nApplication Procedure\nClick Apply Now to complete the SJSU Online Employment Application and attach the following documents.\nLetter of application addressing (a) how you would apply your leadership experience and management philosophy to the Department of Audiology and its community and (b) your\nInstitutional Excellence Statement\n(2-3 pages)\nCurriculum vitae\nPlan for research and/or creative activity (1-2 pages)\nNames and contact information for three references. Letters will be requested of semifinalists.\nInquiries may be directed to the Search Committee Chair: Susan Ross, susan.ross@sjsu.edu\nPursuant to California Education Code 89521, applicants will be required to disclose whether they have received a final administrative decision or final judicial decision determining that they have committed sexual harassment within the last 7 years only after a determination is made that they meet the minimum qualifications for the position, and before an offer of employment is extended.  Applicants who reach the final stages of the application process must also sign a release form that authorizes the release of information by the applicant’s current and/or former employers to the CSU concerning any substantiated allegations of misconduct.\nConditional Offer\nThe work for this faculty position may only be performed in the State of California and requires establishing an on-campus presence. Employment is contingent upon US residence and proof of eligibility to work in the United States. Satisfactory completion of background and reference checks (including criminal records and prior employment) are required. SJSU will make a conditional offer of employment, which may be rescinded if the background and reference checks reveal disqualifying information, and/or it is discovered that the candidate knowingly withheld or falsified information. Failure to satisfactorily complete the background and reference checks may affect the continued employment of a current employee who was conditionally offered the position.\nSan José State University: Silicon Valley's Public University\nLocated in the heart of Silicon Valley — one of the most innovative regions in the world —\nSan José State University\nis the founding campus of the 23-campus California State University (CSU) system and the first public university in the West. Recognized as a leading transformative educational institution, San José State is an essential partner in the technological, economic, cultural, and social development of Silicon Valley, the Bay Area, and California. SJSU is a top-200 school nationally in research funding and second highest in research productivity in the CSU system. Cutting-edge research, world-class scholarship, student-centered learning, diverse communities, and commitment to social justice, allow SJSU to provide life-changing opportunities and advance the public good locally and globally.\nEqual Employment Statement\nSan José State University prohibits discrimination on the basis of Age, Ancestry, Caste, Color, Disability, Ethnicity, Gender, Gender Expression, Gender Identity, Genetic Information, Marital Status, Medical Condition, Military Status, Nationality, Race, Religion, Religious Creed, Sex, Sexual Orientation, Sex Stereotype, and Veteran Status. This policy applies to all San José State University students, faculty, and staff as well as University programs and activities. Reasonable accommodations are made for applicants with disabilities who self-disclose.\nCampus Security and Fire Safety Notification\nPursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, the Annual Security Report (ASR) is also now available for viewing at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Security-Report.pdf\n. The ASR contains the current security and safety-related policy statements, emergency preparedness and evacuation information, crime prevention and Sexual Assault prevention information, and information about drug and alcohol prevention programming. The ASR also contains statistics of Clery crimes for San José State University locations for the three most recent calendar years. A paper copy of the ASR is available upon request by contacting the Office of the Clery Director by phone at 408-924-1501 or by email at clerycompliance@sjsu.edu.\nPursuant to the Higher Education Opportunity Act, the Annual Fire Safety Report (AFSR) is available at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Fire-Safety-Report.pdf\n. The purpose of this report is to disclose statistics for fires that occurred within SJSU on-campus housing facilities for the three most recent calendar years, and to distribute fire safety policies and procedures intended to promote safety on Campus. A paper copy of the AFSR is available upon request by contacting the Housing Office by phone at 408-795-5600 or by email at uhs-frontdesk@sjsu.edu."
    },
    "https://jobs.sjsu.edu/en-us/job/562389/tenure-track-assistant-professor-in-computer-science-bioinformatics": {
      "title": "Tenure Track Assistant Professor in Computer Science - Bioinformatics",
      "description": "Department: Department of Computer Science\nPosition Rank and Title:\nAssistant Professor\nSchool/Department Name:\nDepartment of Computer Science\nCompensation:\nCommensurate with qualifications, experience, and rank as established by the\nCSU Salary Schedule\n. Anticipated hiring academic year annual salary range: $105,000 - $115,000. ]\nFaculty employee benefits are very competitive for the higher education sector.  See the\nCSU Employee Benefits Summary\nfor details about the CSU’s excellent benefits package.\nTarget Start Date:\nAugust 2027\nApplication Deadline:\nDecember 11, 2026\n(Applications accepted until filled or closed)\nPosition Description\nThe Department of Computer Science at San José State University is pleased to announce a search for a tenure-track Assistant Professor in Computer Science with a specialization in bioinformatics or a related field, beginning in August 2027. Related areas include computational biology, genomics and other omics data analysis, biological sequence and structure analysis, biomedical data science, and machine learning and AI methods applied to biological and health data. The position will be required to teach a variety of required and elective courses in Computer Science and Data Science, as well as courses in the MS in Bioinformatics program.\nThe Department of Computer Science offers BS and MS degrees in Computer Science and a BS in Data Science. We also offer a BS in Computer Science and Linguistics (jointly with the Department of Linguistics and Language Development), an MS in Data Science (jointly with the Department of Mathematics and Statistics), and an MS in Bioinformatics, and we support the BS in Software Engineering (curriculum jointly administered with the Department of Computer Engineering). The faculty members are excellent teacher-scholars with rich publication records in the areas of machine learning, data science, information security, mobile networking, social networks, bioinformatics, and other fields. The Department, including both faculty and students, enjoys rich interaction and collaboration with Silicon Valley. More information about the Department is available at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/cs/\n.\nThe Department of Computer Science and San José State University value institutional excellence and are committed to advancing equity through research, teaching, and service. We enroll more than 40,000 students, many of whom are historically underserved; approximately 45% of our undergraduate students are first-generation college students and 38% are Pell Grant recipients. In addition, we are both a Hispanic Serving Institution (HSI) and an Asian American and Native American Pacific Islander Serving Institution (AANAPISI). SJSU aspires to create holistic learning environments that facilitate both individual and collective transformation. We seek teacher-scholars who cultivate inclusive, empowering spaces for mutual learning, skill-building, and knowledge exchange for all students. Our commitment to lifelong learning supports meaningful connections among faculty, staff, students, alumni, and the broader communities we serve.\nWe invite all applicants to include a Statement of Institutional Excellence (or incorporate it into your cover letter) to share how your lived and professional experiences will contribute to the SJSU community—particularly in relation to student success. A guide to writing this statement can be found at\nSJSU Institutional Excellence Statement Guidelines\n.\nRequired Qualifications\nPh.D. in Computer Science or a closely related field.\nDemonstrated excellence in teaching and scholarship.\nApplicants should demonstrate an awareness of and sensitivity to the educational goals of a socially and economically diverse student population as might have been gained in cross-cultural study, training, teaching, and other comparable experience.\nPreferred Qualifications\nPriority will be given to candidates who possess one or more of the following:\nTeaching, research, and/or industry experience in bioinformatics or a related field, such as computational biology, genomics, biomedical data science, or machine learning and AI applied to biological data.\nKey Responsibilities\nThe Department’s faculty members are expected to teach in-person, supervise, and advise students in both the undergraduate and graduate programs, and to establish a successful research program related to their field of interest.\nThe candidate will participate in shared governance, usually in department, college, and university committees and other service assignments.\nFaculty shall organize all their classes within the Canvas Learning Management System (LMS).\nThe candidate must demonstrate awareness and experience in understanding the needs of a student population of great diversity – in age, abilities, cultural background, ethnicity, religion, economic background, primary language, sexual orientation, gender identity, and academic preparation – through inclusive course materials, teaching strategies, and advisement.\nOther Duties\nNote that all San José State University employees are considered mandated reporters under the California Child Abuse and Neglect Reporting Act and are required to comply with the requirements set forth in CSU Executive Order 1083 as a condition of employment. Pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act) and CSU systemwide policy, some faculty duties may lead to designation as a Campus Security Authority (CSA). CSAs are required to complete Clery Act training and to immediately report Clery incidents to the institution.\nFaculty employees must complete CSU employee training as assigned and required based on their role (e.g., Data Security, FERPA, Preventing Discrimination and Harassment, Title IX, Health and Safety). The President may recommend or require compliance with safety measures that decrease the likelihood of COVID-19 transmission or illness and allows the core mission and activities of the campus to continue.\nApplication Procedure\nClick Apply Now to complete the SJSU Online Employment Application and attach the following documents:\nletter of interest\ncurriculum vitae\ninstitutional excellence statement\n(dedicated statement or addressed in letter of interest)\nstatement of teaching interests/philosophy\nresearch plans\nthree references with contact information or three references we may contact for letters\nInquiries may be directed to Chris Pollett, Professor and Department Chair, chris.pollett@sjsu.edu (Subject: CS Recruitment).\nPursuant to California Education Code 89521, applicants will be required to disclose whether they have received a final administrative decision or final judicial decision determining that they have committed sexual harassment within the last 7 years only after a determination is made that they meet the minimum qualifications for the position, and before an offer of employment is extended.  Applicants who reach the final stages of the application process must also sign a release form that authorizes the release of information by the applicant’s current and/or former employers to the CSU concerning any substantiated allegations of misconduct.\nConditional Offer\nThe work for this faculty position may only be performed in the State of California and requires establishing an on-campus presence.  Employment is contingent upon US residence and proof of eligibility to work in the United States. Satisfactory completion of background and reference checks (including criminal records and prior employment) are required. SJSU will make a conditional offer of employment, which may be rescinded if the background and reference checks reveal disqualifying information, and/or it is discovered that the candidate knowingly withheld or falsified information. Failure to satisfactorily complete the background and reference checks may affect the continued employment of a current employee who was conditionally offered the position.\nSan José State University: Silicon Valley's Public University\nLocated in the heart of Silicon Valley — one of the most innovative regions in the world —\nSan José State University\nis the founding campus of the 23-campus California State University (CSU) system and the first public university in the West. Recognized as a leading transformative educational institution, San José State is an essential partner in the technological, economic, cultural, and social development of Silicon Valley, the Bay Area, and California. SJSU is a top-200 school nationally in research funding and second highest in research productivity in the CSU system. Cutting-edge research, world-class scholarship, student-centered learning, diverse communities, and commitment to social justice, allow SJSU to provide life-changing opportunities and advance the public good locally and globally.\nEqual Employment Statement\nSan José State University prohibits discrimination on the basis of Age, Ancestry, Caste, Color, Disability, Ethnicity, Gender, Gender Expression, Gender Identity, Genetic Information, Marital Status, Medical Condition, Military Status, Nationality, Race, Religion, Religious Creed, Sex, Sexual Orientation, Sex Stereotype, and Veteran Status. This policy applies to all San José State University students, faculty, and staff as well as University programs and activities. Reasonable accommodations are made for applicants with disabilities who self-disclose.\nCampus Security and Fire Safety Notification\nPursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, the Annual Security Report (ASR) is also now available for viewing at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Security-Report.pdf\n. The ASR contains the current security and safety-related policy statements, emergency preparedness and evacuation information, crime prevention and Sexual Assault prevention information, and information about drug and alcohol prevention programming. The ASR also contains statistics of Clery crimes for San José State University locations for the three most recent calendar years. A paper copy of the ASR is available upon request by contacting the Office of the Clery Director by phone at 408-924-1501 or by email at clerycompliance@sjsu.edu.\nPursuant to the Higher Education Opportunity Act, the Annual Fire Safety Report (AFSR) is available at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Fire-Safety-Report.pdf\n. The purpose of this report is to disclose statistics for fires that occurred within SJSU on-campus housing facilities for the three most recent calendar years, and to distribute fire safety policies and procedures intended to promote safety on Campus. A paper copy of the AFSR is available upon request by contacting the Housing Office by phone at 408-795-5600 or by email at uhs-frontdesk@sjsu.edu."
    },
    "https://jobs.sjsu.edu/en-us/job/562269/assistant-professor-of-mathematics-statistics-or-data-science": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Mathematics - Statistics or Data Science",
      "description": "Department: Department of Mathematics and Statistics\nPosition Rank and Title:\nOpen Rank\nSchool/Department Name:\nDepartment of Mathematics and Statistics\nSpecialization:\nStatistics or Data Science\nCompensation:\nCommensurate with qualiﬁcations, experience, and rank as established by the\nCSU Salary\nSchedule\n. Anticipated hiring academic year annual salary range: $104,500 - $135,000.\nFaculty employee beneﬁts are very competitive for the higher education sector. See the\nCSU Employee\nBeneﬁts Summary\nfor details about the CSU’s excellent beneﬁts package.\nTarget Start Date:\nAugust 17, 2027\nApplication Deadline:\nOctober 16, 2026 (Applications accepted until ﬁlled or closed)\nPosition Description\nThe Department of Mathematics and Statistics at San José State University is pleased to announce a search for a full-time, tenure-track/tenured faculty position at the rank of Assistant, Associate or Full Professor beginning in August 2027. We are seeking applicants from Statistics, Data Science, or closely related ﬁelds with a strong record of scholarly achievement, eﬀective teaching, and demonstrated interest in departmental governance. The successful candidate would be teaching courses and supervising students in statistics, data science, or mathematics at both the undergraduate and graduate levels, have an active research program that ideally includes students, and participate in departmental service activities. Our department has multiple growing undergraduate and graduate programs in statistics and data science; the successful candidate will help shape the future of these programs through curricular innovation.\nThe Department of Mathematics and Statistics is home to about 500 undergraduates and graduate students, 28 tenure ladder faculty, 25 part-time instructors and 40 teaching associates. We strive to prepare our students in statistics and data science through an array of courses and projects for cutting edge careers in Silicon Valley. CAMCOS is a program within the department that allows students to work on industry sponsored projects under supervision from faculty and industry mentors. San José State University has recently been awarded R2 status. The university oﬀers generous support for research active faculty and faculty who would like to write proposals to support their research agenda. More information about the department and the programs we oﬀer can be found on our website\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/math/.\nThe Department of Mathematics and Statistics and San José State University value institutional excellence and are committed to advancing equity through research, teaching, and service. We enroll more than 40,000 students, many of whom are historically underserved; approximately 45% of our undergraduate students are ﬁrst-generation college students and 38% are Pell Grant recipients. In addition, we are both a Hispanic Serving Institution (HSI) and an Asian American and Native American Paciﬁc Islander Serving Institution (AANAPISI). SJSU aspires to create holistic learning environments that facilitate both individual and collective transformation. We seek teacher-scholars who cultivate inclusive, empowering spaces for mutual learning, skill-building, and knowledge exchange for all students. Our commitment to lifelong learning supports meaningful connections among faculty, staﬀ, students, alumni, and the broader communities we serve.\nWe invite all applicants to include a Statement of Institutional Excellence (or incorporate it into your cover letter) to share how your lived and professional experiences will contribute to the SJSU\ncommunity—particularly in relation to student success. A guide to writing this statement can be found at\nSJSU Institutional Excellence Statement Guidelines\n.\nRequired Qualiﬁcations\nDoctoral degree in Statistics, Data Science or a closely related ﬁeld completed by July 2027.\nApplicants should demonstrate an awareness of and sensitivity to the educational goals of a socially and economically diverse student population as might have been gained in cross-cultural study, training, teaching, and other comparable experience.\nA record of ongoing research and a well-developed agenda for future research.\nExperience or demonstrated potential for eﬀective teaching at the undergraduate and graduate level.\nPreferred Qualiﬁcations\nPriority will be given to candidates who possess one or more of the following:\nEvidence of eﬀective service to their department, university, or profession commensurate with their experience.\nExperience or interest in mentoring undergraduate and/or graduate student research.\nKey Responsibilities\nThe candidate is expected to teach a variety of undergraduate and graduate statistics, data science, or mathematics courses in the Department of Mathematics and Statistics, to maintain a program of scholarly activity, and to work on curriculum and program development.\nThe candidate is expected to supervise graduate students on their research projects in both the department’s MS Statistics program and the College of Science’s MS Data Science program.\nThe candidate is also expected to supervise student teams working on industrial research projects in the department’s Center for Applied Math, Computation, and Statistics (CAMCOS) program.\nThe candidate will participate in shared governance, usually in department, college, and university committees and other service assignments.\nThe candidate is expected to maintain a focused and impactful research agenda with peer-reviewed publications in reputable venues.\nFaculty shall organize all their classes within the Canvas Learning Management System (LMS).\nThe candidate must demonstrate awareness and experience in understanding the needs of a student population of great diversity – in age, abilities, cultural background, ethnicity, religion, economic background, primary language, sexual orientation, gender identity, and academic preparation – through inclusive course materials, teaching strategies, and advisement.\nOther Duties\nNote that all San José State University employees are considered mandated reporters under the California Child Abuse and Neglect Reporting Act and are required to comply with the requirements set forth in CSU Executive Order 1083 as a condition of employment. Pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act) and CSU systemwide policy, some faculty duties may lead to designation as a Campus Security Authority (CSA). CSAs are required to complete Clery Act training and to immediately report Clery incidents to the institution.\nFaculty employees must complete CSU employee training as assigned and required based on their role (e.g., Data Security, FERPA, Preventing Discrimination and Harassment, Title IX, Health and Safety). The President may recommend or require compliance with safety measures that decrease the likelihood of COVID-19 transmission or illness and allows the core mission and activities of the campus to continue.\nApplication Procedure\nClick Apply Now to complete the SJSU Online Employment Application and attach the following documents:\nA letter of interest\nCurriculum vitae\nInstitutional excellence statement\n(dedicated statement or addressed in letter of interest)\nTeaching Statement. Describe your teaching experience, interests and philosophy. Please describe what role faculty play in student success. (1-2 pages)\nService Statement. Include evidence and philosophy of service to the department, university, and profession. (1-2 pages)\nResearch Statement. Describe your research program, emphasizing your future plans. Describe how you plan to include students in your research activities. (1-2 pages)\nThree to ﬁve references with contact information. We will be contacting your references to request letters of recommendation. At least one of the letters should address teaching performance.\nAll student and peer teaching evaluations for classes taught during the last three years.\nInquiries may be directed to the Department Chair or Search Committee Chair: Dr. Martina Bremer\n(martina.bremer@sjsu.edu).\nPursuant to California Education Code 89521, applicants will be required to disclose whether they have received a ﬁnal administrative decision or ﬁnal judicial decision determining that they have committed any violation of the policies governing employee conduct including, but not limited to sexual harassment, sexual assault, or other forms of harassment or discrimination, as deﬁned by the employer within the last 7 years only after a determination is made that they meet the minimum qualiﬁcations for the position, and before an oﬀer of employment is extended. Applicants who reach the ﬁnal stages of the application process must also sign a release form that authorizes the release of information by the applicant’s current and/or former employers to the CSU concerning any substantiated allegations of misconduct.\nConditional Oﬀer\nThe work for this faculty position may only be performed in the State of California and requires establishing an on-campus presence. Employment is contingent upon US residence and proof of eligibility to work in the United States. Satisfactory completion of background and reference checks (including criminal records and prior employment) are required. SJSU will make a conditional oﬀer of employment, which may be rescinded if the background and reference checks reveal disqualifying information, and/or it is discovered that the candidate knowingly withheld or falsiﬁed information. Failure to satisfactorily complete the background and reference checks may aﬀect the continued employment of a current employee who was conditionally oﬀered the position.\nSan José State University: Silicon Valley's Public University\nLocated in the heart of Silicon Valley — one of the most innovative regions in the world —\nSan José State\nUniversity\nis the founding campus of the 23-campus California State University (CSU) system and the ﬁrst public university in the West. Recognized as a leading transformative educational institution, San José State is an essential partner in the technological, economic, cultural, and social development of Silicon Valley, the Bay Area, and California. SJSU is a top-200 school nationally in research funding and second highest in\nresearch productivity in the CSU system. Cutting-edge research, world-class scholarship, student-centered learning, diverse communities, and commitment to social justice, allow SJSU to provide life-changing opportunities and advance the public good locally and globally.\nEqual Employment Statement\nSan José State University prohibits discrimination on the basis of Age, Ancestry, Caste, Color, Disability, Ethnicity, Gender, Gender Expression, Gender Identity, Genetic Information, Marital Status, Medical Condition, Military Status, Nationality, Race, Religion, Religious Creed, Sex, Sexual Orientation, Sex Stereotype, and Veteran Status. This policy applies to all San José State University students, faculty, and staﬀ as well as University programs and activities. Reasonable accommodations are made for applicants with disabilities who self-disclose.\nCampus Security and Fire Safety Notiﬁcation\nPursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, the Annual Security Report (ASR) is also now available for viewing at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Security-Report.pdf\n. The ASR contains the current security and safety-related policy statements, emergency preparedness and evacuation information, crime prevention and Sexual Assault prevention information, and information about drug and alcohol prevention programming. The ASR also contains statistics of Clery crimes for San José State University locations for the three most recent calendar years. A paper copy of the ASR is available upon request by contacting the Oﬃce of the Clery Director by phone at 408-924-1501 or by email at\nclerycompliance@sjsu.edu.\nPursuant to the Higher Education Opportunity Act, the Annual Fire Safety Report (AFSR) is available at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Fire-Safety-Report.pdf\n. The purpose of this report is to disclose statistics for ﬁres that occurred within SJSU on-campus housing facilities for the three most recent calendar years, and to distribute ﬁre safety policies and procedures intended to promote safety on Campus. A paper copy of the AFSR is available upon request by contacting the Housing Oﬃce by phone at 408-795-5600 or by email at\nuhs-frontdesk@sjsu.edu."
    },
    "https://jobs.sjsu.edu/en-us/job/562276/assistant-professor-of-meteorology-climate-science": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Meteorology & Climate Science",
      "description": "Department: Department of Meteorology and Climate Science\nPosition Rank and Title:\nAssistant Professor – Synoptic Meteorology & AI Forecasting\nSchool/Department Name:\nDepartment of Meteorology and Climate Science\nCompensation:\nCommensurate with qualifications, experience, and rank as established by the\nCSU Salary Schedule\n. Anticipated hiring academic year annual salary range: $ 105,000  -  $ 115,000.\nFaculty employee benefits are very competitive for the higher education sector.  See the\nCSU Employee Benefits Summary\nfor details about the CSU’s excellent benefits package.\nTarget Start Date:\nAugust 1st, 2027\nApplication Deadline:\nJanuary 15, 2027\n(Applications accepted until filled or closed)\nPosition Description\nThe Department of Meteorology and Climate Science at San José State University is pleased to announce a search for an assistant professor (tenure-track) with expertise in synoptic meteorology and forecasting beginning in August 2027. We seek candidates with a strong background in synoptic dynamics and forecasting with expertise in AI forecasting platforms. Specific areas of research interest include, but are not limited to: large-scale dynamics, forecasting with AI, mesoscale and severe weather, and wildfire meteorology and fire weather. We seek candidates with teaching interests in synoptic and dynamic meteorology, forecasting, forecasting lab, and/or mesoscale meteorology. The department maintains and active participation in the national forecasting contest as part of its synoptic and forecasting curriculum.\nThe candidate will have the opportunity to participate as a member of the SJSU Wildfire Interdisciplinary Research Center (\nWIRC,\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/wildfire/\n, a National Science Foundation, Industry-University Cooperative Research Center) to seek internal funding and conduct research related to wildfire and fire weather.\nLocated in the heart of Silicon Valley, SJSU Meteorology and Climate Science provides an exceptional setting for teaching, research, and collaboration. The department has strong connections to Silicon Valley's technology companies and California’s utilities that create opportunities for faculty to develop industry partnerships and pursue applied research. The department of Meteorology and Climate Science has extensive research facilities, including a HPC, a number of other computing systems, two mobile Doppler radars, seven Doppler lidars, a network of surface weather stations, and many other observational instruments and platforms.\nThe Department of Meteorology and Climate Science and San José State University value institutional excellence and are committed to advancing equity through research, teaching, and service. We enroll more than 40,000 students, many of whom are historically underserved; approximately 45% of our undergraduate students are first-generation college students and 38% are Pell Grant recipients. In addition, we are both a Hispanic Serving Institution (HSI) and an Asian American and Native American Pacific Islander Serving Institution (AANAPISI). SJSU aspires to create holistic learning environments that facilitate both individual and collective transformation. We seek teacher-scholars who cultivate inclusive, empowering spaces for mutual learning, skill-building, and knowledge exchange for all students. Our commitment to lifelong learning supports meaningful connections among faculty, staff, students, alumni, and the broader communities we serve.\nWe invite all applicants to include a Statement of Institutional Excellence (or incorporate it into your cover letter) to share how your lived and professional experiences will contribute to the SJSU community—particularly in relation to student success. A guide to writing this statement can be found at\nSJSU Institutional Excellence Statement Guidelines\n.\nRequired Qualifications\nPhD in Meteorology, Atmospheric Science, or closely related field by the time of appointment.\nAbility to teach & develop courses in synoptic-dynamics, forecasting, and mesoscale and/or in candidate’s area of expertise at both undergraduate and graduate levels.\nDemonstrated research experience directly relevant to candidate’s expertise and specialization.\nApplicants should demonstrate an awareness of and sensitivity to the educational goals of a socially and economically diverse student population as might have been gained in cross-cultural study, training, teaching, and other comparable experience.\nPreferred Qualifications\nPriority will be given to candidates who possess one or more of the following:\nStrong track record of publication.\nExperience and/or demonstrated interest in teaching and pedagogical methods.\nExperience/exposure to the funding acquisition process.\nKey Responsibilities\nThe candidate will teach graduate and undergraduate courses in synoptic meteorology, forecasting, and any other areas consistent with their professional interests. The instructional responsibilities also include preparation of classes, assessment of course learning outcomes, and development of pedagogical improvements.\nThe candidate will conduct research in their area of expertise, publish results in reputable peer-reviewed journals, generate grants, and supervise student research.\nThe candidate will participate in shared governance, usually in department, college, and university committees and other service assignments.\nFaculty shall organize all their classes within the Canvas Learning Management System (LMS).\nThe candidate must demonstrate awareness and experience in understanding the needs of a student population of great diversity – in age, abilities, cultural background, ethnicity, religion, economic background, primary language, sexual orientation, gender identity, and academic preparation – through inclusive course materials, teaching strategies, and advisement.\nOther Duties\nNote that all San José State University employees are considered mandated reporters under the California Child Abuse and Neglect Reporting Act and are required to comply with the requirements set forth in CSU Executive Order 1083 as a condition of employment. Pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act) and CSU systemwide policy, some faculty duties may lead to designation as a Campus Security Authority (CSA). CSAs are required to complete Clery Act training and to immediately report Clery incidents to the institution.\nFaculty employees must complete CSU employee training as assigned and required based on their role (e.g., Data Security, FERPA, Preventing Discrimination and Harassment, Title IX, Health and Safety). The President may recommend or require compliance with safety measures that decrease the likelihood of COVID-19 transmission or illness and allows the core mission and activities of the campus to continue.\nApplication Procedure\nClick Apply Now to complete the SJSU Online Employment Application and attach the following documents:\nletter of interest (2-page max)\ncurriculum vitae\ninstitutional excellence statement\n(dedicated statement or addressed in letter of interest, 2-page max)\nstatement of teaching interests/philosophy (2 page max)\nresearch plans (2-page max)\nMinimum of three references with contact information\nInquiries may be directed to the Department Chair or Search Committee Chair: Prof. Craig Clements, craig.clements@sjsu.edu\nPursuant to California Education Code 89521, applicants will be required to disclose whether they have received a final administrative decision or final judicial decision determining that they have committed sexual harassment within the last 7 years only after a determination is made that they meet the minimum qualifications for the position, and before an offer of employment is extended.  Applicants who reach the final stages of the application process must also sign a release form that authorizes the release of information by the applicant’s current and/or former employers to the CSU concerning any substantiated allegations of misconduct.\nConditional Offer\nThe work for this faculty position may only be performed in the State of California and requires establishing an on-campus presence. Employment is contingent upon US residence and proof of eligibility to work in the United States. Satisfactory completion of background and reference checks (including criminal records and prior employment) are required. SJSU will make a conditional offer of employment, which may be rescinded if the background and reference checks reveal disqualifying information, and/or it is discovered that the candidate knowingly withheld or falsified information. Failure to satisfactorily complete the background and reference checks may affect the continued employment of a current employee who was conditionally offered the position.\nSan José State University: Silicon Valley's Public University\nLocated in the heart of Silicon Valley — one of the most innovative regions in the world —\nSan José State University\nis the founding campus of the 23-campus California State University (CSU) system and the first public university in the West. Recognized as a leading transformative educational institution, San José State is an essential partner in the technological, economic, cultural, and social development of Silicon Valley, the Bay Area, and California. SJSU is a top-200 school nationally in research funding and second highest in research productivity in the CSU system. Cutting-edge research, world-class scholarship, student-centered learning, diverse communities, and commitment to social justice, allow SJSU to provide life-changing opportunities and advance the public good locally and globally.\nEqual Employment Statement\nSan José State University prohibits discrimination on the basis of Age, Ancestry, Caste, Color, Disability, Ethnicity, Gender, Gender Expression, Gender Identity, Genetic Information, Marital Status, Medical Condition, Military Status, Nationality, Race, Religion, Religious Creed, Sex, Sexual Orientation, Sex Stereotype, and Veteran Status. This policy applies to all San José State University students, faculty, and staff as well as University programs and activities. Reasonable accommodations are made for applicants with disabilities who self-disclose.\nCampus Security and Fire Safety Notification\nPursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, the Annual Security Report (ASR) is also now available for viewing at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Security-Report.pdf\n. The ASR contains the current security and safety-related policy statements, emergency preparedness and evacuation information, crime prevention and Sexual Assault prevention information, and information about drug and alcohol prevention programming. The ASR also contains statistics of Clery crimes for San José State University locations for the three most recent calendar years. A paper copy of the ASR is available upon request by contacting the Office of the Clery Director by phone at 408-924-1501 or by email at clerycompliance@sjsu.edu.\nPursuant to the Higher Education Opportunity Act, the Annual Fire Safety Report (AFSR) is available at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Fire-Safety-Report.pdf\n. The purpose of this report is to disclose statistics for fires that occurred within SJSU on-campus housing facilities for the three most recent calendar years, and to distribute fire safety policies and procedures intended to promote safety on Campus. A paper copy of the AFSR is available upon request by contacting the Housing Office by phone at 408-795-5600 or by email at uhs-frontdesk@sjsu.edu."
    },
    "https://jobs.sjsu.edu/en-us/job/562278/assistant-professor-interior-design": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Interior Design",
      "description": "Department: Department of Design\nJob Number:\n562278\nPosition Rank and Title:\nAssistant Professor, Interior Design\nSchool/Department Name:\nDepartment of Design\nCompensation:\nCommensurate with qualifications, experience, and rank as established by the\nCSU Salary Schedule\n. Anticipated hiring academic year annual salary range: $90,000-$94,000, depending on qualifications.\nFaculty employee benefits are very competitive for the higher education sector.  See the\nCSU Employee Benefits Summary\nfor details about the CSU’s excellent benefits package.\nTarget Start Date:\nAugust 16, 2027 (Fall 2027)\nApplication Deadline:\nJanuary 8, 2027 (Applications accepted until filled or closed)\nPosition Description\nThe\nDepartment of Design\nat San José State University invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position in Interior Design, beginning in Fall 2027. This is an academic-year appointment. We seek a designer, educator, and researcher whose work bridges professional practice and academic inquiry, and who can teach in the undergraduate interior design curriculum, including foundation studios, space planning, materials and construction, digital and computational tools, and advanced electives aligned with the candidate's expertise and research. San Jose State University is committed to excellence and enhancing student experience through engaged teaching and dedicated advising by all faculty. The ideal candidate brings professional practice experience in interior design alongside academic teaching experience, and demonstrates fluency with the emerging technologies shaping the discipline.\nWe are particularly interested in candidates whose research or creative practice advances interior design through emerging technologies, whether through digital fabrication, computational and parametric design, responsive or smart materials, immersive visualization, AI, or related areas of inquiry. The ideal candidate demonstrates rigor in their scholarly and creative research, and will contribute new knowledge through creative practice, design research, scholarship, or public engagement. The department recognizes multiple forms of scholarly and creative activity, including peer-reviewed publications, exhibitions, built work, funded research, public scholarship, and other contributions that advance the discipline.\nThe Department of Design is centered on design research, creative and emerging technologies, and interdisciplinary collaboration. We seek faculty who use technology in adaptive, inclusive, poetic, collaborative, agentic, and experimental ways while advancing socially responsible design practices. The department offers four professional degrees and is organized around five discipline groups: Interior Design, Industrial Design, Interaction Design, Graphic Design, and Animation. The interior design program at SJSU offers two four-year degrees: a BA of Design Studies with a focus in interior design and a professional BFA in Interior Design, both of which are NASAD-accredited and ranked highly in California and in the US. Our faculty includes designers, architects, artists, and historians committed to excellence and collaboration across the department, college, university, Silicon Valley, and beyond. Prospective faculty will have opportunities to engage with the\nAdvanced Institute for Ethical Technologies (AI-ET)\n, which advances interdisciplinary and community-centered dialogue about responsible technology, and the\nDigital Humanities Center\n, a collaborative hub for digitally informed research, teaching, and public engagement.\nThe Department of Design and San José State University value inclusive excellence and are committed to advancing equity through research, teaching, and service. We enroll over 40,000 students, many of whom are historically underserved; approximately 45% of our undergraduate students are first-generation college students and 38% are Pell Grant recipients. In addition, we are both a Hispanic Serving Institution (HSI) and an Asian American and Native American Pacific Islander Serving Institution (AANAPISI). SJSU aspires to create holistic learning environments that facilitate inclusive, empowering spaces for mutual learning, skill-building, and knowledge exchange. We welcome candidates whose creative practice generates new knowledge through research, scholarship, or public engagement.\nWe invite all applicants to include a Statement of Inclusive Excellence (or incorporate it into your cover letter) to share how your lived and professional experiences will contribute to the SJSU community, particularly in relation to student success and inclusive education. A guide to writing this statement can be found at\nSJSU Inclusive Excellence Statement Guidelines\n.\nRequired Qualifications\nA terminal degree (MFA or MIA in Interior Design, MDes, MArch, or PhD) in interior design, architecture or a closely and demonstrably related field. The degree must be completed before the targeted start date.\nEvidence of successful teaching at the undergraduate college level.\nAn active program of creative practice, interior design research, scholarship, or a combination thereof, with a demonstrated research or practice focus on emerging technologies and fabrication in the discipline.\nDemonstrated engagement with contemporary interior design methods, tools, and modes of practice across digital and material media, including critical approaches to design making.\nApplicants must demonstrate an awareness of and sensitivity to the educational goals of a socially and economically diverse student population as might have been gained in cross-cultural study, training, teaching, and other comparable experiences.\nPreferred Qualifications\nPriority will be given to candidates who demonstrate two or more of the following:\nAbility to teach across the curriculum, from foundational design and visualization courses through technical design specialization courses and advanced design studios. Professional visibility through built work, exhibitions, publications, commissions, presentations, professional organizations, or other contributions to the discipline.\nResearch or creative practice specifically engaging emerging technologies, such as digital fabrication, computational or parametric design, immersive (AR/VR/XR) visualization, robotics, AI, or responsive and smart materials.\nEngagement with evolving design practices that advance accessibility, equity, and broader cultural perspectives.\nTrack record of success, or demonstrated potential for success, conducting research or creative activity relevant to the position that results in recognition (e.g., projects that have been published, exhibited, built, commissioned, implemented, recognized through awards, or otherwise disseminated within the discipline).\nKey Responsibilities\nTeach undergraduate students across the interior design curriculum, including foundation studios, space planning and architectural studios, materials and construction, design electives aligned with their expertise and research, and advanced design studios.\nDevelop and sustain an active program of research and/or creative practice appropriate for a tenure-track appointment, with an emphasis on emerging technologies and digital fabrication in interior design.\nMentor, advise, and guide undergraduate students in two interior design degree programs, in portfolio review for the accredited professional degree, and on their professional career paths.\nSupport the interior design program as an integral faculty member by engaging in preservation, administration, and development of program faculty, logistics, spaces, assets, and facilities.\nParticipate in shared governance and development through service at the level of the program, department, college, university, and the profession.\nContribute to an inclusive learning environment, in age, abilities, cultural background, ethnicity, religion, economic background, primary language, sexual orientation, gender identity, and academic preparation, through inclusive course materials, teaching strategies, and advising.\nOther Duties\nNote that all San José State University employees are considered mandated reporters under the California Child Abuse and Neglect Reporting Act and are required to comply with the requirements set forth in CSU Executive Order 1083 as a condition of employment. Pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act) and CSU systemwide policy, some faculty duties may lead to designation as a Campus Security Authority (CSA). CSAs are required to complete Clery Act training and to immediately report Clery incidents to the institution.\nFaculty employees must complete CSU employee training as assigned and required based on their role (e.g., Data Security, FERPA, Preventing Discrimination and Harassment, Title IX, Health and Safety). The President may recommend or require compliance with safety measures that decrease the likelihood of COVID-19 transmission or illness and allows the core mission and activities of the campus to continue.\nApplication Procedure\nSelect Apply Now to complete the SJSU Online Employment Application and attach the following documents:\nLetter of Interest and\ninclusive excellence statement\n(dedicated statement or in letter of interest)\nCurriculum Vitae\nPortfolio of professional, scholarly, or creative work (PDF or website)\nOptional: Additional Portfolio of examples of student design work for which candidate was primary instructor (PDF or link to website)\nStatement of teaching interests and philosophy\nStatement of research and/or creative activity\nReferences: Three names with contact information are due at the time of application.  Note: Three full letters of reference are due at the first stage interview.\nInquiries may be directed to: the Department of Design Chair, Virginia San Fratello (virginia.sanfratello@sjsu.edu), or the Search Committee Chair Eleanor Pries (eleanor.pries@sjsu.edu)\nPursuant to California Education Code 89521, applicants will be required to disclose whether they have received a final administrative decision or final judicial decision determining that they have committed sexual harassment within the last 7 years only after a determination is made that they meet the minimum qualifications for the position, and before an offer of employment is extended.  Applicants who reach the final stages of the application process must also sign a release form that authorizes the release of information by the applicant’s current and/or former employers to the CSU concerning any substantiated allegations of misconduct.\nConditional Offer\nThe work for this faculty position may only be performed in the State of California and requires establishing an on-campus presence. Employment is contingent upon US residence and proof of eligibility to work in the United States. Satisfactory completion of background and reference checks (including criminal records and prior employment) are required. SJSU will make a conditional offer of employment, which may be rescinded if the background and reference checks reveal disqualifying information, and/or it is discovered that the candidate knowingly withheld or falsified information. Failure to satisfactorily complete the background and reference checks may affect the continued employment of a current employee who was conditionally offered the position.\nSan José State University: Silicon Valley's Public University\nLocated in the heart of Silicon Valley — one of the most innovative regions in the world —\nSan José State University\nis the founding campus of the 23-campus California State University (CSU) system and the first public university in the West. Recognized as a leading transformative educational institution, San José State is an essential partner in the technological, economic, cultural, and social development of Silicon Valley, the Bay Area, and California. SJSU is a top-200 school nationally in research funding and second highest in research productivity in the CSU system. Cutting-edge research, world-class scholarship, student-centered learning, diverse communities, and commitment to social justice, allow SJSU to provide life-changing opportunities and advance the public good locally and globally.\nEqual Employment Statement\nSan José State University prohibits discrimination on the basis of Age, Ancestry, Caste, Color, Disability, Ethnicity, Gender, Gender Expression, Gender Identity, Genetic Information, Marital Status, Medical Condition, Military Status, Nationality, Race, Religion, Religious Creed, Sex, Sexual Orientation, Sex Stereotype, and Veteran Status. This policy applies to all San José State University students, faculty, and staff as well as University programs and activities. Reasonable accommodations are made for applicants with disabilities who self-disclose.\nCampus Security and Fire Safety Notification\nPursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, the Annual Security Report (ASR) is also now available for viewing at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Security-Report.pdf\n. The ASR contains the current security and safety-related policy statements, emergency preparedness and evacuation information, crime prevention and Sexual Assault prevention information, and information about drug and alcohol prevention programming. The ASR also contains statistics of Clery crimes for San José State University locations for the three most recent calendar years. A paper copy of the ASR is available upon request by contacting the Office of the Clery Director by phone at 408-924-1501 or by email at clerycompliance@sjsu.edu.\nPursuant to the Higher Education Opportunity Act, the Annual Fire Safety Report (AFSR) is available at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Fire-Safety-Report.pdf\n. The purpose of this report is to disclose statistics for fires that occurred within SJSU on-campus housing facilities for the three most recent calendar years, and to distribute fire safety policies and procedures intended to promote safety on Campus. A paper copy of the AFSR is available upon request by contacting the Housing Office by phone at 408-795-5600 or by email at uhs-frontdesk@sjsu.edu."
    },
    "https://jobs.sjsu.edu/en-us/job/562285/assistant-professor-in-philosophy-of-technology": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Philosophy of Technology",
      "description": "Department: Philosophy\nPosition Rank and Title:\nAssistant Professor in Philosophy of Technology\nSchool/Department Name:\nPhilosophy\nCompensation:\nCommensurate with qualifications, experience, and rank as established by the\nCSU Salary Schedule\n. Anticipated hiring academic year annual salary range: $90,000   -  $94,000\nFaculty employee benefits are very competitive for the higher education sector.  See the\nCSU Employee Benefits Summary\nfor details about the CSU’s excellent benefits package.\nTarget Start Date:\nAugust 17, 2027\nApplication Deadline:\nDecember 1, 2026 (Applications accepted until filled or closed)\nPosition Description\nThe Department of Philosophy at San José State University is pleased to announce a search for a full-time assistant professor (tenure-track) position in Philosophy of Technology beginning in August 2027. While the department welcomes candidates whose research and teaching engage deeply with questions about emerging technologies, the department is open to a broad construal of the technology that is the focus of the candidate’s philosophical work.\nThe College of Humanities & the Arts, and the University more broadly, actively pursues the theme of the intersection of the humanities and the arts with technology. Given our location in Silicon Valley and our commitment to supporting the development of ethical technologies through our new\nAdvanced Institute for Ethical Technologies\n, there are significant interdisciplinary collaborative opportunities for a faculty member exploring these issues through a philosophical lens. The Institute, in the process of being formed, pursues the need for human-centered technology that values privacy and ethical decision-making.  In partnership with the Dr. Martin Luther King, Jr. Library and the San Jose Public Library, the College has also established the\nDigital Humanities Center\nas a site of curriculum development, faculty research, student engagement, and public-facing programming and events.\nThe Department of Philosophy (\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/philosophy/\n) is one of ten departments and schools in the College of Humanities & the Arts (\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/humanitiesandarts/departments.php\n) and offers Philosophy BA and MA programs. We value and engage with a plurality of philosophical traditions and styles, as well as the perspectives of our diverse community of faculty and students. We are committed to creating learning environments, a departmental culture, and a larger philosophical community that welcomes all who want to explore philosophy.\nThe Department of Philosophy and San José State University value institutional excellence and are committed to advancing equity through research, teaching, and service. We enroll more than 40,000 students, many of whom are historically underserved; approximately 45% of our undergraduate students are first-generation college students and 38% are Pell Grant recipients. In addition, we are both a Hispanic Serving Institution (HSI) and an Asian American and Native American Pacific Islander Serving Institution (AANAPISI). SJSU aspires to create holistic learning environments that facilitate both individual and collective transformation. We seek teacher-scholars who cultivate inclusive, empowering spaces for mutual learning, skill-building, and knowledge exchange for all students. Our commitment to lifelong learning supports meaningful connections among faculty, staff, students, alumni, and the broader communities we serve.\nWe invite all applicants to include a Statement of Institutional Excellence (or incorporate it into your cover letter) to share how your lived and professional experiences will contribute to the SJSU community—particularly in relation to student success. A guide to writing this statement can be found at\nSJSU Institutional Excellence Statement Guidelines\n.\nRequired Qualifications\nPh.D. in Philosophy prior to appointment\nA demonstrated record of effective teaching\nEvidence of the ability to publish and engage in professional activities in the area of philosophy of technology\nApplicants should demonstrate an awareness of and sensitivity to the educational goals of a socially and economically diverse student population as might have been gained in cross-cultural study, training, teaching, and other comparable experience\nPreferred Qualifications\nPriority will be given to candidates who possess one or more of the following attributes:\nDemonstrated expertise in an additional area of philosophy (such as global philosophy, ethics, epistemology, political philosophy, philosophy of disability, etc.) that informs their philosophical engagement with technology\nInterests in pursuing cross-tradition engagement in doing philosophical work on technology\nCapacity to contribute to interdisciplinary research and teaching through the Advanced Institute for Ethical Technology (\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/ha-in-action/engage/ai-et.php\n)\nTeaching interests and competencies that could contribute to the department’s teaching needs in history of philosophy, ethical theory, phenomenology, Africana philosophy, Asian philosophy, applied ethics (e.g., professional and business ethics, environmental ethics, medical ethics), or phenomenology\nCapacity to supervise MA students interested in pursuing our\nAdvanced Certificate in Ethics of Science and Emerging Technologies\n.\nKey Responsibilities\nIn addition to teaching courses in the area of specialization, including “Science, Technology, and Human Values” and “Computers, Ethics and Society”, the candidate will be expected to teach other general education courses that could include Introduction to Philosophy; Logic and Critical Reasoning; Moral Issues; Asian Philosophy; and Professional and Business Ethics; or courses in the history of philosophy.  The candidate will also contribute to the department’s ability to offer courses through\nSJSU Online\n.\nThe candidate will sustain an ongoing record of research, scholarship, and/or creative activities, as well as other professional engagement.\nThe candidate will participate in shared governance, usually in department, college, and university committees and other service assignments.\nFaculty shall organize all their classes within the Canvas Learning Management System (LMS).\nThe candidate must demonstrate awareness and experience in understanding the needs of a student population of great diversity – in age, abilities, cultural background, ethnicity, religion, economic background, primary language, sexual orientation, gender identity, and academic preparation – through inclusive course materials, teaching strategies, and advisement.\nOther Duties\nNote that all San José State University employees are considered mandated reporters under the California Child Abuse and Neglect Reporting Act and are required to comply with the requirements set forth in CSU Executive Order 1083 as a condition of employment. Pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act) and CSU systemwide policy, some faculty duties may lead to designation as a Campus Security Authority (CSA). CSAs are required to complete Clery Act training and to immediately report Clery incidents to the institution.\nFaculty employees must complete CSU employee training as assigned and required based on their role (e.g., Data Security, FERPA, Preventing Discrimination and Harassment, Title IX, Health and Safety). The President may recommend or require compliance with safety measures that decrease the likelihood of COVID-19 transmission or illness and allows the core mission and activities of the campus to continue.\nApplication Procedure\nClick Apply Now to complete the SJSU Online Employment Application and attach the following documents:\nletter of interest\ncurriculum vitae\noptional\ninstitutional excellence statement\n(dedicated statement of about 2 pages if not addressed in letter of interest)\nstatement of teaching interests/philosophy (2 pages)\nevidence of teaching proficiency (e.g., student evaluations)\nresearch plans (2 pages)\nwriting sample (preferably 5000 words or less)\nthree references with contact information\nInquiries may be directed to the Department Chair, Prof. Janet D. Stemwedel (janet.stemwedel@sjsu.edu)\nPursuant to California Education Code 89521, applicants will be required to disclose whether they have received a final administrative decision or final judicial decision determining that they have committed sexual harassment within the last 7 years only after a determination is made that they meet the minimum qualifications for the position, and before an offer of employment is extended.  Applicants who reach the final stages of the application process must also sign a release form that authorizes the release of information by the applicant’s current and/or former employers to the CSU concerning any substantiated allegations of misconduct.\nConditional Offer\nThe work for this faculty position may only be performed in the State of California and requires establishing an on-campus presence.  Employment is contingent upon US residence and proof of eligibility to work in the United States. Satisfactory completion of background and reference checks (including criminal records and prior employment) are required. SJSU will make a conditional offer of employment, which may be rescinded if the background and reference checks reveal disqualifying information, and/or it is discovered that the candidate knowingly withheld or falsified information. Failure to satisfactorily complete the background and reference checks may affect the continued employment of a current employee who was conditionally offered the position.\nSan José State University: Silicon Valley's Public University\nLocated in the heart of Silicon Valley — one of the most innovative regions in the world —\nSan José State University\nis the founding campus of the 23-campus California State University (CSU) system and the first public university in the West. Recognized as a leading transformative educational institution, San José State is an essential partner in the technological, economic, cultural, and social development of Silicon Valley, the Bay Area, and California. SJSU is a top-200 school nationally in research funding and second highest in research productivity in the CSU system. Cutting-edge research, world-class scholarship, student-centered learning, diverse communities, and commitment to social justice, allow SJSU to provide life-changing opportunities and advance the public good locally and globally.\nEqual Employment Statement\nSan José State University prohibits discrimination on the basis of Age, Ancestry, Caste, Color, Disability, Ethnicity, Gender, Gender Expression, Gender Identity, Genetic Information, Marital Status, Medical Condition, Military Status, Nationality, Race, Religion, Religious Creed, Sex, Sexual Orientation, Sex Stereotype, and Veteran Status. This policy applies to all San José State University students, faculty, and staff as well as University programs and activities. Reasonable accommodations are made for applicants with disabilities who self-disclose.\nCampus Security and Fire Safety Notification\nPursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, the Annual Security Report (ASR) is also now available for viewing at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Security-Report.pdf\n. The ASR contains the current security and safety-related policy statements, emergency preparedness and evacuation information, crime prevention and Sexual Assault prevention information, and information about drug and alcohol prevention programming. The ASR also contains statistics of Clery crimes for San José State University locations for the three most recent calendar years. A paper copy of the ASR is available upon request by contacting the Office of the Clery Director by phone at 408-924-1501 or by email at clerycompliance@sjsu.edu.\nPursuant to the Higher Education Opportunity Act, the Annual Fire Safety Report (AFSR) is available at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Fire-Safety-Report.pdf\n. The purpose of this report is to disclose statistics for fires that occurred within SJSU on-campus housing facilities for the three most recent calendar years, and to distribute fire safety policies and procedures intended to promote safety on Campus. A paper copy of the AFSR is available upon request by contacting the Housing Office by phone at 408-795-5600 or by email at uhs-frontdesk@sjsu.edu."
    },
    "https://jobs.sjsu.edu/en-us/job/562288/assistant-professor-in-art-art-history-photography": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Art & Art History - Photography",
      "description": "Department: Department of Art and Art History\nPosition Rank and Title:\nTenure-Track Assistant Professor of Photography, emphasis in AI and Digital Imaging\nSchool/Department Name:\nDepartment of Art and Art History\nCompensation:\nCommensurate with qualifications, experience, and rank as established by the\nCSU Salary Schedule\n. Anticipated hiring academic year annual salary range: $90,000 - $94,000.\nFaculty employee benefits are very competitive for the higher education sector.  See the\nCSU Employee Benefits Summary\nfor details about the CSU’s excellent benefits package.\nTarget Start Date:\nAugust 15, 2027\nApplication Deadline:\n15 December, 2026\nPosition Description: Tenure-Track Assistant Professor of Photography, emphasis in AI and Digital Imaging\nThe Department of Art and Art History at San José State University invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor in Photography with a focus on emerging technologies, including Artificial Intelligence (AI), digital imaging, computational photography, and related tools shaping contemporary lens-based practice. We seek an artist-scholar whose work critically and creatively engages new and evolving imaging technologies within interdisciplinary contemporary art practice. At a moment when AI and machine learning are often construed as technological threats to the independence of artistic labor, we are particularly interested in candidates who approach these emerging technologies as tools within a broader, conceptually rigorous set of processes, integrating digital technologies, including AI, with material, embodied, and socially engaged forms of production.\nWe are especially interested in artists whose practices move fluidly between digital and physical forms, and who explore how emerging technologies intersect with material processes, installation, performance, sculpture, print, or other expanded media. Collaborative and cross-disciplinary approaches are highly valued.\nLocated in the heart of Silicon Valley, SJSU offers a unique context for examining the aesthetic, cultural, political, and ethical dimensions of AI and technological innovation. The College of Humanities and the Arts hosts an Advanced Institute for Ethical Technologies (AI-ET) and a Digital Humanities Center, the latter in collaboration with the SJSU King Library. The ideal candidate will help students develop critical, adaptive, and responsible creative practices that prepare them to navigate and shape a rapidly evolving visual landscape. San José State University is committed to excellence through diversity and to fostering an inclusive academic community. We encourage applications from candidates who will contribute to the cultural and intellectual diversity of our campus.\nThe Department of Art and Art History and San José State University value institutional excellence and are committed to advancing equity through research, teaching, and service. We enroll more than 40,000 students, many of whom are historically underserved; approximately 45% of our undergraduate students are first-generation college students and 38% are Pell Grant recipients. In addition, we are both a Hispanic Serving Institution (HSI) and an Asian American and Native American Pacific Islander Serving Institution (AANAPISI). SJSU aspires to create holistic learning environments that facilitate both individual and collective transformation. We seek teacher-scholars who cultivate inclusive, empowering spaces for mutual learning, skill-building, and knowledge exchange for all students. Our commitment to lifelong learning supports meaningful connections among faculty, staff, students, alumni, and the broader communities we serve.\nWe invite all applicants to include a Statement of Institutional Excellence (or incorporate it into your cover letter) to share how your lived and professional experiences will contribute to the SJSU community—particularly in relation to student success. A guide to writing this statement can be found at\nSJSU Institutional Excellence Statement Guidelines\n.\nRequired Qualifications\nTerminal Degree in Relevant Field: MFA, PhD, or equivalent terminal degree in Photography, Fine Art, Media Art, or a closely related field by the time of appointment.\nAn active exhibition record and a clearly articulated research agenda in photography and lens-based media.\nA strong grounding in the history, theory, and philosophy of photography and image culture.\nThe ability to teach across undergraduate (BFA) and graduate (MFA) levels in digital photography, studio lighting, and/or related areas.\nApplicants should demonstrate an awareness of and sensitivity to the educational goals of a socially and economically diverse student population as might have been gained in cross-cultural study, training, teaching, and other comparable experience.\nPreferred Qualifications\nPriority will be given to candidates who possess one or more of the following:\nTwo or more years of university teaching experience.\nExperience working in an interdisciplinary environment.\nTechnical Aptitude for and Critical engagement with emerging technologies such as AI/ML, generative imaging, computational or expanded photography, digital video, and hybrid analog/digital processes.\nCommitment to interdisciplinary collaboration within a department that includes robust programs in Digital Media Art, Studio Practice, and Art History; and within a college that includes an Advanced Institute for Ethical Technologies and a Digital Humanities Center.\nExperience in mentorship and student-centered learning: Preference will be given to applicants with a strong background in mentorship and a commitment to student-centered learning approaches.\nKey Responsibilities\nTeach graduate and undergraduate courses in Photography and appropriate areas of interdisciplinary art.\nAdvise BFA and MFA students in Photography\nConduct research and creative activity related to areas of expertise\nThe candidate will participate in shared governance, usually in department, college, and university committees and other service assignments.\nFaculty shall organize all their classes within the Canvas Learning Management System (LMS).\nThe candidate must demonstrate awareness and experience in understanding the needs of a student population of great diversity – in age, abilities, cultural background, ethnicity, religion, economic background, primary language, sexual orientation, gender identity, and academic preparation – through inclusive course materials, teaching strategies, and advisement.\nContribute to the development and evolution of the Photography curriculum, incorporating contemporary methods and practices in lens-based practices.\nOther Duties\nNote that all San José State University employees are considered mandated reporters under the California Child Abuse and Neglect Reporting Act and are required to comply with the requirements set forth in CSU Executive Order 1083 as a condition of employment. Pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act) and CSU systemwide policy, some faculty duties may lead to designation as a Campus Security Authority (CSA). CSAs are required to complete Clery Act training and to immediately report Clery incidents to the institution.\nFaculty employees must complete CSU employee training as assigned and required based on their role (e.g., Data Security, FERPA, Preventing Discrimination and Harassment, Title IX, Health and Safety). The President may recommend or require compliance with safety measures that decrease the likelihood of COVID-19 transmission or illness and allows the core mission and activities of the campus to continue.\nApplication Procedure\nClick Apply Now to complete the SJSU Online Employment Application and attach the following documents [\ndepartments may specify length of items below (e.g. 2 pages)\n]:\nletter of interest\ncurriculum vitae\ninstitutional excellence statement\n(dedicated statement or addressed in letter of interest)\nstatement of teaching interests/philosophy\nresearch plans\nthree reference letters\nInquiries may be directed to the Acting Department Chair/ Search Committee Chair: Anthony Raynsford\nAnthony.Raynsford@sjsu.edu\nPursuant to California Education Code 89521, applicants will be required to disclose whether they have received a final administrative decision or final judicial decision determining that they have committed sexual harassment within the last 7 years only after a determination is made that they meet the minimum qualifications for the position, and before an offer of employment is extended.  Applicants who reach the final stages of the application process must also sign a release form that authorizes the release of information by the applicant’s current and/or former employers to the CSU concerning any substantiated allegations of misconduct.\nConditional Offer\nThe work for this faculty position may only be performed in the State of California and requires establishing an on-campus presence. Employment is contingent upon US residence and proof of eligibility to work in the United States. Satisfactory completion of background and reference checks (including criminal records and prior employment) are required. SJSU will make a conditional offer of employment, which may be rescinded if the background and reference checks reveal disqualifying information, and/or it is discovered that the candidate knowingly withheld or falsified information. Failure to satisfactorily complete the background and reference checks may affect the continued employment of a current employee who was conditionally offered the position.\nSan José State University: Silicon Valley's Public University\nLocated in the heart of Silicon Valley — one of the most innovative regions in the world —\nSan José State University\nis the founding campus of the 23-campus California State University (CSU) system and the first public university in the West. Recognized as a leading transformative educational institution, San José State is an essential partner in the technological, economic, cultural, and social development of Silicon Valley, the Bay Area, and California. SJSU is a top-200 school nationally in research funding and second highest in research productivity in the CSU system. Cutting-edge research, world-class scholarship, student-centered learning, diverse communities, and commitment to social justice, allow SJSU to provide life-changing opportunities and advance the public good locally and globally.\nEqual Employment Statement\nSan José State University prohibits discrimination on the basis of Age, Ancestry, Caste, Color, Disability, Ethnicity, Gender, Gender Expression, Gender Identity, Genetic Information, Marital Status, Medical Condition, Military Status, Nationality, Race, Religion, Religious Creed, Sex, Sexual Orientation, Sex Stereotype, and Veteran Status. This policy applies to all San José State University students, faculty, and staff as well as University programs and activities. Reasonable accommodations are made for applicants with disabilities who self-disclose.\nCampus Security and Fire Safety Notification\nPursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, the Annual Security Report (ASR) is also now available for viewing at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Security-Report.pdf\n. The ASR contains the current security and safety-related policy statements, emergency preparedness and evacuation information, crime prevention and Sexual Assault prevention information, and information about drug and alcohol prevention programming. The ASR also contains statistics of Clery crimes for San José State University locations for the three most recent calendar years. A paper copy of the ASR is available upon request by contacting the Office of the Clery Director by phone at 408-924-1501 or by email at clerycompliance@sjsu.edu.\nPursuant to the Higher Education Opportunity Act, the Annual Fire Safety Report (AFSR) is available at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Fire-Safety-Report.pdf\n. The purpose of this report is to disclose statistics for fires that occurred within SJSU on-campus housing facilities for the three most recent calendar years, and to distribute fire safety policies and procedures intended to promote safety on Campus. A paper copy of the AFSR is available upon request by contacting the Housing Office by phone at 408-795-5600 or by email at uhs-frontdesk@sjsu.edu."
    },
    "https://jobs.sjsu.edu/en-us/job/562290/assistant-professor-in-dept-humanities-digital-humanities": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Dept Humanities - Digital Humanities",
      "description": "Position\nRank\nand\nTitle:\nAssistant Professor of Digital Humanities / Humanities\nDepartment\nName:\nHumanities Department\nCompensation:\nCommensurate with qualifications, experience, and rank as established by the\nCSU Salary\nSchedule\n. Anticipated hiring academic year annual salary range: $90,000-$94,000\nFaculty employee benefits are very competitive for the higher education sector. See the\nCSU Employee\nBenefits Summary\nfor details about the CSU’s excellent benefits package.\nTarget\nStart\nDate:\n17 August, 2027\nApplication\nDeadline:\n1\nDecember\n2026\n(Applications accepted until filled or closed)\nPosition Description\nThe Department of Humanities at San José State University invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor of Digital Humanities or Humanities, with an expertise that lies at the intersection of critical science and technology studies with the humanities. We seek a teacher-scholar whose research, pedagogy, and community engagement examine the social and cultural roles, implications, impacts, aﬀordances, etc., of technologies within their concomitant historical, economic, social, and cultural contexts.\nSuccessful candidates will be grounded in critical science and technology studies while engaging in other humanities fields and questions, for example: artificial intelligence, knowledge, and creativity; data privacy and sovereignty; social bonds and groups; visual arts; arts and social practice; technology, race, and ethnicity; chatbots and religious experience; technology and religious change; technology and the environment; science, technology, and ethics; etc. We value scholar-teachers who will be able to teach in both the Digital Humanities and in other programs within the department, depending on expertise (American Studies, Creative Arts, Environmental Humanities, and/or Religious Studies) as well as our robust General Education oﬀerings.\nThe Humanities Department at SJSU is a prominent center for creative, critical, interdisciplinary study and research on campus. Both the department and the College of Humanities & Arts are committed to equitable and culturally sustaining teaching and scholarship, recruiting, hiring, and nurturing a diverse faculty that mirrors our student body. We support a full range of scholarly and creative activities, from traditional research to scholarship of engagement and public humanities to creative work. We believe that faculty and student success are inextricably linked and seek faculty who will support and enhance these missions.\nOur Department’s Digital Humanities program is currently growing, with three courses and a certificate (coming Fall 2027). In addition, the Humanities Department oﬀers three B.A. Programs: a Liberal Studies Teacher Preparation B.A.; a Creative Arts B.A., which includes an Arts Therapy Preparation concentration; and a Humanities B.A., in which students pursue a concentration in American Studies, Liberal Arts, or Religious Studies. We also house a range of certificates and minors, including Environmental Humanities and Middle East Studies. The department also houses the Humanities Honors program, which is open to all interested students, and provides multi-semester, enriching and in-depth courses and experiences.\nThe College of Humanities & Arts is committed to the Digital Humanities and to fully participating and intervening from critical humanistic perspectives into the campus engagement with technology. In conjunction with the Martin Luther King, Jr., Library, has created a Digital Humanities Center, partially funded by an NEH grant. This Center is public facing and oﬀers a range of events and opportunities for students and members of the community. Further, the College has created the Advanced Institute for Ethics and Technology (ai-et), and is currently taking application for the first class of Fellows to the institute. The successful candidate will have the opportunity to work with both of these, depending on their interests.\nThe Department of Humanities and San José State University value institutional excellence and are committed to advancing equity through research, teaching, and service. We enroll more than 40,000 students, many of whom are historically underserved; approximately 45% of our undergraduate students are first-generation college students and 38% are Pell Grant recipients. In addition, we are both a Hispanic Serving Institution (HSI) and an Asian American and Native American Pacific Islander Serving Institution (AANAPISI). SJSU aspires to create holistic learning environments that facilitate both individual and collective transformation. We seek teacher-scholars who cultivate inclusive, empowering spaces for mutual learning, skill-building, and knowledge exchange for all students. Our commitment to lifelong learning supports meaningful connections among faculty, staﬀ, students, alumni, and the broader communities we serve.\nWe invite all applicants to include a Statement of Institutional Excellence (or incorporate it into your cover letter) to share how your lived and professional experiences will contribute to the SJSU community—particularly in relation to student success. A guide to writing this statement can be found at\nSJSU\nInstitutional Excellence Statement Guidelines\n.\"\nRequired Qualifications\nA doctorate in Digital Humanities, Critical Science and Technology Studies, Culture Studies, Humanities or related fields (e.g., American Studies, Ethnic Studies, Religious Studies, etc.)\nDemonstrated potential for eﬀective, culturally sustaining teaching, and/or successful teaching experience.\nRecord of, or demonstrated potential for, research, scholarship, or creative activity in the field of Digital Humanities, Critical Science and Technology Studies, and/or related fields.\nApplicants should demonstrate an awareness of and sensitivity to the educational goals of a socially and economically diverse student population as might have been gained in cross-cultural study, training, teaching, and other comparable experience.\nPreferred Qualifications\nPriority will be given to candidates who possess one or more of the following:\nDemonstrated experience, expertise, and/or scholarship in the social and cultural significance and impact of “artificial intelligence,” social media, digital data, and/or surveillance technologies\nWork in engaged scholarship and/or public humanities\nDemonstrated experience or interest in pedagogical approaches that account for broad cultural, learning, and socio-economic diversity within a classroom, including the ability to apply diﬀerent kinds of critical, decolonial, and culturally sustaining pedagogies\nDemonstrated ability to collaborate and lead on issues of research/scholarship, pedagogy, curriculum, etc.\nKey Responsibilities\nTeach undergraduate courses in Digital Humanities and other programs in the department; teaching load is three 3-unit courses per semester (as long as faculty is eligible for the RSCA Assigned Time” program); develop new curriculum as program, department, and student needs and interests dictate.\nMaintain an active research/scholarship, creative, and/or community-engaged agenda as appropriate to your tenure-track appointment and aligned with the broad topic of technology and society/culture.\nAdvise and mentor students and develop cross-departmental and institutional partnerships and collaborations as warranted by teaching and scholarly interests and departmental needs.\nThe candidate will participate in shared governance, usually in department, college, and university committees and other service assignments.\nFaculty shall organize all their classes within the Canvas Learning Management System (LMS).\nThe candidate must demonstrate awareness and experience in understanding the needs of a student population of great diversity – in age, abilities, cultural background, ethnicity, religion, economic background, primary language, sexual orientation, gender identity, and academic preparation – through inclusive course materials, teaching strategies, and advisement.\nOther Duties\nNote that all San José State University employees are considered mandated reporters under the California Child Abuse and Neglect Reporting Act and are required to comply with the requirements set forth in CSU Executive Order 1083 as a condition of employment. Pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act) and CSU systemwide policy, some faculty duties may lead to designation as a Campus Security Authority (CSA). CSAs are required to complete Clery Act training and to immediately report Clery incidents to the institution.\nFaculty employees must complete CSU employee training as assigned and required based on their role (e.g., Data Security, FERPA, Preventing Discrimination and Harassment, Title IX, Health and Safety). The President may recommend or require compliance with safety measures that decrease the likelihood of COVID-19 transmission or illness and allows the core mission and activities of the campus to continue.\nApplication Procedure\nClick Apply Now to complete the SJSU Online Employment Application and attach the following documents [\ndepartments may specify length of items below (e.g. 2 pages)\n]:\nletter of interest (no more than 2 pages)\ncurriculum vitae\ninstitutional excellence statement\n(dedicated statement, no more than 2 pages)\nstatement of teaching interests/philosophy (no more than 2 pages)\nStatement of research/scholarship (2 pages)\nThree references with contact information\nPursuant to California Education Code 89521, applicants will be required to disclose whether they have received a final administrative decision or final judicial decision determining that they have committed sexual harassment within the last 7 years only after a determination is made that they meet the minimum qualifications for the position, and before an oﬀer of employment is extended. Applicants who reach the final stages of the application process must also sign a release form that authorizes the release of information by the applicant’s current and/or former employers to the CSU concerning any substantiated allegations of misconduct.\nInquiries may be directed to the Department Chair or Search Committee Chair: J. Todd Ormsbee\ntodd.ormsbee@sjsu.edu\nConditional Oﬀer\nThe work for this faculty position may only be performed in the State of California and requires establishing an on-campus presence. Employment is contingent upon US residence and proof of eligibility to work in the United States. Satisfactory completion of background and reference checks (including criminal records and prior employment) are required. SJSU will make a conditional oﬀer of employment, which may be\nrescinded if the background and reference checks reveal disqualifying information, and/or it is discovered that the candidate knowingly withheld or falsified information. Failure to satisfactorily complete the background and reference checks may aﬀect the continued employment of a current employee who was conditionally oﬀered the position.\nSan José State University: Silicon Valley's Public University\nLocated in the heart of Silicon Valley — one of the most innovative regions in the world —\nSan José State\nUniversity\nis the founding campus of the 23-campus California State University (CSU) system and the first public university in the West. Recognized as a leading transformative educational institution, San José State is an essential partner in the technological, economic, cultural, and social development of Silicon Valley, the Bay Area, and California. SJSU is a top-200 school nationally in research funding and second highest in research productivity in the CSU system. Cutting-edge research, world-class scholarship, student-centered learning, diverse communities, and commitment to social justice, allow SJSU to provide life-changing opportunities and advance the public good locally and globally.\nEqual Employment Statement\nSan José State University prohibits discrimination on the basis of Age, Ancestry, Caste, Color, Disability, Ethnicity, Gender, Gender Expression, Gender Identity, Genetic Information, Marital Status, Medical Condition, Military Status, Nationality, Race, Religion, Religious Creed, Sex, Sexual Orientation, Sex Stereotype, and Veteran Status. This policy applies to all San José State University students, faculty, and staﬀ as well as University programs and activities. Reasonable accommodations are made for applicants with disabilities who self-disclose.\nCampus Security and Fire Safety Notification\nPursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, the Annual Security Report (ASR) is also now available for viewing at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-\nAnnual-Security-Report.pdf\n. The ASR contains the current security and safety-related policy statements, emergency preparedness and evacuation information, crime prevention and Sexual Assault prevention information, and information about drug and alcohol prevention programming. The ASR also contains statistics of Clery crimes for San José State University locations for the three most recent calendar years. A paper copy of the ASR is available upon request by contacting the Oﬃce of the Clery Director by phone at 408-924-1501 or by email at\nclerycompliance@sjsu.edu.\nPursuant to the Higher Education Opportunity Act, the Annual Fire Safety Report (AFSR) is available at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Fire-Safety-Report.pdf.\nThe purpose of this report is to disclose statistics for fires that occurred within SJSU on-campus housing facilities for the three most recent calendar years, and to distribute fire safety policies and procedures intended to promote safety on Campus. A paper copy of the AFSR is available upon request by contacting the Housing Oﬃce by phone at 408-795-5600 or by email at\nuhs-frontdesk@sjsu.edu."
    },
    "https://jobs.sjsu.edu/en-us/job/561936/assistant-professor-marketing-business-analytics-business-analytics": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Marketing & Business Analytics - Business Analytics",
      "description": "Department: Marketing and Business Analytics\nPosition Rank and Title:\nAssistant Professor of Business Analytics\nSchool/Department Name:\nMarketing and Business Analytics\nCompensation:\nCommensurate with qualifications, experience, and rank as established by the\nCSU Salary Schedule\n. Anticipated hiring academic year annual salary range: $135,000  -  $141,750.\nFaculty employee benefits are very competitive for the higher education sector.  See the\nCSU Employee Benefits Summary\nfor details about the CSU’s excellent benefits package.\nTarget Start Date:\nAugust 17, 2027\nApplication Deadline:\nOctober 16, 2026 (Applications accepted until filled or closed)\nPosition Description\nThe Marketing and Business Analytics Department at San José State University is pleased to announce a search for a tenure-track faculty position in Business Analytics at the Assistant Professor level. Applicants should be interested in research and teaching in areas such as AI in Business Analytics, Generative AI and Business Decision-Making, Optimization, Predictive Analytics, Stochastic Processes, Data Mining, and Data Visualization. Teaching will be at the undergraduate and Masters level.\nThe Department is home to a large community of scholars and students. We foster strong ties to the Silicon Valley business community. We have top level Silicon Valley executives teach and engage with our students, and have student teams actively pursuing projects with Silicon Valley companies. We are a balanced Department, and are searching for candidates who share our dedication to both quality teaching and research. We teach at both the undergraduate and Masters levels. We conduct methodological and applied research. For more information, visit\nour website\n.\nWe are housed in the Lucas College and Graduate School of Business, the largest provider of business graduates to Silicon Valley. Blending a multi-cultural, global perspective with Silicon Valley’s historic foundation of technological innovation, our programs reflect our esteemed local heritage. With over 80% of our students working in the Valley, our business students and alumni are truly Powering Silicon Valley. We provide the resources, skills, and opportunities for students to succeed. The college as well as the university are highly supportive and committed to faculty success. Our faculty are highly engaged with our students and serve as mentors and advisors to student organizations including the Marketing Association, Spartan Analytics, Latino Business Student Association, Women in Business, and more.\nThe Department of Marketing and Business Analytics and San José State University value institutional excellence and are committed to advancing equity through research, teaching, and service. We enroll more than 40,000 students, many of whom are historically underserved; approximately 45% of our undergraduate students are first-generation college students and 38% are Pell Grant recipients. In addition, we are both a Hispanic Serving Institution (HSI) and an Asian American and Native American Pacific Islander Serving Institution (AANAPISI). SJSU aspires to create holistic learning environments that facilitate both individual and collective transformation. We seek teacher-scholars who cultivate inclusive, empowering spaces for mutual learning, skill-building, and knowledge exchange for all students. Our commitment to lifelong learning supports meaningful connections among faculty, staff, students, alumni, and the broader communities we serve.\nWe invite all applicants to include a Statement of Institutional Excellence (or incorporate it into your cover letter) to share how your lived and professional experiences will contribute to the SJSU community—particularly in relation to student success. A guide to writing this statement can be found at\nSJSU Institutional Excellence Statement Guidelines\n.\nRequired Qualifications\nA doctorate from an accredited university. A doctoral degree must be fully awarded by the date of appointment in order to start, but preferably by May 31, 2027.\nEvidence of teaching excellence or potential for teaching excellence.\nDemonstrated record of, or potential for, scholarly publications and the development of a strong scholarly record commensurate with rank and suitable for advancement.\nInterest in engaging with Silicon Valley professionals and connecting our students – largely first generation, economically diverse, and multicultural – to the business community.\nEvidence of awareness and sensitivity to the educational goals of a socially and economically diverse student population as might have been gained in cross-cultural study, training, teaching, and other comparable experience.\nPreferred Qualifications\nCandidates whose research focus incorporates AI will be given priority.\nKey Responsibilities\nTeaching Business Analytics courses, with an emphasis on AI – for example, AI in Business Analytics, Generative AI and Business Decision-Making – along with traditional Business Analytics topics such as Optimization, Predictive Analytics, Stochastic Processes, Data Mining, and Data Visualization. Teaching assignments are at both the undergraduate and graduate levels in traditional and accelerated formats.\nEngaging in scholarly activities as evidenced by developing and maintaining an active research agenda in peer reviewed high-quality journals and by presenting at academic and professional conferences.\nThe candidate will participate in shared governance, usually in department, college, and university committees and other service assignments.\nFaculty shall organize all their classes within the Canvas Learning Management System (LMS).\nThe candidate must demonstrate awareness and experience in understanding the needs of a student population of great diversity – in age, abilities, cultural background, ethnicity, religion, economic background, primary language, sexual orientation, gender identity, and academic preparation – through inclusive course materials, teaching strategies, and advisement.\nOther Duties\nNote that all San José State University employees are considered mandated reporters under the California Child Abuse and Neglect Reporting Act and are required to comply with the requirements set forth in CSU Executive Order 1083 as a condition of employment. Pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act) and CSU systemwide policy, some faculty duties may lead to designation as a Campus Security Authority (CSA). CSAs are required to complete Clery Act training and to immediately report Clery incidents to the institution.\nFaculty employees must complete CSU employee training as assigned and required based on their role (e.g., Data Security, FERPA, Preventing Discrimination and Harassment, Title IX, Health and Safety). The President may recommend or require compliance with safety measures that decrease the likelihood of COVID-19 transmission or illness and allows the core mission and activities of the campus to continue.\nApplication Procedure\nClick Apply Now to complete the SJSU Online Employment Application and attach the following documents:\nletter of interest\ncurriculum vitae\ninstitutional excellence statement\n(dedicated statement or addressed in letter of interest)\nstatement of teaching interests/philosophy (please list the courses you have taught, and/or would like to teach).\nstatement of research agenda and future plans (one page)\nthree reference letters with contact information\nInquiries may be directed to the Department Chair, Dr. David Czerwinski, david.czerwinski@sjsu.edu\n.\nPursuant to California Education Code 89521, applicants will be required to disclose whether they have received a final administrative decision or final judicial decision determining that they have committed sexual harassment within the last 7 years only after a determination is made that they meet the minimum qualifications for the position, and before an offer of employment is extended.  Applicants who reach the final stages of the application process must also sign a release form that authorizes the release of information by the applicant’s current and/or former employers to the CSU concerning any substantiated allegations of misconduct.\nConditional Offer\nThe work for this faculty position may only be performed in the State of California and requires establishing an on-campus presence. Employment is contingent upon US residence and proof of eligibility to work in the United States. Satisfactory completion of background and reference checks (including criminal records and prior employment) are required. SJSU will make a conditional offer of employment, which may be rescinded if the background and reference checks reveal disqualifying information, and/or it is discovered that the candidate knowingly withheld or falsified information. Failure to satisfactorily complete the background and reference checks may affect the continued employment of a current employee who was conditionally offered the position.\nSan José State University: Silicon Valley's Public University\nLocated in the heart of Silicon Valley — one of the most innovative regions in the world —\nSan José State University\nis the founding campus of the 23-campus California State University (CSU) system and the first public university in the West. Recognized as a leading transformative educational institution, San José State is an essential partner in the technological, economic, cultural, and social development of Silicon Valley, the Bay Area, and California. SJSU is a top-200 school nationally in research funding and second highest in research productivity in the CSU system. Cutting-edge research, world-class scholarship, student-centered learning, diverse communities, and commitment to social justice, allow SJSU to provide life-changing opportunities and advance the public good locally and globally.\nEqual Employment Statement\nSan José State University prohibits discrimination on the basis of Age, Ancestry, Caste, Color, Disability, Ethnicity, Gender, Gender Expression, Gender Identity, Genetic Information, Marital Status, Medical Condition, Military Status, Nationality, Race, Religion, Religious Creed, Sex, Sexual Orientation, Sex Stereotype, and Veteran Status. This policy applies to all San José State University students, faculty, and staff as well as University programs and activities. Reasonable accommodations are made for applicants with disabilities who self-disclose.\nCampus Security and Fire Safety Notification\nPursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, the Annual Security Report (ASR) is also now available for viewing at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Security-Report.pdf\n. The ASR contains the current security and safety-related policy statements, emergency preparedness and evacuation information, crime prevention and Sexual Assault prevention information, and information about drug and alcohol prevention programming. The ASR also contains statistics of Clery crimes for San José State University locations for the three most recent calendar years. A paper copy of the ASR is available upon request by contacting the Office of the Clery Director by phone at 408-924-1501 or by email at clerycompliance@sjsu.edu.\nPursuant to the Higher Education Opportunity Act, the Annual Fire Safety Report (AFSR) is available at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Fire-Safety-Report.pdf\n. The purpose of this report is to disclose statistics for fires that occurred within SJSU on-campus housing facilities for the three most recent calendar years, and to distribute fire safety policies and procedures intended to promote safety on Campus. A paper copy of the AFSR is available upon request by contacting the Housing Office by phone at 408-795-5600 or by email at uhs-frontdesk@sjsu.edu."
    },
    "https://jobs.sjsu.edu/en-us/job/561937/assistant-professor-marketing-business-analytics-marketing": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Marketing & Business Analytics - Marketing",
      "description": "Department: Marketing and Business Analytics\nPosition Rank and Title:\nAssistant Professor of Marketing\nSchool/Department Name:\nMarketing and Business Analytics\nCompensation:\nCommensurate with qualifications, experience, and rank as established by the\nCSU Salary Schedule\n. Anticipated hiring academic year annual salary range: $135,000  -  $141,750.\nFaculty employee benefits are very competitive for the higher education sector.  See the\nCSU Employee Benefits Summary\nfor details about the CSU’s excellent benefits package.\nTarget Start Date:\nAugust 17, 2027\nApplication Deadline:\nOctober 30, 2026 (Applications accepted until filled or closed)\nPosition Description\nThe Marketing and Business Analytics Department at San José State University is pleased to announce a search for a tenure-track faculty position in Marketing at the Assistant Professor level. Applicants should be interested in teaching AI-based Marketing courses, such as Marketing Automation, Digital Marketing with AI, AI-Driven Customer Insights, and AI in Marketing Analytics, as well as traditional Marketing courses such as Marketing Management, Marketing Analytics, New Product Development, and International Marketing. Applicants ideally should have research that is related to AI-based Marketing. Teaching will be at the undergraduate and Masters level.\nThe Department is home to a large community of scholars and students. We foster strong ties to the Silicon Valley business community. We have top level Silicon Valley executives teach and engage with our students, and have student teams actively pursuing projects with Silicon Valley companies. We are a balanced Department, and are searching for candidates who share our dedication to both quality teaching and research. We teach courses at both the undergraduate and Masters levels. Our research includes analytical, behavioral, strategic, and applied methodologies and interests. For more information, visit\nour website\n.\nWe are housed in the Lucas College and Graduate School of Business, the largest provider of business graduates to Silicon Valley. Blending a multi-cultural, global perspective with Silicon Valley’s historic foundation of technological innovation, our programs reflect our esteemed local heritage. With over 80% of our students working in the Valley, our business students and alumni are truly Powering Silicon Valley. We provide the resources, skills, and opportunities for students to succeed. The college as well as the university are highly supportive and committed to faculty success. Our faculty are highly engaged with our students and serve as mentors and advisors to student organizations including the Marketing Association, Spartan Analytics, Latino Business Student Association, Women in Business, and more.\nThe Department of Marketing and Business Analytics and San José State University value institutional excellence and are committed to advancing equity through research, teaching, and service. We enroll more than 40,000 students, many of whom are historically underserved; approximately 45% of our undergraduate students are first-generation college students and 38% are Pell Grant recipients. In addition, we are both a Hispanic Serving Institution (HSI) and an Asian American and Native American Pacific Islander Serving Institution (AANAPISI). SJSU aspires to create holistic learning environments that facilitate both individual and collective transformation. We seek teacher-scholars who cultivate inclusive, empowering spaces for mutual learning, skill-building, and knowledge exchange for all students. Our commitment to lifelong learning supports meaningful connections among faculty, staff, students, alumni, and the broader communities we serve.\nWe invite all applicants to include a Statement of Institutional Excellence (or incorporate it into your cover letter) to share how your lived and professional experiences will contribute to the SJSU community—particularly in relation to student success. A guide to writing this statement can be found at\nSJSU Institutional Excellence Statement Guidelines\n.\nRequired Qualifications\nA doctorate from an accredited university. A doctoral degree must be fully awarded by the date of appointment in order to start, but preferably by May 31, 2027.\nEvidence of teaching excellence or potential for teaching excellence.\nDemonstrated record of, or potential for, scholarly publications and the development of a strong scholarly record commensurate with rank and suitable for advancement.\nInterest in engaging with Silicon Valley professionals and connecting our students – largely first generation, economically diverse, and multicultural – to the business community.\nEvidence of awareness and sensitivity to the educational goals of a socially and economically diverse student population as might have been gained in cross-cultural study, training, teaching, and other comparable experience.\nPreferred Qualifications\nCandidates whose research focus is AI marketing will be given priority.\nKey Responsibilities\nTeaching Marketing courses with an emphasis on AI and Automation – for example, AI-Based Marketing, Marketing Automation, Digital Marketing with AI, AI-Driven Customer Insights, AI in Marketing Analytics, and AI applications in Marketing. Teaching assignments are at both the undergraduate and graduate levels in traditional and accelerated formats.\nEngaging in scholarly activities as evidenced by developing and maintaining an active research agenda in peer reviewed high-quality journals and by presenting at academic and professional conferences.\nThe candidate will participate in shared governance, usually in department, college, and university committees and other service assignments.\nFaculty shall organize all their classes within the Canvas Learning Management System (LMS).\nThe candidate must demonstrate awareness and experience in understanding the needs of a student population of great diversity – in age, abilities, cultural background, ethnicity, religion, economic background, primary language, sexual orientation, gender identity, and academic preparation – through inclusive course materials, teaching strategies, and advisement.\nOther Duties\nNote that all San José State University employees are considered mandated reporters under the California Child Abuse and Neglect Reporting Act and are required to comply with the requirements set forth in CSU Executive Order 1083 as a condition of employment. Pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act) and CSU systemwide policy, some faculty duties may lead to designation as a Campus Security Authority (CSA). CSAs are required to complete Clery Act training and to immediately report Clery incidents to the institution.\nFaculty employees must complete CSU employee training as assigned and required based on their role (e.g., Data Security, FERPA, Preventing Discrimination and Harassment, Title IX, Health and Safety). The President may recommend or require compliance with safety measures that decrease the likelihood of COVID-19 transmission or illness and allows the core mission and activities of the campus to continue.\nApplication Procedure\nClick Apply Now to complete the SJSU Online Employment Application and attach the following documents:\nletter of interest\ncurriculum vitae\ninstitutional excellence statement\n(dedicated statement or addressed in letter of interest)\nstatement of teaching interests/philosophy (please list the courses you have taught, and/or would like to teach).\nstatement of research agenda and future plans (one page)\nthree reference letters with contact information\nInquiries may be directed to the Department Chair, Dr. David Czerwinski, david.czerwinski@sjsu.edu\n.\nPursuant to California Education Code 89521, applicants will be required to disclose whether they have received a final administrative decision or final judicial decision determining that they have committed sexual harassment within the last 7 years only after a determination is made that they meet the minimum qualifications for the position, and before an offer of employment is extended.  Applicants who reach the final stages of the application process must also sign a release form that authorizes the release of information by the applicant’s current and/or former employers to the CSU concerning any substantiated allegations of misconduct.\nConditional Offer\nThe work for this faculty position may only be performed in the State of California and requires establishing an on-campus presence. Employment is contingent upon US residence and proof of eligibility to work in the United States. Satisfactory completion of background and reference checks (including criminal records and prior employment) are required. SJSU will make a conditional offer of employment, which may be rescinded if the background and reference checks reveal disqualifying information, and/or it is discovered that the candidate knowingly withheld or falsified information. Failure to satisfactorily complete the background and reference checks may affect the continued employment of a current employee who was conditionally offered the position.\nSan José State University: Silicon Valley's Public University\nLocated in the heart of Silicon Valley — one of the most innovative regions in the world —\nSan José State University\nis the founding campus of the 23-campus California State University (CSU) system and the first public university in the West. Recognized as a leading transformative educational institution, San José State is an essential partner in the technological, economic, cultural, and social development of Silicon Valley, the Bay Area, and California. SJSU is a top-200 school nationally in research funding and second highest in research productivity in the CSU system. Cutting-edge research, world-class scholarship, student-centered learning, diverse communities, and commitment to social justice, allow SJSU to provide life-changing opportunities and advance the public good locally and globally.\nEqual Employment Statement\nSan José State University prohibits discrimination on the basis of Age, Ancestry, Caste, Color, Disability, Ethnicity, Gender, Gender Expression, Gender Identity, Genetic Information, Marital Status, Medical Condition, Military Status, Nationality, Race, Religion, Religious Creed, Sex, Sexual Orientation, Sex Stereotype, and Veteran Status. This policy applies to all San José State University students, faculty, and staff as well as University programs and activities. Reasonable accommodations are made for applicants with disabilities who self-disclose.\nCampus Security and Fire Safety Notification\nPursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, the Annual Security Report (ASR) is also now available for viewing at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Security-Report.pdf\n. The ASR contains the current security and safety-related policy statements, emergency preparedness and evacuation information, crime prevention and Sexual Assault prevention information, and information about drug and alcohol prevention programming. The ASR also contains statistics of Clery crimes for San José State University locations for the three most recent calendar years. A paper copy of the ASR is available upon request by contacting the Office of the Clery Director by phone at 408-924-1501 or by email at clerycompliance@sjsu.edu.\nPursuant to the Higher Education Opportunity Act, the Annual Fire Safety Report (AFSR) is available at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Fire-Safety-Report.pdf\n. The purpose of this report is to disclose statistics for fires that occurred within SJSU on-campus housing facilities for the three most recent calendar years, and to distribute fire safety policies and procedures intended to promote safety on Campus. A paper copy of the AFSR is available upon request by contacting the Housing Office by phone at 408-795-5600 or by email at uhs-frontdesk@sjsu.edu."
    },
    "https://jobs.sjsu.edu/en-us/job/562131/assistant-professor-school-of-information-systems-technology": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, School of Information Systems & Technology",
      "description": "Department: School of Information Systems & Technology (SISTech)\nPosition Rank and Title:\nAssistant Professor\nSchool/Department Name:\nSchool of Information Systems & Technology (SISTech)\nCompensation:\nCommensurate with qualiﬁcations, experience, and rank as established by the\nCSU Salary\nSchedule\n. Anticipated hiring academic year annual salary range: $134,520-$ 141,246\nFaculty employee beneﬁts are very competitive for the higher education sector. See the\nCSU Employee\nBeneﬁts Summary\nfor details about the CSU’s excellent beneﬁts package.\nTarget Start Date:\nAugust 17, 2027\nApplication Deadline:\nOctober 1, 2026 (Applications accepted until ﬁlled or closed)\nPosition Description\nThe School of Information Systems & Technology at San José State University invites applications for an Assistant Professor of Information Systems position to begin August 2027. The successful candidate will be able to teach in our MIS curriculum which includes traditional core courses such as Systems Analysis & Design, Fundamentals of MIS, and Business Programming, as well as electives in emerging topics such as Data Analytics & Visualization and Artiﬁcial Intelligence (AI). They will also be able to engage actively in research on IS-related topics, demonstrated by conference presentations and peer-reviewed journal publications, and will be expected to support the school, the college, and the university through service activities.\nSISTech resides in the Lucas College and Graduate School of Business at SJSU. We prepare students to excel professionally and contribute to society through the undergraduate MIS concentration within the Bachelor of Science in Business Administration degree. Our program reﬂects IS best practices and provides students with the skills and hands-on experience they will need to succeed today and tomorrow. Our location in Silicon Valley provides both students and faculty with unparalleled opportunities to engage with leading technology ﬁrms. Our diverse school includes tenured / tenure track faculty who contribute meaningfully to academic literature and clinical faculty with years of experience at leading organizations in Silicon Valley. Consequently, our students are well-represented throughout the high-tech and non-tech industries locally and beyond. To learn more about our school, programs, and faculty, please visit our\nSISTech website\n.\nSISTech and San José State University value institutional excellence and are committed to advancing equity through research, teaching, and service. We enroll more than 40,000 students, many of whom are historically underserved; approximately 45% of our undergraduate students are ﬁrst-generation college students and 38% are Pell Grant recipients. In addition, we are both a Hispanic Serving Institution (HSI) and an Asian American and Native American Paciﬁc Islander Serving Institution (AANAPISI). SJSU aspires to create holistic learning environments that facilitate both individual and collective transformation. We seek teacher-scholars who cultivate inclusive, empowering spaces for mutual learning, skill-building, and knowledge exchange for all students. Our commitment to lifelong learning supports meaningful connections among faculty, staﬀ, students, alumni, and the broader communities we serve.\nWe invite all applicants to include a Statement of Institutional Excellence (or incorporate it into your cover letter) to share how your lived and professional experiences will contribute to the SJSU community—particularly in relation to student success. A guide to writing this statement can be found at\nSJSU Institutional Excellence Statement Guidelines\n.\nRequired Qualiﬁcations\nA PhD in Management Information Systems, Information Science, Computer Information Systems, Computer Science, or related ﬁeld, from an appropriately accredited institution\nApplicant must be qualiﬁed as a scholarly academic (SA) according to AACSB standards\nApplicants should demonstrate an awareness of and sensitivity to the educational goals of a socially and economically diverse student population as might have been gained in cross-cultural study, training, teaching, and other comparable experience.\nPreferred Qualiﬁcations\nPriority will be given to candidates who possess one or more of the following:\nAbility to teach a variety of traditional IS courses\nExpertise in Python, R, Data Analytics / Visualization, Project Management or Artiﬁcial Intelligence (AI)\nCollegial, cooperative and collaborative work style\nStrong teamwork, communication, interpersonal skills\nDemonstrated record of teaching, research, and service excellence\nKey Responsibilities\nMaintain ongoing research activity providing valued contributions relevant to the IS academic community, including peer-reviewed journal papers and conference proceedings.\nDeliver engaging, relevant, and timely learning experiences that contribute meaningfully to our students' professional readiness for high-impact careers in IS.\nParticipate in shared governance, usually in department, college, and university committees and other service assignments.\nOrganize classes within the university policy guidelines and use the Canvas Learning Management System (LMS).\nDemonstrate awareness and experience in understanding the needs of a student population of great diversity – in age, abilities, cultural background, ethnicity, religion, economic background, primary language, sexual orientation, gender identity, and academic preparation – through inclusive course materials, teaching strategies, and advisement.\nOther Duties\nNote that all San José State University employees are considered mandated reporters under the California Child Abuse and Neglect Reporting Act and are required to comply with the requirements set forth in CSU Executive Order 1083 as a condition of employment. Pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act) and CSU systemwide policy, some faculty duties may lead to designation as a Campus Security Authority (CSA). CSAs are required to complete Clery Act training and to immediately report Clery incidents to the institution.\nFaculty employees must complete CSU employee training as assigned and required based on their role (e.g., Data Security, FERPA, Preventing Discrimination and Harassment, Title IX, Health and Safety). The President may recommend or require compliance with safety measures that decrease the likelihood of COVID-19 transmission or illness and allows the core mission and activities of the campus to continue.\nApplication Procedure\nClick Apply Now to complete the SJSU Online Employment Application and attach the following documents [\ndepartments may specify length of items below (e.g. 2 pages)\n]:\nletter of interest\ncurriculum vitae\ninstitutional excellence statement\n(dedicated statement or addressed in letter of interest)\nstatement of teaching interests/philosophy\nresearch plans\nthree reference letters\nInquiries may be directed to the Department Chair or Search Committee Chair: Dr. Timothy Hill\ntimothy.hill@sjsu.edu\n.\nPursuant to California Education Code 89521, applicants will be required to disclose whether they have received a ﬁnal administrative decision or ﬁnal judicial decision determining that they have committed sexual harassment within the last 7 years only after a determination is made that they meet the minimum qualiﬁcations for the position, and before an oﬀer of employment is extended. Applicants who reach the ﬁnal stages of the application process must also sign a release form that authorizes the release of information by the applicant’s current and/or former employers to the CSU concerning any substantiated allegations of misconduct.\nConditional Oﬀer\nThe work for this faculty position may only be performed in the State of California and requires establishing an on-campus presence. Employment is contingent upon US residence and proof of eligibility to work in the United States. Satisfactory completion of background and reference checks (including criminal records and prior employment) are required. SJSU will make a conditional oﬀer of employment, which may be rescinded if the background and reference checks reveal disqualifying information, and/or it is discovered that the candidate knowingly withheld or falsiﬁed information. Failure to satisfactorily complete the background and reference checks may aﬀect the continued employment of a current employee who was conditionally oﬀered the position.\nSan José State University: Silicon Valley's Public University\nLocated in the heart of Silicon Valley — one of the most innovative regions in the world —\nSan José State\nUniversity\nis the founding campus of the 23-campus California State University (CSU) system and the ﬁrst public university in the West. Recognized as a leading transformative educational institution, San José State is an essential partner in the technological, economic, cultural, and social development of Silicon Valley, the Bay Area, and California. SJSU is a top-200 school nationally in research funding and second highest in research productivity in the CSU system. Cutting-edge research, world-class scholarship, student-centered learning, diverse communities, and commitment to social justice, allow SJSU to provide life-changing opportunities and advance the public good locally and globally.\nEqual Employment Statement\nSan José State University prohibits discrimination on the basis of Age, Ancestry, Caste, Color, Disability, Ethnicity, Gender, Gender Expression, Gender Identity, Genetic Information, Marital Status, Medical Condition, Military Status, Nationality, Race, Religion, Religious Creed, Sex, Sexual Orientation, Sex Stereotype, and Veteran Status. This policy applies to all San José State University students, faculty, and staﬀ as well as University programs and activities. Reasonable accommodations are made for applicants with disabilities who self-disclose.\nCampus Security and Fire Safety Notiﬁcation\nPursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, the Annual Security Report (ASR) is also now available for viewing at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Security-Report.pdf\n. The ASR contains the current security and safety-related policy statements, emergency preparedness and evacuation information, crime prevention and Sexual Assault prevention information, and information about drug and alcohol prevention programming. The ASR also contains statistics of Clery crimes for San José State University locations for the three most recent calendar years. A paper copy of the ASR is available upon request by contacting the Oﬃce of the Clery Director by phone at 408-924-1501 or by email at\nclerycompliance@sjsu.edu.\nPursuant to the Higher Education Opportunity Act, the Annual Fire Safety Report (AFSR) is available at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Fire-Safety-Report.pdf\n. The purpose of this report is to disclose statistics for ﬁres that occurred within SJSU on-campus housing facilities for the three most recent calendar years, and to distribute ﬁre safety policies and procedures intended to promote safety on Campus. A paper copy of the AFSR is available upon request by contacting the Housing Oﬃce by phone at 408-795-5600 or by email at\nuhs-frontdesk@sjsu.edu."
    },
    "https://jobs.sjsu.edu/en-us/job/562135/assistant-professor-of-mathematics-discrete-mathematicsgraph-theory": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Mathematics - Discrete Mathematics/Graph Theory",
      "description": "Department: Department of Mathematics and Statistics\nPosition Rank and Title:\nTenure-track assistant professor\nSchool/Department Name:\nDepartment of Mathematics and Statistics\nCompensation:\nCommensurate with qualifications, experience, and rank as established by the\nCSU Salary Schedule\n. Anticipated hiring academic year annual salary range: $104,500-$107,000.\nFaculty employee benefits are very competitive for the higher education sector.  See the\nCSU Employee Benefits Summary\nfor details about the CSU’s excellent benefits package.\nStartup allowance:\n$30,000\nTarget Start Date:\nAugust 17, 2027\nApplication Deadline:\nNovember 6, 2026. Applications will be accepted until the position is filled or closed.\nPosition Description\nThe Department of Mathematics and Statistics at San José State University is pleased to announce a search for one tenure-track faculty position in Discrete Mathematics/Graph Theory at the rank of Assistant Professor beginning in August 2027. We seek applicants with demonstrated evidence of excellence in teaching, outstanding research potential and potential for securing grants related to discrete mathematics/graph theory, and a commitment to department service. Applicants are required to have a doctoral degree in mathematics or a closely related field by the time of appointment.\nOur department has 28 Tenure/Tenure Track faculty, with research interests across Pure Mathematics, Applied Mathematics, Mathematics Education, Statistics, and Data Science.  Our department research culture has developed and grown significantly in recent years, with a strong majority of faculty hired in the last ten years being not just research active, but also receiving significant amounts of external funding, not just from NSF, but also from industry (e.g., Ethereum Foundation, Applied Materials) and governmental agencies (e.g., Wildfire Interdisciplinary Research Center, Office of the Governor of California).  Departmental pedagogy has advanced significantly in recent years, with many faculty members embracing student-centered approaches like team-based and activity-based learning. Initiatives from younger department members have also greatly enhanced departmental community among both students and faculty. The department and the College of Science are also supportive of assigned time (relief from teaching) and sabbatical leaves. Assigned time is regularly awarded to recognize work including faculty research, faculty support of student research, and course coordination, and at least 8 sabbaticals and 3 parental leaves have been taken in the department since 2023.\nThe Department of Mathematics and Statistics and San José State University value institutional excellence and are committed to advancing equity through research, teaching, and service. We enroll more than 40,000 students, many of whom are historically underserved; approximately 45% of our undergraduate students are first-generation college students and 38% are Pell Grant recipients. In addition, we are both a Hispanic Serving Institution (HSI) and an Asian American and Native American Pacific Islander Serving Institution (AANAPISI). SJSU aspires to create holistic learning environments that facilitate both individual and collective transformation. We seek teacher-scholars who cultivate inclusive, empowering spaces for mutual learning, skill-building, and knowledge exchange for all students. Our commitment to lifelong learning supports meaningful connections among faculty, staff, students, alumni, and the broader communities we serve.\nWe invite all applicants to include a Statement of Institutional Excellence (or incorporate it into your cover letter) to share how your lived and professional experiences will contribute to the SJSU community—particularly in relation to student success. A guide to writing this statement can be found at\nSJSU Institutional Excellence Statement Guidelines\n.\nRequired Qualifications\nPh.D. in mathematics or a closely related field\nEither a clear and well-defined research agenda or a beginning record of high-impact research, scholarly publications, or external research funding in analysis\nEvidence of teaching excellence as demonstrated by, for example, successful engagement with high-impact teaching practices like active learning, or documented positive impacts for student success, as shown by teaching awards or other significant recognition\nApplicants must demonstrate an awareness of and sensitivity to the educational goals of a socially and economically diverse student population as might have been gained in cross-cultural study, training, teaching, and other comparable experience\nPreferred Qualifications\nPriority will be given to candidates who possess one or more of the following:\nSuccess in publishing and disseminating research in discrete mathematics/graph theory\nExperience with, or demonstrated potential for, teaching undergraduate and graduate classes in discrete mathematics/graph theory\nExperience with, or commitment to, student-centered pedagogy and active learning\nExperience in mentoring undergraduate and graduate student research in mathematics\nUse of formal verification tools (e.g. Lean, Roq) in mathematics research\nKey Responsibilities\nThe candidate is expected to teach a variety of classes, including service and lower division courses, as well as upper division courses and graduate courses, particularly in discrete mathematics or graph theory.\nThe candidate is expected to conduct research and secure grants to support research in discrete mathematics/graph theory.\nThe candidate is expected to supervise SJSU students in research projects and Master’s theses in mathematics.\nThe candidate will participate in shared governance, usually in department, college, and university committees and other service assignments.\nFaculty shall organize all their classes within the Canvas Learning Management System (LMS).\nThe candidate must demonstrate awareness and experience in understanding the needs of a student population of great diversity – in age, abilities, cultural background, ethnicity, religion, economic background, primary language, sexual orientation, gender identity, and academic preparation – through inclusive course materials, teaching strategies, and advisement.\nOther Duties\nNote that all San José State University employees are considered mandated reporters under the California Child Abuse and Neglect Reporting Act and are required to comply with the requirements set forth in CSU Executive Order 1083 as a condition of employment. Pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act) and CSU systemwide policy, some faculty duties may lead to designation as a Campus Security Authority (CSA). CSAs are required to complete Clery Act training and to immediately report Clery incidents to the institution.\nFaculty employees must complete CSU employee training as assigned and required based on their role (e.g., Data Security, FERPA, Preventing Discrimination and Harassment, Title IX, Health and Safety). The President may recommend or require compliance with safety measures that decrease the likelihood of COVID-19 transmission or illness and allows the core mission and activities of the campus to continue.\nApplication Procedure\nClick Apply Now to complete the SJSU Online Employment Application and attach the following documents:\nletter of interest\ncurriculum vitae\ninstitutional excellence statement\n(dedicated statement or addressed in letter of interest)\nstatement of teaching interests/philosophy\nresearch plans\nthree references with contact information\nInquiries may be directed to the Search Committee Chair: Tim Hsu, tim.hsu@sjsu.edu\nPursuant to California Education Code 89521, applicants will be required to disclose whether they have received a final administrative decision or final judicial decision determining that they have committed sexual harassment within the last 7 years only after a determination is made that they meet the minimum qualifications for the position, and before an offer of employment is extended.  Applicants who reach the final stages of the application process must also sign a release form that authorizes the release of information by the applicant’s current and/or former employers to the CSU concerning any substantiated allegations of misconduct.\nConditional Offer\nThe work for this faculty position may only be performed in the State of California and requires establishing an on-campus presence.  Employment is contingent upon US residence and proof of eligibility to work in the United States. Satisfactory completion of background and reference checks (including criminal records and prior employment) are required. SJSU will make a conditional offer of employment, which may be rescinded if the background and reference checks reveal disqualifying information, and/or it is discovered that the candidate knowingly withheld or falsified information. Failure to satisfactorily complete the background and reference checks may affect the continued employment of a current employee who was conditionally offered the position.\nSan José State University: Silicon Valley's Public University\nLocated in the heart of Silicon Valley — one of the most innovative regions in the world —\nSan José State University\nis the founding campus of the 23-campus California State University (CSU) system and the first public university in the West. Recognized as a leading transformative educational institution, San José State is an essential partner in the technological, economic, cultural, and social development of Silicon Valley, the Bay Area, and California. SJSU is a top-200 school nationally in research funding and second highest in research productivity in the CSU system. Cutting-edge research, world-class scholarship, student-centered learning, diverse communities, and commitment to social justice, allow SJSU to provide life-changing opportunities and advance the public good locally and globally.\nEqual Employment Statement\nSan José State University prohibits discrimination on the basis of Age, Ancestry, Caste, Color, Disability, Ethnicity, Gender, Gender Expression, Gender Identity, Genetic Information, Marital Status, Medical Condition, Military Status, Nationality, Race, Religion, Religious Creed, Sex, Sexual Orientation, Sex Stereotype, and Veteran Status. This policy applies to all San José State University students, faculty, and staff as well as University programs and activities. Reasonable accommodations are made for applicants with disabilities who self-disclose.\nCampus Security and Fire Safety Notification\nPursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, the Annual Security Report (ASR) is also now available for viewing at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Security-Report.pdf\n. The ASR contains the current security and safety-related policy statements, emergency preparedness and evacuation information, crime prevention and Sexual Assault prevention information, and information about drug and alcohol prevention programming. The ASR also contains statistics of Clery crimes for San José State University locations for the three most recent calendar years. A paper copy of the ASR is available upon request by contacting the Office of the Clery Director by phone at 408-924-1501 or by email at clerycompliance@sjsu.edu.\nPursuant to the Higher Education Opportunity Act, the Annual Fire Safety Report (AFSR) is available at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Fire-Safety-Report.pdf\n. The purpose of this report is to disclose statistics for fires that occurred within SJSU on-campus housing facilities for the three most recent calendar years, and to distribute fire safety policies and procedures intended to promote safety on Campus. A paper copy of the AFSR is available upon request by contacting the Housing Office by phone at 408-795-5600 or by email at uhs-frontdesk@sjsu.edu."
    },
    "https://jobs.sjsu.edu/en-us/job/562138/assistant-professor-of-special-education": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Special Education",
      "description": "Department: Lurie College of Education / Department of Special Education\nPosition Rank and Title:\nAssistant Professor of Special Education\nSchool/Department Name:\nLurie College of Education / Department of Special Education\nCompensation:\nCommensurate with qualifications, experience, and rank as established by the\nCSU Salary Schedule\n. Anticipated hiring academic year annual salary range: $95,928-97,224\nFaculty employee benefits are very competitive for the higher education sector.  See the\nCSU Employee Benefits Summary\nfor details about the CSU’s excellent benefits package.\nTarget Start Date:\nAugust 17, 2027\nApplication Deadline:\nDecember 20, 2026 (Applications accepted until filled or closed)\nPosition Description\nThe Department of Special Education at San José State University is pleased to announce a search for an Assistant Professor of Special Education beginning in August 2027. We seek candidates who possess strong potential for scholarly research, writing, and teaching in the Master of Arts (MA) in Special Education and Education Specialist Credential programs across undergraduate, credential, and MA degree pathways. Candidates with expertise in working with students who are bilingual and from diverse and underrepresented backgrounds, and who have Mild to Moderate disabilities, are especially welcome to apply.\nThe Department of Special Education within the Lurie College of Education at SJSU takes pride in preparing transformative special educators who possess the knowledge and skills to meet the intersectional identities of all learners and work as change agents to create equitable and inclusive environments in schools. The Department offers a Master’s in Special Education program and three Education Specialist credential programs accredited by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing. The department prepares candidates to work in over 40 school districts across the diverse communities of the greater San Jose area, including satellite programs in the Tri-Valley area and Santa Cruz County. The department has developed strong collaborative relationships with many Local Education Agencies and operates three Teacher Residency Programs.  In addition, the department partners with the Liberal Studies, Child and Adolescent Development, and Math departments to run five-year blended BA to MA programs that include the Education Specialist credential. Applicants can learn more about the Department of Special Education by previewing the program website at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/specialed/\nThe Department of Special Education and San José State University value institutional excellence and are committed to advancing equity through research, teaching, and service. We enroll more than 40,000 students, many of whom are historically underserved; approximately 45% of our undergraduate students are first-generation college students and 38% are Pell Grant recipients. In addition, we are both a Hispanic Serving Institution (HSI) and an Asian American and Native American Pacific Islander Serving Institution (AANAPISI). SJSU aspires to create holistic learning environments that facilitate both individual and collective transformation. We seek teacher-scholars who cultivate inclusive, empowering spaces for mutual learning, skill-building, and knowledge exchange for all students. Our commitment to lifelong learning supports meaningful connections among faculty, staff, students, alumni, and the broader communities we serve.\nWe invite all applicants to include a Statement of Institutional Excellence (or incorporate it into your cover letter) to share how your lived and professional experiences will contribute to the SJSU community—particularly in relation to student success. A guide to writing this statement can be found at\nSJSU Institutional Excellence Statement Guidelines\n.\nRequired Qualifications\nPhD or EdD in Special Education or related field\nDemonstrated potential for research and scholarly writing\nApplicants should demonstrate an awareness of and sensitivity to the educational goals of a socially and economically diverse student population as might have been gained in cross-cultural study, training, teaching, and other comparable experience.\nPreferred Qualifications\nPriority will be given to candidates who possess one or more of the following:\nMinimum of three years of public school teaching experience with bilingual students with mild to moderate disabilities in K-12 settings\nAbility and interest in supporting teacher candidates earning a bilingual authorization, including field supervision in bilingual settings\nAbility and interest in teaching and/or advising dissertations for doctoral students in Lurie College’s EdD program.\nStrong potential for research and scholarly work in the field of bilingual special education, as evidenced by one or more of the following: publications in professional journals, grant proposals, or conference presentations\nKey Responsibilities\nThe candidate will teach a range of courses in the Department of Special Education, which may include any combination of Master’s, credential, and/or undergraduate students.\nThe candidate will maintain an active scholarly agenda as necessary for tenure and promotion.\nThe candidate will provide service to the Department of Special Education through a variety of tasks as deemed necessary for the department’s operation, such as advising students, curriculum development, assessment and accreditation tasks, and recruitment activities. In particular, the candidate will guide advisement for the Department’s bilingual authorization program.\nThe candidate will participate in shared governance, usually in department, college, and university committees and other service assignments.\nFaculty shall organize all their classes within the Canvas Learning Management System (LMS).\nThe candidate must demonstrate awareness and experience in understanding the needs of a student population of great diversity – in age, abilities, cultural background, ethnicity, religion, economic background, primary language, sexual orientation, gender identity, and academic preparation – through inclusive course materials, teaching strategies, and advisement.\nOther Duties\nNote that all San José State University employees are considered mandated reporters under the California Child Abuse and Neglect Reporting Act and are required to comply with the requirements set forth in CSU Executive Order 1083 as a condition of employment. Pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act) and CSU systemwide policy, some faculty duties may lead to designation as a Campus Security Authority (CSA). CSAs are required to complete Clery Act training and to immediately report Clery incidents to the institution.\nFaculty employees must complete CSU employee training as assigned and required based on their role (e.g., Data Security, FERPA, Preventing Discrimination and Harassment, Title IX, Health and Safety). The President may recommend or require compliance with safety measures that decrease the likelihood of COVID-19 transmission or illness and allows the core mission and activities of the campus to continue.\nApplication Procedure\nClick Apply Now to complete the SJSU Online Employment Application and attach the following documents [\ndepartments may specify length of items below (e.g. 2 pages)\n]:\nLetter of interest\nCurriculum vitae\nI\nnstitutional excellence statement\n(dedicated statement or addressed in letter of interest)\nStatement of teaching interests/philosophy\nResearch plans\nList of three references with contact information (Letters of recommendation may be requested at a later date.)\nInquiries may be directed to the Committee Chair: Dr. Andrea Golloher\nandrea.golloher@sjsu.edu\nPursuant to California Education Code 89521, applicants will be required to disclose whether they have received a final administrative decision or final judicial decision determining that they have committed sexual harassment within the last 7 years only after a determination is made that they meet the minimum qualifications for the position, and before an offer of employment is extended.  Applicants who reach the final stages of the application process must also sign a release form that authorizes the release of information by the applicant’s current and/or former employers to the CSU concerning any substantiated allegations of misconduct.\nConditional Offer\nThe work for this faculty position may only be performed in the State of California and requires establishing an on-campus presence. Employment is contingent upon US residence and proof of eligibility to work in the United States. Satisfactory completion of background and reference checks (including criminal records and prior employment) are required. SJSU will make a conditional offer of employment, which may be rescinded if the background and reference checks reveal disqualifying information, and/or it is discovered that the candidate knowingly withheld or falsified information. Failure to satisfactorily complete the background and reference checks may affect the continued employment of a current employee who was conditionally offered the position.\nSan José State University: Silicon Valley's Public University\nLocated in the heart of Silicon Valley — one of the most innovative regions in the world —\nSan José State University\nis the founding campus of the 23-campus California State University (CSU) system and the first public university in the West. Recognized as a leading transformative educational institution, San José State is an essential partner in the technological, economic, cultural, and social development of Silicon Valley, the Bay Area, and California. SJSU is a top-200 school nationally in research funding and second highest in research productivity in the CSU system. Cutting-edge research, world-class scholarship, student-centered learning, diverse communities, and commitment to social justice, allow SJSU to provide life-changing opportunities and advance the public good locally and globally.\nEqual Employment Statement\nSan José State University prohibits discrimination on the basis of Age, Ancestry, Caste, Color, Disability, Ethnicity, Gender, Gender Expression, Gender Identity, Genetic Information, Marital Status, Medical Condition, Military Status, Nationality, Race, Religion, Religious Creed, Sex, Sexual Orientation, Sex Stereotype, and Veteran Status. This policy applies to all San José State University students, faculty, and staff as well as University programs and activities. Reasonable accommodations are made for applicants with disabilities who self-disclose.\nCampus Security and Fire Safety Notification\nPursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, the Annual Security Report (ASR) is also now available for viewing at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Security-Report.pdf\n. The ASR contains the current security and safety-related policy statements, emergency preparedness and evacuation information, crime prevention and Sexual Assault prevention information, and information about drug and alcohol prevention programming. The ASR also contains statistics of Clery crimes for San José State University locations for the three most recent calendar years. A paper copy of the ASR is available upon request by contacting the Office of the Clery Director by phone at 408-924-1501 or by email at clerycompliance@sjsu.edu.\nPursuant to the Higher Education Opportunity Act, the Annual Fire Safety Report (AFSR) is available at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Fire-Safety-Report.pdf\n. The purpose of this report is to disclose statistics for fires that occurred within SJSU on-campus housing facilities for the three most recent calendar years, and to distribute fire safety policies and procedures intended to promote safety on Campus. A paper copy of the AFSR is available upon request by contacting the Housing Office by phone at 408-795-5600 or by email at uhs-frontdesk@sjsu.edu."
    },
    "https://jobs.sjsu.edu/en-us/job/561778/assistant-professor-in-mechanical-engineering-wildfire-science-and-engineering": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Mechanical Engineering - Wildfire Science and Engineering",
      "description": "Department: Mechanical Engineering\nPosition Rank and Title:\nAssistant Professor - Wildfire Science and Engineering\nSchool/Department Name: Mechanical Engineering\nCompensation:\nCommensurate with qualifications, experience, and rank as established by the\nCSU Salary\nSchedule.\nAnticipated hiring academic year annual salary range: $ 101k - $ 124k\nFaculty employee benefits are very competitive for the higher education sector. See the\nCSU Employee\nBenefits Summary\nfor details about the CSU’s excellent benefits package.\nTarget Start Date:\nAugust 1st, 2027\nApplication Deadline:\nNovember 1st, 2026\n(Applications accepted until filled or closed)\nPosition Description\nThe Mechanical Engineering Department at San José State University is pleased to announce a search for an assistant professor (tenure-track) with expertise in wildfire science research and teaching potential in thermo-fluids beginning in August 2027. We seek candidates with a strong engineering, computational, or physical-science background whose research directly addresses problems in wildfire or fire science and engineering A key aspect of this position is that the faculty member will actively collaborate with the SJSU Wildfire Interdisciplinary Research Center\n(WIRC,\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/wildfire/,\na National Science Foundation, Industry-University Cooperative Research Center) to seek funding and conduct research related to wildfire science and management. The Center is dedicated to developing strategies, technologies, and science-based approaches to improve wildfire science and management. Specific areas of research interest include, but are not limited to: fire and fluid dynamics; coupled fire-atmosphere modeling; wildfire behavior and spread; wildland-urban interface fire; combustion and heat transfer in wildland and urban fuels; computational fluid dynamics and multiphysics modeling; high-performance scientific computing; data assimilation; scientific machine learning and AI applied to wildfire prediction; smoke and fire-atmosphere interactions; and experimental or observational approaches supporting wildfire model development and validation.\nThe successful candidate will join SJSU's Wildfire Interdisciplinary Research Center (WIRC), an NSF Industry–University Cooperative Research Center that brings together expertise in wildfire dynamics, fire weather, fire ecology, remote sensing, modeling, and related disciplines. WIRC offers extensive observational and computational capabilities, as well as access to operational systems for fire danger, fuel moisture, fire spread, and smoke prediction. Strong partnerships with industry, government agencies, utilities, and other wildfire stakeholders provide opportunities to connect fundamental research with operational applications. The successful candidate will establish an independent research program that complements and expands these capabilities while leveraging WIRC's interdisciplinary expertise, infrastructure, and external partnerships.\nLocated in the heart of Silicon Valley, SJSU Mechanical Engineering provides an exceptional setting for teaching, research, and collaboration. The department has strong programs in robotics and mechatronics, advanced manufacturing, thermal and energy systems, and mechanical design, supported by hands-on laboratories and interdisciplinary research. Its proximity and connections to Silicon Valley's technology, semiconductor, manufacturing, and emerging engineering industries create opportunities for faculty to develop industry partnerships, pursue applied research, and bring real-world engineering challenges into the classroom.\nThe Mechanical Engineering Department and San José State University value institutional excellence and are committed to advancing equity through research, teaching, and service. We enroll more than 40,000 students, many of whom are historically underserved; approximately 45% of our undergraduate students are first-generation college students and 38% are Pell Grant recipients. In addition, we are both a Hispanic Serving Institution (HSI) and an Asian American and Native American Pacific Islander Serving Institution (AANAPISI). SJSU aspires to create holistic learning environments that facilitate both individual and collective transformation. We seek teacher-scholars who cultivate inclusive, empowering spaces for mutual learning, skill-building, and knowledge exchange for all students. Our commitment to lifelong learning supports meaningful connections among faculty, staff, students, alumni, and the broader communities we serve.\nWe invite all applicants to include a Statement of Institutional Excellence (or incorporate it into your cover letter) to share how your lived and professional experiences will contribute to the SJSU community—particularly in relation to student success. A guide to writing this statement can be found at\nSJSU\nInstitutional Excellence Statement Guidelines.\nRequired Qualifications\nPhD in Mechanical Engineering or closely related engineering, computational, or physical-science discipline by the time of appointment.\nAbility to teach & develop courses in the thermo-fluids area for Mechanical Engineering students at both undergraduate and graduate levels.\nDemonstrated research experience directly relevant to wildfire or fire science and engineering.\nApplicants should demonstrate an awareness of and sensitivity to the educational goals of a socially and economically diverse student population as might have been gained in cross-cultural study, training, teaching, and other comparable experience.\nPreferred Qualifications\nPriority will be given to candidates who possess one or more of the following:\nStrong track record of publication.\nExperience and/or demonstrated interest in teaching and pedagogical methods.\nExperience/exposure to the funding acquisition process.\nDemonstrated interest in establishing industrial partnership in the Silicon Valley area.\nProject supervision experience with undergraduate or masters level students.\nInterdisciplinary project experience in a collaborative environment.\nKey Responsibilities\nThe candidate will teach graduate and undergraduate courses in mechanical engineering and other areas consistent with their professional interests. The instructional responsibilities also include preparation of classes, assessment of course learning outcomes, and development of pedagogical improvements.\nThe candidate will conduct research in their area of expertise, publish results in reputable peer-reviewed journals, generate grants, and supervise student research.\nThe candidate will actively work with other faculty at SJSU Wildfire Interdisciplinary Research Center to seek funding and conduct research related to wildfire science and management.\nThe candidate will participate in shared governance, usually in department, college, and university committees and other service assignments.\nFaculty shall organize all their classes within the Canvas Learning Management System (LMS).\nThe candidate must demonstrate awareness and experience in understanding the needs of a student population of great diversity – in age, abilities, cultural background, ethnicity, religion, economic background, primary language, sexual orientation, gender identity, and academic preparation – through inclusive course materials, teaching strategies, and advisement.\nOther Duties\nNote that all San José State University employees are considered mandated reporters under the California Child Abuse and Neglect Reporting Act and are required to comply with the requirements set forth in CSU Executive Order 1083 as a condition of employment. Pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act) and CSU systemwide policy, some faculty duties may lead to designation as a Campus Security Authority (CSA). CSAs are required to complete Clery Act training and to immediately report Clery incidents to the institution.\nFaculty employees must complete CSU employee training as assigned and required based on their role (e.g., Data Security, FERPA, Preventing Discrimination and Harassment, Title IX, Health and Safety). The President may recommend or require compliance with safety measures that decrease the likelihood of COVID-19 transmission or illness and allows the core mission and activities of the campus to continue.\nApplication Procedure\nClick Apply Now to complete the SJSU Online Employment Application and attach the following documents:\nletter of interest (2 page max)\ncurriculum vitae\ninstitutional excellence statement\n(dedicated statement or addressed in letter of interest, 2 page max)\nstatement of teaching interests/philosophy (2 page max)\nresearch plans (2 page max)\nMinimum of three references with contact information\nInquiries may be directed to the Department Chair or Search Committee Chair: Hohyun Lee,\nhohyun.lee@sjsu.edu\nPursuant to California Education Code 89521, applicants will be required to disclose whether they have received a final administrative decision or final judicial decision determining that they have committed sexual harassment within the last 7 years only after a determination is made that they meet the minimum qualifications for the position, and before an offer of employment is extended. Applicants who reach the final stages of the application process must also sign a release form that authorizes the release of information by the applicant’s current and/or former employers to the CSU concerning any substantiated allegations of misconduct.\nConditional Offer\nThe work for this faculty position may only be performed in the State of California and requires establishing an on-campus presence. Employment is contingent upon US residence and proof of eligibility to work in the United States. Satisfactory completion of background and reference checks (including criminal records and prior employment) are required. SJSU will make a conditional offer of employment, which may be rescinded if the background and reference checks reveal disqualifying information, and/or it is discovered that the candidate knowingly withheld or falsified information. Failure to satisfactorily complete the background and reference checks may affect the continued employment of a current employee who was conditionally offered the position.\nSan José State University: Silicon Valley's Public University\nLocated in the heart of Silicon Valley — one of the most innovative regions in the world —\nSan José State\nUniversity\nis the founding campus of the 23-campus California State University (CSU) system and the first public university in the West. Recognized as a leading transformative educational institution, San José State is an essential partner in the technological, economic, cultural, and social development of Silicon Valley, the Bay Area, and California. SJSU is a top-200 school nationally in research funding and second highest in research productivity in the CSU system. Cutting-edge research, world-class scholarship, student-centered learning, diverse communities, and commitment to social justice, allow SJSU to provide life-changing opportunities and advance the public good locally and globally.\nEqual Employment Statement\nSan José State University prohibits discrimination on the basis of Age, Ancestry, Caste, Color, Disability, Ethnicity, Gender, Gender Expression, Gender Identity, Genetic Information, Marital Status, Medical Condition, Military Status, Nationality, Race, Religion, Religious Creed, Sex, Sexual Orientation, Sex Stereotype, and Veteran Status. This policy applies to all San José State University students, faculty, and staff as well as University programs and activities. Reasonable accommodations are made for applicants with disabilities who self-disclose.\nCampus Security and Fire Safety Notification\nPursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, the Annual Security Report (ASR) is also now available for viewing at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-\nAnnual-Security-Report.pdf.\nThe ASR contains the current security and safety-related policy statements, emergency preparedness and evacuation information, crime prevention and Sexual Assault prevention information, and information about drug and alcohol prevention programming. The ASR also contains statistics of Clery crimes for San José State University locations for the three most recent calendar years. A paper copy of the ASR is available upon request by contacting the Office of the Clery Director by phone at 408-924-1501 or by email at\nclerycompliance@sjsu.edu.\nPursuant to the Higher Education Opportunity Act, the Annual Fire Safety Report (AFSR) is available at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Fire-Safety-Report.pdf.\nThe purpose of this report is to disclose statistics for fires that occurred within SJSU on-campus housing facilities for the three most recent calendar years, and to distribute fire safety policies and procedures intended to promote safety on Campus. A paper copy of the AFSR is available upon request by contacting the Housing Office by phone at 408-795-5600 or by email at\nuhs-frontdesk@sjsu.edu."
    },
    "https://jobs.sjsu.edu/en-us/job/561887/assistant-professor-accounting": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - Accounting",
      "description": "Department: Department of Accounting and Finance\nPosition Rank and Title:\nAssistant Professor in Accounting\nSchool/Department Name:\nDepartment of Accounting and Finance\nCompensation:\nCommensurate with qualifications, experience, and rank as established by the\nCSU Salary Schedule\n. Anticipated hiring academic year annual salary range: $147,636 - $155,376\nFaculty employee benefits are very competitive for the higher education sector.  See the\nCSU Employee Benefits Summary\nfor details about the CSU’s excellent benefits package.\nTarget Start Date:\nAugust 17, 2027\nApplication Deadline:\nOctober 7, 2026 (Applications accepted until filled or closed)\nPosition Description\nThe Department of Accounting & Finance in the Lucas College of Business at San José State University is pleased to announce a search for Assistant Professor of Accounting beginning in August 2027.  The successful candidate will teach broadly in accounting coursework, which may include financial, managerial, analytics, or internal audit courses in our undergraduate, MS Accounting Analytics, or MBA programs. The faculty will engage in research targeted to highly respected accounting journals and in service at department, college, or university levels.\nThe Department of Accounting & Finance teaches undergraduate coursework in finance, accounting, and tax disciplines with a technology flavor and encourages cross-discipline research. It houses both the Masters of Science in Accounting Analytics and the Masters of Science in Financial Analytics degrees. Centers of excellence include the Center for Banking and Financial Services, the Accounting Advancement Center, and the High Technology Tax Institute. For more information, please visit\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/acctfin/\nThe Department of Accounting & Finance and San José State University value institutional excellence and are committed to advancing equity through research, teaching, and service. We enroll more than 40,000 students, many of whom are historically underserved; approximately 45% of our undergraduate students are first-generation college students and 38% are Pell Grant recipients. In addition, we are both a Hispanic Serving Institution (HSI) and an Asian American and Native American Pacific Islander Serving Institution (AANAPISI). SJSU aspires to create holistic learning environments that facilitate both individual and collective transformation. We seek teacher-scholars who cultivate inclusive, empowering spaces for mutual learning, skill-building, and knowledge exchange for all students. Our commitment to lifelong learning supports meaningful connections among faculty, staff, students, alumni, and the broader communities we serve.\nWe invite all applicants to include a Statement of Institutional Excellence (or incorporate it into your cover letter) to share how your lived and professional experiences will contribute to the SJSU community—particularly in relation to student success. A guide to writing this statement can be found at\nSJSU Institutional Excellence Statement Guidelines\n.\nRequired Qualifications\nPh.D. in Accounting by start of appointment\nScholarly Academic qualification under AACSB standards\nExcellent communication and interpersonal skills\nApplicants should demonstrate an awareness of and sensitivity to the educational goals of a socially and economically diverse student population as might have been gained in cross-cultural study, training, teaching, and other comparable experience.\nPreferred Qualifications\nPriority will be given to candidates who possess one or more of the following:\nProfessional experience in corporate accounting or assurance\nProfessional designations such as Certified Public Accountant\nExcellent teaching experience at the university level\nKey Responsibilities\nTeaching assignments are at both the graduate and undergraduate levels in traditional and accelerated formats.\nThe candidate must be actively engaged in scholarly activities conducting research, developing grants, presenting professional papers, and publishing in peer-reviewed journals.\nThe candidate will participate in shared governance, usually in department, college, and university committees and other service assignments.\nFaculty shall organize all their classes within the Canvas Learning Management System (LMS).\nThe candidate must demonstrate awareness and experience in understanding the needs of a student population of great diversity – in age, abilities, cultural background, ethnicity, religion, economic background, primary language, sexual orientation, gender identity, and academic preparation – through inclusive course materials, teaching strategies, and advisement.\nOther Duties\nNote that all San José State University employees are considered mandated reporters under the California Child Abuse and Neglect Reporting Act and are required to comply with the requirements set forth in CSU Executive Order 1083 as a condition of employment. Pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act) and CSU systemwide policy, some faculty duties may lead to designation as a Campus Security Authority (CSA). CSAs are required to complete Clery Act training and to immediately report Clery incidents to the institution.\nFaculty employees must complete CSU employee training as assigned and required based on their role (e.g., Data Security, FERPA, Preventing Discrimination and Harassment, Title IX, Health and Safety). The President may recommend or require compliance with safety measures that decrease the likelihood of COVID-19 transmission or illness and allows the core mission and activities of the campus to continue.\nApplication Procedure\nClick Apply Now to complete the SJSU Online Employment Application and attach the following documents [\ndepartments may specify length of items below (e.g. 2 pages)\n]:\nletter of interest\ncurriculum vitae\ninstitutional excellence statement\n(dedicated statement or addressed in letter of interest)\nstatement of teaching interests/philosophy\nresearch plans\nthree letters of recommendation\nInquiries may be directed to the Department Chair: Prof. Elizabeth Grace\nElizabeth.grace@sjsu.edu\n.\nPursuant to California Education Code 89521, applicants will be required to disclose whether they have received a final administrative decision or final judicial decision determining that they have committed sexual harassment within the last 7 years only after a determination is made that they meet the minimum qualifications for the position, and before an offer of employment is extended.  Applicants who reach the final stages of the application process must also sign a release form that authorizes the release of information by the applicant’s current and/or former employers to the CSU concerning any substantiated allegations of misconduct.\nConditional Offer\nThe work for this faculty position may only be performed in the State of California and requires establishing an on-campus presence.  Employment is contingent upon US residence and proof of eligibility to work in the United States. Satisfactory completion of background and reference checks (including criminal records and prior employment) are required. SJSU will make a conditional offer of employment, which may be rescinded if the background and reference checks reveal disqualifying information, and/or it is discovered that the candidate knowingly withheld or falsified information. Failure to satisfactorily complete the background and reference checks may affect the continued employment of a current employee who was conditionally offered the position.\nSan José State University: Silicon Valley's Public University\nLocated in the heart of Silicon Valley — one of the most innovative regions in the world —\nSan José State University\nis the founding campus of the 23-campus California State University (CSU) system and the first public university in the West. Recognized as a leading transformative educational institution, San José State is an essential partner in the technological, economic, cultural, and social development of Silicon Valley, the Bay Area, and California. SJSU is a top-200 school nationally in research funding and second highest in research productivity in the CSU system. Cutting-edge research, world-class scholarship, student-centered learning, diverse communities, and commitment to social justice, allow SJSU to provide life-changing opportunities and advance the public good locally and globally.\nEqual Employment Statement\nSan José State University prohibits discrimination on the basis of Age, Ancestry, Caste, Color, Disability, Ethnicity, Gender, Gender Expression, Gender Identity, Genetic Information, Marital Status, Medical Condition, Military Status, Nationality, Race, Religion, Religious Creed, Sex, Sexual Orientation, Sex Stereotype, and Veteran Status. This policy applies to all San José State University students, faculty, and staff as well as University programs and activities. Reasonable accommodations are made for applicants with disabilities who self-disclose.\nCampus Security and Fire Safety Notification\nPursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, the Annual Security Report (ASR) is also now available for viewing at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Security-Report.pdf\n. The ASR contains the current security and safety-related policy statements, emergency preparedness and evacuation information, crime prevention and Sexual Assault prevention information, and information about drug and alcohol prevention programming. The ASR also contains statistics of Clery crimes for San José State University locations for the three most recent calendar years. A paper copy of the ASR is available upon request by contacting the Office of the Clery Director by phone at 408-924-1501 or by email at clerycompliance@sjsu.edu.\nPursuant to the Higher Education Opportunity Act, the Annual Fire Safety Report (AFSR) is available at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Fire-Safety-Report.pdf\n. The purpose of this report is to disclose statistics for fires that occurred within SJSU on-campus housing facilities for the three most recent calendar years, and to distribute fire safety policies and procedures intended to promote safety on Campus. A paper copy of the AFSR is available upon request by contacting the Housing Office by phone at 408-795-5600 or by email at uhs-frontdesk@sjsu.edu."
    },
    "https://jobs.sjsu.edu/en-us/job/561888/assistant-professor-finance": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - Finance",
      "description": "Department: Department of Accounting and Finance\nPosition Rank and Title:\nAssistant Professor in Finance\nSchool/Department Name:\nDepartment of Accounting and Finance\nCompensation:\nCommensurate with qualifications, experience, and rank as established by the\nCSU Salary Schedule\n. Anticipated hiring academic year annual salary range: $148,848 - $156,290\nFaculty employee benefits are very competitive for the higher education sector.  See the\nCSU Employee Benefits Summary\nfor details about the CSU’s excellent benefits package.\nTarget Start Date:\nAugust 17, 2027\nApplication Deadline:\nOctober 7, 2026 (Applications accepted until filled or closed)\nPosition Description\nThe Department of Accounting & Finance in the Lucas College of Business at San José State University is pleased to announce a search for Assistant Professor of Finance beginning in August 2027.  The successful candidate will teach broadly in finance coursework, which may include financial management, financial analytics, or banking courses in our undergraduate, MS Financial Analytics, or MBA programs. The faculty will engage in research targeted to highly respected finance journals and in service at department, college, or university levels.\nThe Department of Accounting & Finance teaches undergraduate coursework in finance, accounting, and tax disciplines with a technology flavor and encourages cross-discipline research. It houses both the Masters of Science in Accounting Analytics and the Masters of Science in Financial Analytics degrees. Centers of excellence include the Center for Banking and Financial Services, the Accounting Advancement Center, and the High Technology Tax Institute. For more information, please visit\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/acctfin/\nThe Department of Accounting & Finance and San José State University value institutional excellence and are committed to advancing equity through research, teaching, and service. We enroll more than 40,000 students, many of whom are historically underserved; approximately 45% of our undergraduate students are first-generation college students and 38% are Pell Grant recipients. In addition, we are both a Hispanic Serving Institution (HSI) and an Asian American and Native American Pacific Islander Serving Institution (AANAPISI). SJSU aspires to create holistic learning environments that facilitate both individual and collective transformation. We seek teacher-scholars who cultivate inclusive, empowering spaces for mutual learning, skill-building, and knowledge exchange for all students. Our commitment to lifelong learning supports meaningful connections among faculty, staff, students, alumni, and the broader communities we serve.\nWe invite all applicants to include a Statement of Institutional Excellence (or incorporate it into your cover letter) to share how your lived and professional experiences will contribute to the SJSU community—particularly in relation to student success. A guide to writing this statement can be found at\nSJSU Institutional Excellence Statement Guidelines\n.\nRequired Qualifications\nPh.D. in Finance by start of appointment\nScholarly Academic qualification under AACSB standards\nExcellent communication and interpersonal skills\nApplicants should demonstrate an awareness of and sensitivity to the educational goals of a socially and economically diverse student population as might have been gained in cross-cultural study, training, teaching, and other comparable experience.\nPreferred Qualifications\nPriority will be given to candidates who possess one or more of the following:\nProfessional experience in corporate finance, investments, or financial institutions\nProfessional designations such as Chartered Financial Analyst\nExcellent teaching experience at the university level\nKey Responsibilities\nTeaching assignments are at both the graduate and undergraduate levels in traditional and accelerated formats.\nThe candidate must be actively engaged in scholarly activities conducting research, developing grants, presenting professional papers, and publishing in peer-reviewed journals.\nThe candidate will participate in shared governance, usually in department, college, and university committees and other service assignments.\nFaculty shall organize all their classes within the Canvas Learning Management System (LMS).\nThe candidate must demonstrate awareness and experience in understanding the needs of a student population of great diversity – in age, abilities, cultural background, ethnicity, religion, economic background, primary language, sexual orientation, gender identity, and academic preparation – through inclusive course materials, teaching strategies, and advisement.\nOther Duties\nNote that all San José State University employees are considered mandated reporters under the California Child Abuse and Neglect Reporting Act and are required to comply with the requirements set forth in CSU Executive Order 1083 as a condition of employment. Pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act) and CSU systemwide policy, some faculty duties may lead to designation as a Campus Security Authority (CSA). CSAs are required to complete Clery Act training and to immediately report Clery incidents to the institution.\nFaculty employees must complete CSU employee training as assigned and required based on their role (e.g., Data Security, FERPA, Preventing Discrimination and Harassment, Title IX, Health and Safety). The President may recommend or require compliance with safety measures that decrease the likelihood of COVID-19 transmission or illness and allows the core mission and activities of the campus to continue.\nApplication Procedure\nClick Apply Now to complete the SJSU Online Employment Application and attach the following documents [\ndepartments may specify length of items below (e.g. 2 pages)\n]:\nletter of interest\ncurriculum vitae\ninstitutional excellence statement\n(dedicated statement or addressed in letter of interest)\nstatement of teaching interests/philosophy\nresearch plans\nthree letters of recommendation\nInquiries may be directed to the Department Chair: Prof. Elizabeth Grace\nElizabeth.grace@sjsu.edu\n.\nPursuant to California Education Code 89521, applicants will be required to disclose whether they have received a final administrative decision or final judicial decision determining that they have committed sexual harassment within the last 7 years only after a determination is made that they meet the minimum qualifications for the position, and before an offer of employment is extended.  Applicants who reach the final stages of the application process must also sign a release form that authorizes the release of information by the applicant’s current and/or former employers to the CSU concerning any substantiated allegations of misconduct.\nConditional Offer\nThe work for this faculty position may only be performed in the State of California and requires establishing an on-campus presence.  Employment is contingent upon US residence and proof of eligibility to work in the United States. Satisfactory completion of background and reference checks (including criminal records and prior employment) are required. SJSU will make a conditional offer of employment, which may be rescinded if the background and reference checks reveal disqualifying information, and/or it is discovered that the candidate knowingly withheld or falsified information. Failure to satisfactorily complete the background and reference checks may affect the continued employment of a current employee who was conditionally offered the position.\nSan José State University: Silicon Valley's Public University\nLocated in the heart of Silicon Valley — one of the most innovative regions in the world —\nSan José State University\nis the founding campus of the 23-campus California State University (CSU) system and the first public university in the West. Recognized as a leading transformative educational institution, San José State is an essential partner in the technological, economic, cultural, and social development of Silicon Valley, the Bay Area, and California. SJSU is a top-200 school nationally in research funding and second highest in research productivity in the CSU system. Cutting-edge research, world-class scholarship, student-centered learning, diverse communities, and commitment to social justice, allow SJSU to provide life-changing opportunities and advance the public good locally and globally.\nEqual Employment Statement\nSan José State University prohibits discrimination on the basis of Age, Ancestry, Caste, Color, Disability, Ethnicity, Gender, Gender Expression, Gender Identity, Genetic Information, Marital Status, Medical Condition, Military Status, Nationality, Race, Religion, Religious Creed, Sex, Sexual Orientation, Sex Stereotype, and Veteran Status. This policy applies to all San José State University students, faculty, and staff as well as University programs and activities. Reasonable accommodations are made for applicants with disabilities who self-disclose.\nCampus Security and Fire Safety Notification\nPursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, the Annual Security Report (ASR) is also now available for viewing at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Security-Report.pdf\n. The ASR contains the current security and safety-related policy statements, emergency preparedness and evacuation information, crime prevention and Sexual Assault prevention information, and information about drug and alcohol prevention programming. The ASR also contains statistics of Clery crimes for San José State University locations for the three most recent calendar years. A paper copy of the ASR is available upon request by contacting the Office of the Clery Director by phone at 408-924-1501 or by email at clerycompliance@sjsu.edu.\nPursuant to the Higher Education Opportunity Act, the Annual Fire Safety Report (AFSR) is available at\nhttps://www.sjsu.edu/clery/docs/SJSU-Annual-Fire-Safety-Report.pdf\n. The purpose of this report is to disclose statistics for fires that occurred within SJSU on-campus housing facilities for the three most recent calendar years, and to distribute fire safety policies and procedures intended to promote safety on Campus. A paper copy of the AFSR is available upon request by contacting the Housing Office by phone at 408-795-5600 or by email at uhs-frontdesk@sjsu.edu."
